cb2c7893ec87974 2026-09-23] Nebius Group announced another increase in prices for NVIDIA GPU rentals, and CoreWeave said it was continuing to contract new compute capacity at higher prices; the article cites both as fresh evidence that demand for NVIDIA-powered computing remains strong. No price levels, capacity amounts or dates are given.\n    Quote: Meanwhile, demand from AI cloud providers remains robust as Nebius Group (NBIS) announced another increase in prices for Nvidia GPU rentals, while CoreWeave (CRWV) said it was continuing to contract new compute capacity at higher prices, providing fresh evidence that demand for Nvidia-powered computing remains strong.\n- e90 [party_statement; barchart.com; original source NVIDIA management 2026-08-26; from gnews:a5837f6a8cb2c7893ec87974 2026-09-23] NVIDIA management pointed to full production of its next-generation Vera Rubin AI platform, and the article says continued AI infrastructure strength is accelerating NVIDIA's AI growth trajectory. The statement does not say Vera Rubin has begun volume customer shipments or revenue recognition.\n    Quote: It's worth noting that Nvidia is not merely maintaining its AI growth trajectory; it is accelerating it, with management pointing to full production of its next-generation Vera Rubin platform and continued strength across AI infrastructure.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e85; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e181 [fact; GuruFocus (gurufocus.com); original source Super Micro Computer 2026-09-24; from gnews:cd0056006ad115260ae2b646 2026-09-24] Super Micro Computer moved into commercial shipments of servers built around NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin platform, shipping Vera Rubin NVL72 racks through its Data Center Building Block Solutions that combine computing hardware with liquid cooling, networking, storage and power components; each rack design uses 72 Rubin GPUs and 36 Vera CPUs, according to a GuruFocus report published 24 September 2026.\n    Quote: the server maker moved into commercial shipments of systems built around NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin platform, adding another data-center product to its AI infrastructure lineup. … The company is shipping Vera Rubin NVL72 racks through its Data Center Building Block Solutions, combining computing hardware with liquid cooling, networking, storage and power components. The rack design uses 7\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e859, e951; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e188 [party_statement; fool.com (article via Yahoo Finance); original source SpaceX (Elon Musk, CEO) ; from gnews:943a57daed49d866fe468605 2026-09-24] On SpaceX's second-quarter earnings call, SpaceX CEO Elon Musk said SpaceX has decided to build exclusively on NVIDIA hardware because he considers the Vera Rubin architecture the best architecture and the best AI computer, citing close cooperation and partnership with NVIDIA; the article characterises this as making SpaceX (which owns xAI and its Grok model) exclusive to NVIDIA.\n    Quote: During SpaceX's second-quarter earnings call, Musk had this to say about SpaceX's relationship with Nvidia: … We've decided to build exclusively on NVIDIA because we think the Vera Rubin architecture is the best architecture. We think it's the best AI computer, and we greatly value our close cooperation and partnership on many levels with NVIDIA. So, we're exclusive to NVIDIA.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e387, e396; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e208 [fact; Zacks (zacks.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:7fb0b2b7d22b8bb07625b212 2026-09-24] NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin platform began production shipments in the third quarter, according to Zacks; the item gives no volume, customer or revenue-recognition figure for the shipments.\n    Quote: Vera Rubin began production shipments in the third quarter.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e234, e409, e598, e650, e721, e857; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e234 [fact; Trefis; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:06218d9421731e5e59c1dbef 2026-09-24] NVIDIA began production shipments of its Vera Rubin platform in August, with purchase orders already received from every major hyperscaler, AI cloud and system OEM, according to a 24 September 2026 article.\n    Quote: Production shipments began in August, with purchase orders already in from every major hyperscaler, AI cloud, and system OEM.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e208; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e236 [party_statement; Trefis; original source NVIDIA (CFO) ; from gnews:06218d9421731e5e59c1dbef 2026-09-24] NVIDIA's CFO expects supply to remain a bottleneck at least through the end of fiscal 2028 and calls memory pricing extreme; the article reports memory prices have risen more than management expected.\n    Quote: The CFO expects supply to stay a bottleneck at least through the end of fiscal 2028 and calls memory pricing extreme. … Memory prices have risen more than management expected, and the guide has that margin settling in the 72% to 73% range in fiscal 2028, helped by price increases NVIDIA has already executed.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e271, e413, e414, e556, e653, e774, e864, e865; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e243 [fact; Kieron Allen, Cloud Wars (cloudwars.com); original source Amazon Web Services and NVIDIA (joint announcement) ; from gnews:7c4283c55cf02ba9cfd9a835 2026-09-24] Amazon Web Services and NVIDIA announced an expansion of their long-standing partnership under which AWS will deploy 2 million additional NVIDIA GPUs across its infrastructure between 2027 and 2028, spanning NVIDIA's Blackwell Ultra, Rubin and Rubin Ultra architectures and deployed alongside NVIDIA Vera CPUs; the addition builds on the more than 1 million NVIDIA GPUs AWS had previously committed to deploying beginning in 2026, and Amazon said the aim is to meet surging demand for AI infrastructure from frontier labs, global enterprises, start-ups and governments. The number of GPUs is a planned deployment, not reported deliveries or revenue.\n    Quote: Now, the two powerhouses have announced a staggering expansion of this existing partnership which will, among other things — but this is certainly the standout headline — include the deployment of 2 million additional NVIDIA GPUs across AWS infrastructure between 2027 and 2028. … The aim, according to Amazon, is to meet surging demand for AI infrastructure “from frontier labs, global enterprises, \n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e105; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e279 [third_party_view; qz.com, citing MarketWatch; original source Mehdi Hosseini, Susquehanna analyst (remarks to MarketWatch) ; from gnews:460160402f7a10882d1e925f 2026-09-25] Susquehanna analyst Mehdi Hosseini, in remarks reported by MarketWatch and cited by qz.com, said Micron's November-quarter (fiscal Q1 2026) sales forecast is strong enough to satisfy investors but will not fully reflect the benefits of the Nvidia product ramp; he said some estimated revenue is expected to shift toward next year instead of this year, implying the Nvidia-chip-related revenue payoff may be delayed.\n    Quote: Micron $MU Technology posted record fiscal fourth-quarter revenue and issued strong guidance for the current quarter, but one analyst says investors may need to wait longer to see the full payoff from an Nvidia $NVDA chip launch. … Susquehanna analyst Mehdi Hosseini told MarketWatch that Micron 's November-quarter sales forecast looks strong enough to satisfy investors, though it won't fully refle\n- e283 [party_statement; MarketBeat; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:91b917994e36036e304616e8 2026-09-25] NVIDIA states that its Vera Rubin AI platform is already in full production, with production shipments beginning in August 2026 and purchase orders received from every major hyperscaler, AI cloud and system original equipment manufacturer (OEM), as reported by MarketBeat.\n    Quote: The trigger for this move could come from subsequent earnings reports and continued progress with Vera Rubin. The platform is already in full production, with NVIDIA reporting production shipments beginning in August and purchase orders from every major hyperscaler, AI cloud and system original equipment manufacturer (OEM).\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e85; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e370 [fact; cryptorank.io; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:844106b7a25ecc1175290290 2026-09-26] Nvidia reported that in the latest MLPerf Inference v6.1 tests its next-generation Vera Rubin NVL72 delivered up to 3.7x the throughput of its GB300 NVL72 on the Qwen3-VL benchmark and up to 2.5x on DeepSeek-R1, and described the Vera Rubin entry in its official MLPerf announcement as a preview submission ahead of broader deployment.\n    Quote: In the latest MLPerf Inference v6.1 tests, Vera Rubin NVL72 delivered up to 3.7x the throughput of Nvidia’s GB300 NVL72 on the Qwen3-VL benchmark. On DeepSeek-R1, the improvement reached as much as 2.5x. … Nvidia detailed the results in its official MLPerf announcement , describing Vera Rubin’s entry as a preview submission ahead of broader deployment.\n- e409 [fact; Trefis; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:d4f3bddf04a4d029a097541c 2026-09-27] NVIDIA began production shipments of its newest data center system, Vera Rubin, in August 2026, according to the article.\n    Quote: The company began production shipments of Vera Rubin, its newest data center system, in August 2026.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e208; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e410 [party_statement; NVIDIA management; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:d4f3bddf04a4d029a097541c 2026-09-27] NVIDIA management expects Vera Rubin to account for about 20% of data center revenue in fiscal Q3 2027.\n    Quote: Management expects Vera Rubin to bring in about 20% of data center revenue in fiscal Q3 2027.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e555, e764, e782, e813, e858; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e416 [party_statement; NVIDIA management; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:d4f3bddf04a4d029a097541c 2026-09-27] NVIDIA management expects sales to its largest cloud companies to speed up again in fiscal Q4 2027 as Vera Rubin supply grows.\n    Quote: Management expects sales to the largest cloud companies to speed up again in fiscal Q4 2027, as Vera Rubin supply grows.\n- e551 [fact; Trefis; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:4334522792e44c6fd9d18ccd 2026-09-28] NVIDIA management said on its fiscal Q2 2027 earnings call that inventory rose to $32 billion as the company prepared for the Vera Rubin launch.\n    Quote: On the fiscal Q2 2027 call, management said inventory rose to $32 billion as NVIDIA prepared for the Vera Rubin launch.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e588; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e555 [party_statement; Trefis; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:4334522792e44c6fd9d18ccd 2026-09-28] NVIDIA management expects the newly launched Vera Rubin platform to make up about 20% of data center revenue in fiscal Q3 2027, according to Trefis.\n    Quote: The newly launched Vera Rubin platform is one source of that revenue. Management expects it to make up about 20% of data center revenue in fiscal Q3 2027.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e410; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e633 [third_party_view; Adam Spatacco, fool.com; original source  ; from gnews:0e4e3f9b17e0cda6b72d47fc 2026-09-29] The article reports a forecast that Nvidia's next-generation Vera Rubin CPU architecture will reach $20 billion in sales next quarter.\n    Quote: Meanwhile, the next-generation Vera Rubin CPU architecture is forecast to reach $20 billion in sales next quarter.\n- e659 [fact; NVIDIA Corporation (NVIDIA Blog); original source CoreWeave ; from nvblog:coreweave-agentic-ai-vera-rubin 2026-09-30] CoreWeave announced at its CoreWeave Fully Connected event (held the week of the 30 September 2026 NVIDIA blog post) that NVIDIA Vera Rubin NVL72 systems with Spectrum-X 102.4T Ethernet networking are available on CoreWeave Cloud, making CoreWeave one of the first cloud providers to deliver the platform in customers' hands; per the announcement, Cognition, the applied AI lab behind the Devin AI software engineer, is the first customer running production workloads on Vera Rubin.\n    Quote: At CoreWeave Fully Connected, running this week in San Francisco, CoreWeave announced availability of NVIDIA Vera Rubin NVL72 systems with Spectrum-X 102.4T Ethernet networking. Cognition, the applied AI lab behind the Devin AI software engineer, is the first customer running production workloads on Vera Rubin. … CoreWeave announced availability of NVIDIA Vera Rubin NVL72 on CoreWeave Cloud, makin\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e751, e860, e894, e895; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e660 [fact; NVIDIA Corporation (NVIDIA Blog); original source CoreWeave ; from nvblog:coreweave-agentic-ai-vera-rubin 2026-09-30] CoreWeave received its first NVIDIA Vera Rubin NVL72 production racks earlier in September 2026, before the 30 September 2026 article.\n    Quote: Earlier this month, CoreWeave received its first Vera Rubin NVL72 production racks.\n- e720 [fact; CRN; original source TD Synnex (CEO Patrick Zammit), with customer identity reported by CRN ; from gnews:84569ea837e5d2d93ff16093 2026-09-30] TD Synnex CEO Patrick Zammit said on the distributor's third-quarter earnings call (the Thursday before the 30 September 2026 article) that TD Synnex and Houston-based systems integrator Mark III Systems recently signed an agreement to support an NVIDIA AI factory powered by Vera Rubin NVL72 systems, which he described as 'one of the largest' enterprise AI factory infrastructure deployments expected to be delivered through the channel, bundling design, integration, deployment, Day 2 co-admin operations, financing and supply chain. CRN reported, citing what it learned, that the customer is a US-based Fortune 100 company. No contract value, system count or delivery date was disclosed.\n    Quote: After TD Synnex revealed that Mark III Systems is set to deliver ‘one of the largest’ enterprise AI factory deployments in the channel, an executive at the systems integrator explained to CRN how its AI factory expertise and enterprise trends helped it seal the deal. … As hyperscalers and neoclouds begin to launch offerings based on Nvidia’s new flagship Vera Rubin rack-scale AI platform, one syst\n- e751 [fact; GuruFocus (gurufocus.com); original source CoreWeave ; from gnews:2b6641fb175eb6c57e2bdff2 2026-09-30] CoreWeave (CRWV) announced at its Fully Connected AI conference in San Francisco that it is bringing NVIDIA (NVDA) Vera CPUs into its cloud portfolio for AI-agent workloads and will offer NVIDIA Vera Rubin NVL72 systems for production use, with Cognition named as the first customer running workloads on the platform. CoreWeave said the Rubin systems are already operating on its cloud service, which it described as a move from infrastructure deployment toward commercial use, and that customers can access the hardware under its existing operating model for NVIDIA systems.\n    Quote: The company is bringing NVIDIA ( NVDA ) Vera CPUs to its cloud portfolio for workloads tied to AI agents. It will also offer NVIDIA Vera Rubin NVL72 systems for production use, with Cognition serving as the first customer running workloads on the platform. … CoreWeave said the Rubin systems are already operating on its cloud service, marking a move from infrastructure deployment toward commercial \n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e659; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e753 [third_party_view; NVIDIA Corporation (NVIDIA Blog); original source SemiAnalysis (AgentX data) ; from nvblog:productive-durable-fungible-ai-factories 2026-10-01] SemiAnalysis AgentX data cited in NVIDIA's blog states that NVIDIA Vera Rubin NVL72 systems deliver over 30x higher throughput per megawatt than NVIDIA GB300 NVL72 systems, and up to 45x lower cost per million tokens on the DeepSeek V4 Pro model.\n    Quote: SemiAnalysis AgentX data shows NVIDIA Vera Rubin NVL72 systems deliver over 30x higher throughput per megawatt than NVIDIA GB300 NVL72, and up to 45x lower cost per million tokens on the DeepSeek V4 Pro model.\n- e764 [party_statement; Zacks; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:95eab8d6d71dd0f94a8f763e 2026-10-01] NVIDIA said it started shipping its Vera Rubin platform in August 2026 and expects Vera Rubin to account for about 20% of Data Center revenues in fiscal Q3 2027.\n    Quote: NVIDIA started shipping Vera Rubin in August and expects the platform to account for about 20% of Data Center revenues in the third quarter.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e410; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e765 [party_statement; Zacks; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:95eab8d6d71dd0f94a8f763e 2026-10-01] NVIDIA estimates revenue opportunity per gigawatt of AI infrastructure at approximately $18 billion for Hopper, $25 billion for Blackwell and $40 billion for Vera Rubin.\n    Quote: The company estimates that revenue opportunity per gigawatt of AI infrastructure has increased from approximately $18 billion with Hopper to $25 billion with Blackwell and $40 billion with Vera Rubin.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e488; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e775 [party_statement; finance.yahoo.com article (contributor piece); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:890bfe7d206f0531a54beb16 2026-10-01] NVIDIA's Vera Rubin platform is now in full production, and the article states revenue per gigawatt of deployed capacity has risen from $18 billion for Hopper to $25 billion for Blackwell and $40 billion for Vera Rubin.\n    Quote: Revenue per gigawatt rose from $18 billion for Hopper to $25 billion for Blackwell and $40 billion for Vera Rubin, now in full production.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e488; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e782 [third_party_view; finance.yahoo.com article (contributor piece); original source  ; from gnews:890bfe7d206f0531a54beb16 2026-10-01] The article estimates Vera Rubin will make up about 20% of NVIDIA's data center revenue in Q3 FY2027, with NVIDIA's next earnings report due November 17.\n    Quote: Vera Rubin should make up about 20% of data center revenue in Q3, and the next earnings report lands November 17.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e410; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e811 [fact; fool.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:efb160acb7c94e14a52df9fa 2026-10-01] Nvidia launched its Vera CPU, a central processing unit positioned for agentic-AI workloads; the article reports the launch while noting that the agentic AI boom will likely raise CPU demand. The item gives no launch date, volume, customer or shipment timing.\n    Quote: There is a significant opportunity in some of the niches it targets. For instance, the agentic AI boom will likely lead to increased demand for CPUs (central processing units), and to that end, Nvidia launched its Vera CPU.\n- e813 [party_statement; Daniel Foelber, fool.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:e84d920e300dc3c29e501037 2026-10-01] Nvidia began shipping its Rubin platform in August and expects Rubin to account for 20% of data center revenue in the third quarter of fiscal 2027, which the company will report in November, according to the article.\n    Quote: Nvidia began shipping Rubin in August and already expects it to account for 20% of data center revenue in the third quarter of fiscal 2027, which it will report in November.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e410; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e814 [party_statement; Daniel Foelber, fool.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:e84d920e300dc3c29e501037 2026-10-01] Nvidia is guiding for 70% year-over-year revenue growth in fiscal 2028, which the article attributes to massive demand for its Rubin platform.\n    Quote: Demand for Rubin is so massive that Nvidia is guiding for 70% year-over-year revenue growth in fiscal 2028.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e104; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e832 [third_party_view; Keithen Drury, fool.com; original source Keithen Drury (fool.com) 2026-10-01; from gnews:6793603da478ba6858e0827d 2026-10-01] In his 1 October 2026 column, Keithen Drury (fool.com) wrote that Nvidia's new Rubin-generation chips are starting to become available, will provide more advanced capabilities than prior generations, and that Nvidia will charge a higher price for them, which he called a built-in revenue escalator 'launching shortly'. No shipment volumes, dates or prices are given, and the claim is the author's characterisation rather than a company announcement.\n    Quote: Even though competition is intensifying for which company has the best available option, Nvidia's new Rubin generation of chips will make an even greater impact than prior iterations. These computing units are starting to become available and will provide more advanced capabilities than prior generations.