September 30 that one of its customers runs production workloads on the system.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e659; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e864 [party_statement; Yahoo Finance (finance.yahoo.com); original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:8867ec84bb48b27c3ae694f5 2026-10-02] NVIDIA management says the company cannot build enough to meet demand, called its fiscal 2028 forecast supply-constrained, expects supply to stay a bottleneck through at least the end of that year, and said on the fiscal Q2 2027 call that its entire supply chain is running flat out.\n    Quote: NVIDIA cannot build enough to meet demand, management says. Management called its fiscal 2028 forecast supply-constrained, and expects supply to stay a bottleneck through at least the end of that year. Asked on the fiscal Q2 2027 call what holds supply back, management said its entire supply chain is running flat out.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e236; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e894 [fact; wccftech.com, quoting NVIDIA VP Ian Buck; original source NVIDIA and CoreWeave ; from gnews:4875d3af5072a2d9c8532a7f 2026-10-02] NVIDIA and CoreWeave announced at CoreWeave Fully Connected 2026 that the NVIDIA Vera Rubin NVL72 platform is available on CoreWeave Cloud, with NVIDIA VP Ian Buck stating that CoreWeave is bringing Vera Rubin NVL72 into production even as its older V100 GPUs still run customer workloads nearly a decade after Volta launched.\n    Quote: At CoreWeave Fully Connected 2026, NVIDIA and CoreWeave announce the availability of NVIDIA Vera Rubin NVL72 on CoreWeave Cloud. … “NVIDIA accelerated computing delivers value across generations,” said Ian Buck, vice president of hyperscale and high-performance computing at NVIDIA. “CoreWeave’s NVIDIA V100 GPUs are still running customer workloads nearly a decade after Volta launched, even as Core\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e659; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e895 [fact; wccftech.com; original source CoreWeave / Cognition ; from gnews:4875d3af5072a2d9c8532a7f 2026-10-02] Cognition is the first customer to secure installations of NVIDIA Vera Rubin NVL72 servers at CoreWeave, and CoreWeave stood up a production Vera Rubin cluster for it in days, with Cognition scaling to thousands of GPUs within nine months; Cognition had earlier benchmarked Vera Rubin against Grace Blackwell in September, recording a 4.8x gain in total token throughput for SWE-2 inference workloads.\n    Quote: Cognition is the first to secure a hold on these latest server installations at CoreWeave, and has been able to scale to thousands of GPUs within nine months. Earlier in September, Cognition was the first to benchmark Vera Rubin against Grace Blackwell in inference, and was able to record a 4.8x gain in total token throughput for SWE-2 inference workloads. … In days, CoreWeave stood up a productio\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e659; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e907 [party_statement; Daniel Foelber (The Motley Fool); original source Nvidia (guidance); margin/demand attribution by the article's author ; from gnews:d9f1147865cff881040d3dd0 2026-10-02] The article states that Nvidia is already guiding for a 70% increase in revenue for fiscal 2028, and the author attributes continued high margins to what he calls insatiable demand for the Vera Rubin compute platform.\n    Quote: For fiscal 2028, Nvidia is already guiding for a 70% increase in revenue. … And its margins should remain sky high thanks to insatiable demand for the Vera Rubin compute platform .\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e104; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e951 [fact; finance.yahoo.com; original source Supermicro 2026-09-23; from gnews:d38374c4abf4bd13d2cd2ccd 2026-10-02] On Sept. 23, Supermicro said it had started shipping racks built on Nvidia's newest platform, Vera Rubin; each rack carries 72 Rubin GPUs.\n    Quote: On Sept. 23, Supermicro said it had started shipping racks, the standard cabinets that hold servers inside a data center, built on Nvidia's newest platform, Vera Rubin. Each rack carries 72 Rubin GPUs.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e181; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e978 [fact; barchart.com; original source Akamai Technologies 2026-09-24; from gnews:6121a00939cb0069709b5c54 2026-10-03] Akamai Technologies said on 24 September 2026 that it had significantly expanded its partnership with Anthropic; Anthropic committed to spend $11.6 billion over seven years on dedicated cloud capacity and related services for its growing CPU workloads, with an option to commit an additional $9 billion if conditions are met, bringing the total value to about $20 billion. The article says the deal does not directly involve Nvidia based on public disclosure.\n    Quote: A major new agreement between cloud infrastructure provider Akamai Technologies (AKAM) and frontier AI lab Anthropic could add another piece to that story. The nearly $12 billion deal does not directly involve Nvidia, at least based on what has been publicly disclosed so far. … On Sept. 24, cloud infrastructure company Akamai Technologies significantly expanded its partnership with frontier AI lab\n- e979 [third_party_view; barchart.com; original source GF Securities (Henry Huang) ; from gnews:6121a00939cb0069709b5c54 2026-10-03] GF Securities analyst Henry Huang said the Akamai-Anthropic CPU cloud deal is expected to be on Nvidia's Vera CPUs, which he said Akamai had previously disclosed as its planned platform for agentic workloads; the article presents this as an expectation, not confirmation.\n    Quote: \"The deal is expected to be on Nvidia's Vera CPUs, which Akamai previously disclosed as its planned platform for agentic workloads,\" according to GF Securities analyst Henry Huang.\n- e980 [fact; barchart.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:6121a00939cb0069709b5c54 2026-10-03] Nvidia said in an August 2026 blog post that it had delivered Vera CPU systems to Anthropic; the article also notes Nvidia had named Anthropic among AI labs planning to adopt Vera at GTC Taipei in May. The delivery is reported as completed, but no volume or revenue is disclosed.\n    Quote: Nvidia said at GTC Taipei that Anthropic was among the AI labs planning to adopt Vera, and the chipmaker added in an August blog post that it had delivered Vera CPU systems to Anthropic.\n- e981 [party_statement; barchart.com; original source Akamai Technologies ; from gnews:6121a00939cb0069709b5c54 2026-10-03] Akamai expects roughly $5.5 billion of capital expenditures tied to Anthropic's commitment, including an additional $1.7 billion in 2026 to secure and pre-purchase critical supply-chain components; the article says not all of that spending will go toward Nvidia's CPUs because Akamai also needs memory, ABF substrates and other components.\n    Quote: Akamai expects roughly $5.5 billion in capital expenditures tied to Anthropic's commitment, including an additional $1.7 billion in 2026 to secure and pre-purchase critical supply-chain components. It is worth noting that not all of that spending will go toward Nvidia's CPUs, as Akamai will also need to purchase memory, ABF substrates, and other components.\n- e982 [party_statement; barchart.com; original source NVIDIA (CFO Colette Kress) ; from gnews:6121a00939cb0069709b5c54 2026-10-03] On Nvidia's Q2 earnings call, CFO Colette Kress said trailing-12-month revenue from Grace CPUs had already exceeded $5 billion and that next-generation Vera was in full production; she said Nvidia expects Vera to be deployed by every major hyperscaler, neocloud, AI lab and system OEM, continues to see demand for roughly $20 billion of server CPUs, and preliminarily expects CPU revenue to more than double year-over-year in fiscal 2028.\n    Quote: Nvidia CFO Colette Kress said on the Q2 earnings call last month that trailing-12-month revenue from Grace CPUs had already exceeded $5 billion, while next-generation Vera was in full production. Kress said Nvidia expects Vera to be deployed by every major hyperscaler, neocloud, AI lab, and system OEM, adding that the company continues to see demand for roughly $20 billion of server CPUs. She also\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e632; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e1046 [party_statement; Yahoo Finance; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:ed14a9d1371ea43f3c5dcd3d 2026-10-04] NVIDIA says Grace Blackwell is already one generation behind Vera Rubin and that Vera Rubin delivers 35 times lower token costs than Grace Blackwell Ultra; NVIDIA estimates about $25 billion of revenue per gigawatt for Grace Blackwell and roughly $40 billion per gigawatt for Vera Rubin.\n    Quote: Grace Blackwell is already one generation behind Vera Rubin. NVIDIA says Vera Rubin delivers 35X lower token costs than Grace Blackwell Ultra. NVIDIA estimates about $25 billion per gigawatt of revenue for Grace Blackwell and roughly $40 billion for Vera Rubin.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e117; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e1060 [fact; Brett Schafer, Fool.com; original source Taiwan Semiconductor Manufacturing (TSMC) ; from gnews:654a1658fd6ce4b1694951fc 2026-10-04] Taiwan Semiconductor Manufacturing (TSMC), the sole at-scale manufacturer of Nvidia's chips, reported revenue of $143 billion over the past 12 months and revenue up 34% year over year in the most recent quarter, with its high-performance-compute (HPC) segment rising 20% quarter over quarter and now at 66% of the overall business, per Fool.com's account.\n    Quote: Revenue was $143 billion during the past 12 months and rose 34% year-over-year last quarter. The company is seeing booming demand from its high performance compute (HPC) segment, which increased 20% quarter-over-quarter and now makes up 66% of the overall business.\n- e1098 [fact; finance.yahoo.com; original source Foxconn (Hon Hai Precision Industry) 2026-10-05; from gnews:3b878790fc298c0b29ba8205 2026-10-05] Foxconn, described as Nvidia's biggest server maker, reported third-quarter 2026 revenue up 47% to T$3.03 trillion ($95.4 billion), above the LSEG SmartEstimate of T$2.83 trillion — a supplier volume signal for Nvidia's AI server business, though the item does not specify which Nvidia platform drove it.\n    Quote: This morning, Foxconn, Nvidia's biggest server maker, reported third-quarter revenue up 47% to T$3.03 trillion ($95.4 billion), above the LSEG SmartEstimate of T$2.83 trillion.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1180; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e1101 [fact; fool.com; original source NVIDIA (product status reported by fool.com) ; from gnews:a6cfdb974af32e5f975e0d4f 2026-10-05] NVIDIA's GPU release cadence continues: after Blackwell and Blackwell Ultra launched in recent years, the release of the current Vera Rubin system is now unfolding, according to fool.com's 5 October 2026 article.\n    Quote: Nvidia has pledged to update its GPUs on an annual basis, and it's following through on this, with Blackwell and Blackwell Ultra launched in recent years and the current release of the Vera Rubin system now unfolding.\n- e1125 [party_statement; NVIDIA management (via finance.yahoo.com); original source NVIDIA 2026-08-26; from gnews:17d71c8bf611131c4216e2eb 2026-10-05] NVIDIA management said each new product generation brings in more money for the same data-center capacity, putting revenue per gigawatt at roughly $18 billion for Hopper, $25 billion for Blackwell and $40 billion for Vera Rubin.\n    Quote: Each new product generation also brings in more money for the same amount of data center capacity. Management put revenue per gigawatt at roughly $18 billion for Hopper, $25 billion for Blackwell and $40 billion for Vera Rubin.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e488; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e1148 [party_statement; MarketBeat; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:db898bbdf8476a62d9167361 2026-10-05] NVIDIA itself expects its volume sales to double, an expectation the MarketBeat article ties to the Vera Rubin release and to the next wave of data center construction.\n    Quote: NVIDIA itself expects volume sales to double, underpinned by the Vera Rubin release.\n- e1177 [fact; Business Wire (AM Intelligence press release, via Yahoo Finance UK); original source AM Intelligence (AMI) 2026-10-05; from gnews:8ec8d22f933c8556d24c6c9d 2026-10-05] AM Intelligence (AMI), the AI infrastructure platform set up by promoters of Greenko, announced on 5 October 2026 two further firm and binding orders for 20,000 NVIDIA Rubin GPUs to be deployed as NVIDIA Vera Rubin NVL72 rack-scale systems, on top of its inaugural commitment announced 25 August 2026 for 9,000 NVIDIA Rubin GPUs at its first AI Factory in Hyderabad. The follow-on capacity totals about 70 MW at Malaysia and India with delivery scheduled for Q2 2027, and the three orders together bring AMI's committed frontier compute to approximately 29,000 Rubin GPUs and close to 100 MW, which AMI describes as making it among the earliest and largest committed buyers of Rubin GPUs outside North America.\n    Quote: Two further binding orders of ~20,000 NVIDIA GPUs take AMI's committed NVIDIA Vera Rubin capacity to approximately ~29,000 GPUs and close to 100 MW, part of its global development pipeline of ~400 MW … today announced two further firm and binding orders for 20,000 NVIDIA Rubin GPUs , to be deployed as NVIDIA Vera Rubin NVL72 rack-scale systems. … These orders are in addition to the AMI's inaugural\n- e1178 [party_statement; Business Wire (AM Intelligence press release, via Yahoo Finance UK); original source AM Intelligence (AMI) 2026-10-05; from gnews:8ec8d22f933c8556d24c6c9d 2026-10-05] AM Intelligence said it plans to bring an additional 300 MW of compute capacity to market in the next 15 months, requiring capital expenditure of more than $20 billion on top of the $6 billion already committed for its initial 100 MW. It said it has developed a pipeline of about 400 MW of Compute-as-a-Service capacity across India, the United States, Europe and Malaysia, that the three Rubin GPU orders are the first committed tranche of that pipeline, and that the balance is under development for delivery in subsequent phases in 2027 and early 2028.\n    Quote: AMI plans to bring additional 300 MWs of capacity to market in the next 15 months, involving capital expenditure of more than $20+ billion in addition to the $6 billion already committed for the initial $100 MW. … AMI has developed a pipeline of ~400 MW of Compute-as-a-Service capacity across global locations, including India, the United States, Europe and Malaysia. The three orders placed to date\n\n[Themes in force, 6]\n- v10 (ai-capex, due 2027-03-31) How many gigawatts of new data-center capacity will be added in the United States during calendar 2026, as determined by full-year 2026 grid-operator and utility interconnection/capacity data and industry data-center capacity reports?: now rung 2 from 10 GW to below 15 GW; judged 2026-10-05; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The reference is Huang’s rough 10–20 GW annual estimate: its 10–<15 portion is rung 2, its 15–<20 portion is rung 3, and 20 GW reaches rung 4; the midpoint, 15 GW, is rung 3. I forecast below that midpoint because the quantified trades shortfall, the stated net power shortfall, and the two-to-three-\n- v11 (semiconductor-cycle, due 2027-03-31) What is the percentage change in average DRAM (including HBM) contract prices in calendar 2026 versus calendar 2025, according to TrendForce/DRAMeXchange full-year 2026 contract-price data?: now rung 5 30 percent or more; judged 2026-10-05; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The reference is the opening no-reference position, rung 3 (10% to below 20%); no consensus, company guidance, or TrendForce/DRAMeXchange full-year figure for this measure is supplied. The forecast is above that reference: Micron says the DRAM imbalance has no visible return-to-balance date and that\n- v3 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will U.S. authorities authorize exports of Nvidia’s current Blackwell-generation AI chips to China and Chinese authorities allow those shipments by January 31, 2027, as verified by official export-control or licensing decisions, Chinese regulatory or customs actions, or Nvidia disclosures?: now rung 3 Still open; judged 2026-10-05; reasons: The nearest observable market reference is NVIDIA’s Q3 FY2027 guidance, which assumes no China Data Center compute revenue; that sits at rung 3, Still open, rather than establishing that approval is formally ruled out by the due date. The evidence implies no verified U.S. authorization for Blackwell\n- v5 (ai-capex, due 2027-03-31) What will the five largest AI hyperscalers' combined data-center capital expenditure be in calendar 2026, according to their full-year earnings and capital-expenditure disclosures?: now rung 3 from 750,000,000,000 USD to below 850,000,000,000 USD; judged 2026-10-05; reasons: The reference is Nvidia's estimate of nearly $800 billion for the big five in calendar 2026, which sits in rung 3. Amazon's approximately $220 billion 2026 capex projection, versus its previous $200 billion level, supports about a $20 billion increase to that reference: roughly $820 billion, still w\n- v7 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will China's Ministry of Industry and Information Technology (MIIT) formally approve purchases of Nvidia's RTX Pro 5500 by Alibaba, ByteDance, and other Chinese firms by January 31, 2027?: now rung 1 Yes; judged 2026-09-30; reasons: The reference is rung 3: no market consensus or formal decision is supplied, and the reported status is still deliberation rather than approval or rejection. The forecast nevertheless favors rung 1: the specific report says officials indicated purchases were likely to be approved and MIIT intended t\n- v9 (ai-capex, due 2028-03-30) How much will the five largest AI hyperscalers spend in total on data centers in calendar 2027, as shown by their capex disclosures and reported 2027 results?: now rung 3 from 1,200,000,000,000 USD to below 1,400,000,000,000 USD; judged 2026-10-05; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The reference is Nvidia's projection of $1.3 trillion for calendar 2027, which sits in rung 3. Barclays' approximately $1.1 trillion figure is for cloud capital spending and is not a like-for-like estimate of the five hyperscalers' data-center capex; the demand evidence, including the new customer-g\n\n[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 1; reasons: The reference is NVIDIA management's expectation that Vera Rubin will make up about 20% of Data Center revenue in fiscal Q3 2027, which is rung 1 because Q3 falls within FY2027. That guidance alone is a forecast, not proof of recognized revenue. But the evidence also says production shipments began in August, Supermicro is shipping Vera Rubin racks, and CoreWeave has the platform running production workloads for Cognition; these make the guided Q3 revenue share more likely to be realized and disclosed by the due date. The supply-bottleneck and possible-delay evidence is a counterweight, but does not outweigh the near-term guidance and customer deployment evidence. The updated capacity and DRAM themes add further caution; they do not move the forecast below rung 1.\n\nAnswer per the JSON schema. cites may name only the evidence ids listed above; cites_views must be empty: this thesis depends on no other thesis; theme_pushes only the theme ids listed above; a theme id goes only in theme_pushes, never in cites or cites_views.",
