docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/carried-run/packet-figures-2.json:1309:   "request": "Thesis v13 (NVDA, asset, focus \"Nvidia's CPO switch shipment volume from Q4 2026 through calendar 2027 (GF Securities: 15,000 units in Q4 2026, 16,000 in early 2027, 30,000 by mid-2027, 100,000 for all of 2027), as reflected in Nvidia's networking revenue disclosures and suppliers' CPO/NPO laser shipments.\", 2027, due 2027-12-31): Will Nvidia’s CPO switch shipments reach at least 100,000 units during calendar 2027?\nLadder: rung 1 Yes: Nvidia’s CPO switch shipments reach at least 100,000 units during calendar 2027, as shown by Nvidia’s shipment disclosures. / rung 2 Ruled out: After 2026-10-04 and before reaching 100,000 units, Nvidia formally abandons CPO switch shipments for calendar 2027. This rung holds when that, or any other decision against the event of rung 1 as a whole, or a formal abandonment or withdrawal of it as a whole, was made after 2026-10-04 and stands on 2027-12-31, and only when rung 1 does not. / rung 3 Still open: Neither rung 1 nor rung 2 holds on 2027-12-31.\n\n[Evidence in view under this thesis, 4 items]\n- e1068 [third_party_view; Barchart.com; original source GF Securities ; from gnews:f3cd04775dd78548245c9aaf 2026-10-04] GF Securities said Nvidia is scaling out co-packaged optics (CPO) faster than expected and forecast Nvidia's CPO switch shipments at 15,000 units in the fourth quarter of 2026, 16,000 in early 2027, rising to 30,000 by mid-2027 and potentially 100,000 units for all of 2027. The figures are the analyst's estimate, not an Nvidia disclosure; the article links them to supplier Lumentum, whose laser chips and photonic components go into optical systems.\n    Quote: GF Securities says Nvidia (NVDA) is scaling out co-packaged optics, or CPO, faster than expected, creating another potential growth driver for Lumentum. … The forecast is striking. GF Securities expects Nvidia's CPO switch shipments to reach 15,000 units in the fourth quarter of 2026, rising to 16,000 in early 2027 and 30,000 by mid-2027. For all of 2027, shipments could reach 100,000 units.\n- e1069 [fact; Barchart.com; original source Lumentum (LITE) ; from gnews:f3cd04775dd78548245c9aaf 2026-10-04] Lumentum reported fiscal Q4 revenue of $1.01 billion, more than doubling from a year earlier and its eighth consecutive quarter of year-over-year growth, with Components revenue up 103% to $649.4 million and Systems revenue up 123% to $356.9 million; gross margin rose to 50% and operating margin to 36.6%, net income was $326.3 million, adjusted EPS was $3.23 (over three times the year-ago quarter), free cash flow was $196.2 million, and cash and short-term investments totaled $2.74 billion at period end. Lumentum supplies laser chips and photonic components used in optical systems.\n    Quote: In Q4, the company generated $1.01 billion in revenue, more than doubling from a year ago and extending its streak of year-over-year growth to eight straight quarters. … The increase was widespread throughout the company. Components' revenue grew 103% to $649.4 million, while Systems' revenue increased 123% to $356.9 million. Profitability also improved, as the gross margin climbed to 50% and the \n- e1070 [party_statement; Barchart.com; original source Lumentum (LITE) ; from gnews:f3cd04775dd78548245c9aaf 2026-10-04] Lumentum management guided fiscal Q1 2027 revenue to roughly $1.225–1.275 billion, an increase of more than $150 million sequentially, with adjusted earnings per share of $4.05–$4.35 and expected gains in profitability, saying it expects the current trend to persist through fiscal 2027.\n    Quote: Management anticipates the trend to persist through fiscal 2027 as well. Lumentum is projecting an increase of more than $150 million in sales for the first quarter, along with gains in profitability, as revenue for the period is expected to be in the neighborhood of $1.225 billion to $1.275 billion and adjusted earnings per share are projected to range between $4.05 and $4.35.\n- e1071 [third_party_view; Barchart.com; original source GF Securities ; from gnews:f3cd04775dd78548245c9aaf 2026-10-04] GF Securities forecast that Lumentum's CPO laser revenue could rise from $427 million in 2027 to $750 million in 2028, and that its near-packaged optics (NPO) laser revenue could jump from $501 million in 2027 to $2.55 billion in 2028, as Nvidia's shift to co-packaged and near-packaged optics drives optical component demand. These are the analyst's estimates of a supplier's revenue, not Nvidia figures.\n    Quote: GF Securities sees Lumentum benefiting directly from that shift. The firm forecasts Lumentum's CPO laser revenue could rise from $427 million in 2027 to $750 million in 2028. … Near-packaged optics, or NPO, could become an even larger opportunity. GF Securities estimates Lumentum's NPO laser revenue could jump from $501 million in 2027 to $2.55 billion in 2028.\n\n[Themes in force, 6]\n- v10 (ai-capex, due 2027-03-31) How many gigawatts of new data-center capacity will be added in the United States during calendar 2026, as determined by full-year 2026 grid-operator and utility interconnection/capacity data and industry data-center capacity reports?: now rung 2 from 10 GW to below 15 GW; judged 2026-10-05; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The reference is Huang’s rough 10–20 GW annual estimate: its 10–<15 portion is rung 2, its 15–<20 portion is rung 3, and 20 GW reaches rung 4; the midpoint, 15 GW, is rung 3. I forecast below that midpoint because the quantified trades shortfall, the stated net power shortfall, and the two-to-three-\n- v11 (semiconductor-cycle, due 2027-03-31) What is the percentage change in average DRAM (including HBM) contract prices in calendar 2026 versus calendar 2025, according to TrendForce/DRAMeXchange full-year 2026 contract-price data?: now rung 5 30 percent or more; judged 2026-10-05; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The reference is the opening no-reference position, rung 3 (10% to below 20%); no consensus, company guidance, or TrendForce/DRAMeXchange full-year figure for this measure is supplied. The forecast is above that reference: Micron says the DRAM imbalance has no visible return-to-balance date and that\n- v3 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will U.S. authorities authorize exports of Nvidia’s current Blackwell-generation AI chips to China and Chinese authorities allow those shipments by January 31, 2027, as verified by official export-control or licensing decisions, Chinese regulatory or customs actions, or Nvidia disclosures?: now rung 3 Still open; judged 2026-10-05; reasons: The nearest observable market reference is NVIDIA’s Q3 FY2027 guidance, which assumes no China Data Center compute revenue; that sits at rung 3, Still open, rather than establishing that approval is formally ruled out by the due date. The evidence implies no verified U.S. authorization for Blackwell\n- v5 (ai-capex, due 2027-03-31) What will the five largest AI hyperscalers' combined data-center capital expenditure be in calendar 2026, according to their full-year earnings and capital-expenditure disclosures?: now rung 3 from 750,000,000,000 USD to below 850,000,000,000 USD; judged 2026-10-05; reasons: The reference is Nvidia's estimate of nearly $800 billion for the big five in calendar 2026, which sits in rung 3. Amazon's approximately $220 billion 2026 capex projection, versus its previous $200 billion level, supports about a $20 billion increase to that reference: roughly $820 billion, still w\n- v7 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will China's Ministry of Industry and Information Technology (MIIT) formally approve purchases of Nvidia's RTX Pro 5500 by Alibaba, ByteDance, and other Chinese firms by January 31, 2027?: now rung 1 Yes; judged 2026-09-30; reasons: The reference is rung 3: no market consensus or formal decision is supplied, and the reported status is still deliberation rather than approval or rejection. The forecast nevertheless favors rung 1: the specific report says officials indicated purchases were likely to be approved and MIIT intended t\n- v9 (ai-capex, due 2028-03-30) How much will the five largest AI hyperscalers spend in total on data centers in calendar 2027, as shown by their capex disclosures and reported 2027 results?: now rung 3 from 1,200,000,000,000 USD to below 1,400,000,000,000 USD; judged 2026-10-05; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The reference is Nvidia's projection of $1.3 trillion for calendar 2027, which sits in rung 3. Barclays' approximately $1.1 trillion figure is for cloud capital spending and is not a like-for-like estimate of the five hyperscalers' data-center capex; the demand evidence, including the new customer-g\n\n[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 3; reasons: The supplied market-facing reference is GF Securities’ forecast of 100,000 Nvidia CPO switch shipments in 2027, exactly the rung 1 boundary. Because this is a forecast for a product with no shipment volume shown in hand, it is discounted: the supplier evidence supports optical demand but does not establish Nvidia CPO switch unit volume or convert to 100,000 Nvidia switches. The most likely outcome is therefore below 100,000, while no evidence shows a formal abandonment or other decision against the event as a whole; rung 3 is the best forecast.\n\nAnswer per the JSON schema. cites may name only the evidence ids listed above; cites_views must be empty: this thesis depends on no other thesis; theme_pushes only the theme ids listed above; a theme id goes only in theme_pushes, never in cites or cites_views.",
docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/carried-run/packet-figures-2.json:1327:   "request": "Thesis v1 (NVDA, asset, focus \"The disclosed amount of guarantees, credit-support commitments and revenue backstops NVIDIA has extended to AI labs and cloud partners (including the credit support for nearly 2 gigawatts of computing capacity for one additional AI lab), versus the equity investments it has made, at NVIDIA's next annual report.\", FY2027, due 2027-04-01): What total amount of credit-support commitments and minimum-revenue guarantees to AI labs and cloud partners will NVIDIA disclose in its FY2027 annual report, as determined from the Form 10-K and any 8-K or earnings disclosures quantifying these commitments?\nLadder: rung 1 Strongly bullish: below 5,000,000,000 USD / rung 2 Bullish: from 5,000,000,000 USD to below 15,000,000,000 USD / rung 3 Neutral: from 15,000,000,000 USD to below 30,000,000,000 USD (no reference figure when the question opened) / rung 4 Bearish: from 30,000,000,000 USD to below 50,000,000,000 USD / rung 5 Strongly bearish: 50,000,000,000 USD or more\n\n[Evidence in view under this thesis, 54 items]\n[Not shown: 22 more pieces in force, read in earlier judgements; the previous conclusion carries them]\n- e18 [party_statement; Trefis; original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:5594f685fcb554db910e45f8 2026-09-22] NVIDIA management expects roughly a quarter (about 25%) of the company's fiscal 2028 business to come from AI labs that NVIDIA will back with its own balance sheet.\n    Quote: If you hold NVIDIA (NVDA) stock, one number deserves your attention: roughly a quarter. That is the share of fiscal 2028 business management expects from AI labs it will back with its own balance sheet. … Roughly a quarter of expected fiscal 2028 business comes from AI labs NVIDIA plans to back.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e60, e274, e791, e1050, e1144; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e22 [party_statement; Trefis; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:5594f685fcb554db910e45f8 2026-09-22] NVIDIA plans to provide credit support for nearly 2 gigawatts of computing capacity for one additional AI lab.\n    Quote: For one more AI lab, it will provide credit support for nearly 2 gigawatts of computing capacity.\n- e23 [party_statement; Trefis; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:5594f685fcb554db910e45f8 2026-09-22] NVIDIA guarantees a minimum level of revenue on part of a facility's capacity with some cloud partners.\n    Quote: With some cloud partners, it guarantees a minimum level of revenue on part of a facility’s capacity.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1138; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e39 [fact; Yahoo Finance; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:1b632a31e83a56abe9f3c5fa 2026-09-22] NVIDIA had already invested $1.5 billion in SB Energy and committed another $1.5 billion tied to the developer's IPO.\n    Quote: NVIDIA Corporation (NASDAQ: NVDA ) has already invested $1.5 billion and committed another $1.5 billion tied to the IPO.\n- e41 [fact; Yahoo Finance; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:1b632a31e83a56abe9f3c5fa 2026-09-22] NVIDIA is the exclusive AI-compute provider for SB Energy's PORTS-Pike campus in Ohio and is providing credit support for an initial 4.25 gigawatts of capacity there, with an option covering another 3.75 gigawatts.\n    Quote: Nvidia is the exclusive AI-compute provider for that site and is providing credit support for an initial 4.25 gigawatts, with an option covering another 3.75.\n- e42 [fact; Yahoo Finance; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:1b632a31e83a56abe9f3c5fa 2026-09-22] NVIDIA has disclosed maximum guarantee exposure that can reach $105 billion under defined conditions, tying its economics more closely to OpenAI's ability to meet its lease obligations.\n    Quote: Nvidia has disclosed maximum guarantee exposure that can reach $105 billion under defined conditions, so the economics become more intertwined with OpenAI's ability to meet its lease obligations.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e61, e143, e1063; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e44 [third_party_view; Zacks Research Daily; original source Zacks analyst (Zacks Investment Research) 2026-09-22; from gnews:529d51ca192a19aae5f1c7eb 2026-09-22] A Zacks analyst research report on NVIDIA, published in the 22 September 2026 Zacks Research Daily, stated that NVIDIA is taking on larger supply commitments, customer credit support and long-dated guarantees, while noting that China remains largely closed to data center compute. No amounts, counterparties or dates were given for the increased commitments.\n    Quote: China remains largely closed to data center compute, and NVIDIA is taking on larger supply commitments, customer credit support and long-dated guarantees.\n- e60 [third_party_view; barchart.com; original source barchart.com (article analysis) 2026-09-22; from gnews:355e074116b277efe4dcaaab 2026-09-22] The article reports that NVIDIA is financing a growing share of its sales to AI startups, with roughly 25% of fiscal 2028 revenue expected to come from customers receiving some form of company-backed financing — potentially $175 billion funded off NVIDIA's own balance sheet.\n    Quote: Nvidia is financing a growing share of its sales to AI startups, with roughly 25% of fiscal 2028 revenue expected to come from customers receiving some form of company-backed financing — potentially $175 billion funded off its own balance sheet.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e18; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e61 [third_party_view; barchart.com; original source barchart.com (article analysis) 2026-09-22; from gnews:355e074116b277efe4dcaaab 2026-09-22] The article states that NVIDIA has off-balance-sheet guarantees exceeding $100 billion tied to projects such as the SB Energy campus for OpenAI, which it says introduces credit risk that traditional valuation metrics do not capture.\n    Quote: Additionally, off-balance-sheet guarantees exceeding $100 billion tied to projects like the SB Energy campus for OpenAI introduce credit risk that traditional valuation metrics do not capture.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e42; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e93 [fact; finance.yahoo.com (article, no byline); original source NVIDIA Corporation ; from gnews:c035b24406ae5bac4a8b8c97 2026-09-23] NVIDIA is committing $1 billion to a $3.1 billion convertible financing for AI cloud provider Nscale, the IPO candidate whose filing the article discusses; the article notes this gives NVIDIA direct capital exposure in addition to its role as hardware supplier.\n    Quote: Yet NVIDIA Corporation (NASDAQ: NVDA ) is committing $1 billion to a $3.1 billion convertible financing, while Microsoft Corporation (NASDAQ: MSFT ) is expected to become one of Nscale's most important customers. … Nvidia can still benefit from Nscale's expansion by selling the picks and shovels, although its $1 billion financing commitment gives it direct capital exposure as well.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e195; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e114 [fact; 24/7 Wall St. (finance.yahoo.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:dac89e12f005c43e4ec94571 2026-09-23] The article lists guarantee obligations capped at $108.5 billion among NVIDIA's real risks.\n    Quote: Real risks include supply commitments of $279 billion, guarantee obligations capped at $108.5 billion, and DSO extending to 60 days.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e136, e141, e778; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e135 [fact; Yahoo Finance (finance.yahoo.com); Jensen Huang quoted; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:3e5fcbff91ec0ab7c56e3c7e 2026-09-23] NVIDIA has invested nearly $50 billion in the Frontier AI Labs and helped arrange over $500 billion of third-party capital with six infrastructure providers, and CEO Jensen Huang acknowledged the scale of that support while saying the company does not see it as 'circular financing'.\n    Quote: The louder concern is circular financing. NVIDIA has invested nearly $50 billion in the Frontier AI Labs and helped arrange over $500 billion of third-party capital with six infrastructure providers. CEO Jensen Huang addressed the skepticism directly: \"We recognize the scale of this support, and we know some will call this circular financing. We see it differently.\"\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e20; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e136 [fact; Yahoo Finance (finance.yahoo.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:3e5fcbff91ec0ab7c56e3c7e 2026-09-23] The article reports that NVIDIA's supply commitments have surged to $279 billion and its guarantee obligations are capped at $108.5 billion, describing both as real balance-sheet exposures if demand slips.\n    Quote: Bear: the sale is large, outside a prearranged plan, and arrives as the market questions whether the spending cycle is self-funded. Supply commitments surged to $279 billion, and guarantee obligations are capped at $108.5 billion. Those are real balance-sheet exposures if demand slips.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e114; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e143 [fact; fool.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:7f14bde8195bae63bcb71249 2026-09-23] NVIDIA agreed to guarantee up to $105 billion of data center lease obligations for OpenAI.\n    Quote: For example, Nvidia agreed to guarantee up to $105 billion of data center lease obligations for OpenAI.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e42; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e196 [fact; Beatrice Nolan (Fortune), citing Nscale's U.K. company accounts filed October 2025; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:0a4dc52800a886079fb749e0 2026-09-24] Nscale's own U.K. company accounts, filed in October 2025, showed that Nvidia bought warrants for over 157,945 Nscale shares for $60 million and, in exchange, agreed to guarantee up to $860.3 million of Nscale's lease obligations at a facility in Ward County, Texas.\n    Quote: Previous filings from Nscale’s own U.K. company accounts in October 2025 showed that Nvidia bought warrants for over 157,945 Nscale shares for $60 million—and in exchange, agreed to guarantee up to $860.3 million of Nscale’s lease obligations at a facility in Ward County, Texas.\n- e220 [fact; techrepublic.com; original source Aolani 2026-09-21; from gnews:fd2ffd8b19aaab49b7359ce8 2026-09-24] Aolani said its arrangement with Nvidia includes a revenue-sharing and credit-support model intended to align infrastructure deployment with customer demand; the financial terms of that revenue-sharing and credit-support model were not disclosed.\n    Quote: Aolani’s arrangement with Nvidia also includes a revenue-sharing and credit-support model intended to align infrastructure deployment with customer demand. Financial terms were not disclosed.\n- e328 [rumour; Daniel Sparks, fool.com; original source Reuters (unnamed source) ; from gnews:8907543619da0857c9de2b29 2026-09-26] Nvidia is in talks to be an anchor investor in Anthropic's planned initial public offering, according to Reuters, and an unnamed source put Nvidia's possible investment at up to $10 billion; the article describes it as a possible second $10 billion check.\n    Quote: Nvidia ( NVDA +2.55% ) might be gearing up to write Anthropic a second $10 billion check.\nThe chipmaker's in talks to be an anchor investor in Anthropic's planned initial public offering (IPO) , Reuters said this month, a deal that could deepen its ties to a major customer. A source put Nvidia's possible investment at up to $10 billion.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e798; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e335 [fact; Daniel Sparks, fool.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:8907543619da0857c9de2b29 2026-09-26] Nvidia carried stakes in private companies at $47.9 billion on its books as of July 26, 2026, compared with $22.3 billion in late January 2026 when its fiscal year started and $3.8 billion a year earlier; including public holdings, its equity investments came to $99 billion as of July 26, with another $25 billion committed.\n    Quote: Nvidia carried its stakes in private companies on its books at $47.9 billion as of July 26. That compares to $22.3 billion in late January, when its fiscal year started, and just $3.8 billion a year earlier. Including public holdings, the company's equity investments came to $99 billion as of July 26, with another $25 billion committed.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e21; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e498 [rumour; Financial Times (reported by Investing.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:62d89ab530bbd287d3772f1a 2026-09-28] NVIDIA has held talks with insurance companies about ways to shift part of the financing risk tied to its chips as it seeks to expand demand beyond major technology companies, including potential insurance for loans extended to smaller \"neocloud\" companies that would protect lenders if a borrower defaults and NVIDIA chips pledged as collateral cannot be resold for enough to cover the debt, the Financial Times reported, citing people familiar with the talks; the discussions are at an early stage and may not result in any deals.\n    Quote: NVIDIA Corp (NASDAQ:NVDA) has held talks with insurance companies over ways to shift part of the financing risk tied to its chips as the chipmaker seeks to expand demand beyond major technology companies, the Financial Times reported on Tuesday. … The discussions include potential insurance for loans extended to smaller \"neocloud\" companies, which could protect lenders if a borrower defaults and N\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e581, e599, e738; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e499 [rumour; Financial Times (reported by Investing.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:62d89ab530bbd287d3772f1a 2026-09-28] NVIDIA has shared data on chip depreciation and the expected future value of computing power with at least one insurer, and is working with broker Howden Re on a structure involving insurers, people familiar with the discussions told the Financial Times (Howden declined to comment); NVIDIA has also explored using insurance groups to syndicate risk to hedge funds and other alternative investors, with the potential size of deals possibly exceeding the balance sheets of even large insurers, and has considered joining consortia alongside insurers, hedge funds and asset managers.\n    Quote: Nvidia has shared data on chip depreciation and the expected future value of computing power with at least one insurer, while people familiar with the discussions said the company was also working with broker Howden Re on a structure involving insurers. Howden declined to comment, the report said. … The potential structures could extend beyond traditional insurers. Nvidia has explored using insura\n- e500 [fact; Financial Times (reported by Investing.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:62d89ab530bbd287d3772f1a 2026-09-28] NVIDIA has offered to backstop part of financing deals designed to unlock $500 billion of capital from Wall Street firms including Goldman Sachs and Apollo, according to the Financial Times; the backstop offer is reported as preceding the insurer talks.\n    Quote: The insurance push comes after Nvidia offered to backstop part of financing deals designed to unlock $500 billion of capital from Wall Street firms including Goldman Sachs and Apollo.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e582, e828, e992; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e581 [rumour; Financial Times (reported by Yahoo Finance); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:791d301ec1a1c19ef4c6fbbe 2026-09-29] NVIDIA has held early-stage discussions with insurance companies, working with insurance broker Howden Re, about potential structures to transfer part of the financing risk associated with its chips, including potentially insuring loans made to smaller \"neocloud\" companies so lenders are protected if a borrower defaults and NVIDIA chips pledged as collateral cannot be resold for enough to repay the debt. The effort is part of CEO Jensen Huang's push to make NVIDIA chips and AI infrastructure easier for external investors to finance; NVIDIA provided at least one insurer with information on chip depreciation and the expected future value of computing capacity, explored arrangements under which insurers could syndicate risk to hedge funds and other alternative investors, and considered participating in financing consortia alongside insurers, hedge funds and asset managers. The FT, citing people familiar with the matter, said the talks remain at an early stage and may not result in agreements; no pricing, counterparties or amount of financing that could be covered was disclosed.\n    Quote: Nvidia (NASDAQ:NVDA) has held discussions with insurance companies about potential structures to transfer part of the financing risk associated with its chips as the company seeks to expand demand for artificial intelligence infrastructure beyond major technology groups, the Financial Times reported. … The discussions include potentially insuring loans made to smaller \"neocloud\" companies, accordi\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e498; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e582 [fact; Financial Times (reported by Yahoo Finance); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:791d301ec1a1c19ef4c6fbbe 2026-09-29] According to the FT-sourced material cited in the article, NVIDIA has offered to backstop part of financing deals intended to unlock $500 billion of capital from Wall Street firms including Goldman Sachs and Apollo. The item does not state the size of NVIDIA's backstop portion or the timing of the offer.\n    Quote: According to the supplied material, Nvidia has offered to backstop part of financing deals intended to unlock $500 billion of capital from Wall Street firms including Goldman Sachs and Apollo.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e500; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e778 [fact; finance.yahoo.com article (contributor piece); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:890bfe7d206f0531a54beb16 2026-10-01] NVIDIA's guarantee obligations for partners are capped at $108.5 billion, according to the article.\n    Quote: Supply obligations reached $279.0 billion, mostly memory for Vera Rubin, and guarantee obligations for partners are capped at $108.5 billion.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e114; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e791 [party_statement; finance.yahoo.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:9126c31bb44c2cc3f54c641a 2026-10-01] NVIDIA management said on the fiscal Q2 2027 earnings call that the company is using its own balance sheet to support purchases by some AI labs, that those AI labs should contribute roughly a quarter of its business the following year, and that some will call this support circular financing (NVIDIA helping fund customers who then buy from NVIDIA).\n    Quote: NVIDIA is also using its own balance sheet to support purchases by some AI labs. On the fiscal Q2 2027 call, management said those AI labs should contribute roughly a quarter of its business the following year. Management also said some people will call that support circular financing. The term means NVIDIA helps fund customers who then buy from NVIDIA.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e18; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e796 [party_statement; Yahoo Finance; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:a0b58e332b9b0027cd82bb5a 2026-10-01] NVIDIA outlined more than US$500 billion of compute financing agreements and plans with partners including Apollo, BlackRock, Blackstone, Brookfield, Goldman Sachs and KKR, intended to mobilize more than US$500 billion of third-party capital for AI infrastructure over time.\n    Quote: Nvidia outlined more than US$500b of compute financing agreements and plans with partners such as Apollo, BlackRock, Blackstone, Brookfield, Goldman Sachs and KKR to mobilize over US$500b of third party capital for AI infrastructure over time.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e830, e1011, e1049, e1129, e1137, e1164; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e798 [rumour; Yahoo Finance; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:a0b58e332b9b0027cd82bb5a 2026-10-01] NVIDIA is in talks to invest up to US$10 billion in Anthropic, and is pursuing further, unsized investments in AI firms including Perplexity and Decart AI, according to the article; no terms or completion are reported.\n    Quote: is in talks to invest up to US$10b in Anthropic, and is pursuing further investments in AI firms including Perplexity and Decart AI\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e328; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e828 [party_statement; gurufocus.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:ff3c940a31d608112807883e 2026-10-01] Nvidia said independent financiers in its $500 billion infrastructure-financing push will assess customer quality, utilization, cash flow and equipment values project by project, and that Nvidia may provide residual-value support for up to 25% of selected opportunities — a contingent credit backstop that would support lenders' recoveries on GPU-backed customer financing.\n    Quote: Nvidia says independent financiers will assess customer quality, utilization, cash flow and equipment values project by project. It may provide residual-value support for up to 25% of selected opportunities.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e500; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e829 [third_party_view; Reuters, via gurufocus.com; original source Credit investors (cited by Reuters) ; from gnews:ff3c940a31d608112807883e 2026-10-01] Credit investors, as reported by Reuters, said banks commonly depreciate GPUs over three to four years, while Nvidia argues its leading systems can keep earning money much longer; the article says that gap in collateral values could mean more equity upfront, tighter lending terms or higher borrowing costs for GPU-backed financing.\n    Quote: According to Reuters, credit investors said banks commonly depreciate GPUs over three to four years, while Nvidia argues its leading systems can keep earning money much longer. That gap matters. Lower collateral values could mean more equity upfront, tighter lending terms or higher borrowing costs.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e994; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e830 [third_party_view; gurufocus.com; original source gurufocus.com ; from gnews:ff3c940a31d608112807883e 2026-10-01] The article states that Nvidia's $500 billion infrastructure-financing push is a capital-mobilization target over time, rather than booked chip sales or a fully funded pool of cash.\n    Quote: The $500 billion is a capital-mobilization target over time, rather than booked chip sales or a fully funded pool of cash.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e796; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e927 [fact; The Information, reported by TradingView/Stocktwits; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:d91c54ec44da3c9a5cbef369 2026-10-02] Nvidia completed its final $10 billion investment in OpenAI's latest funding round, according to The Information, part of the strategic capital backing OpenAI and its plans to expand AI infrastructure.\n    Quote: Nvidia has also completed its final $10 billion investment, showcasing the size of the strategic capital backing OpenAI and its plans to expand AI infrastructure.\n- e952 [fact; finance.yahoo.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:d38374c4abf4bd13d2cd2ccd 2026-10-02] Nvidia committed up to $100 billion to OpenAI in September 2025, but its final stake came to $30 billion, with another $10 billion going to Anthropic, according to the article.\n    Quote: Nvidia also funds its own customers. It committed up to $100 billion to OpenAI in September 2025, but the final stake came to $30 billion, with another $10 billion going to Anthropic.\n- e961 [rumour; fxleaders.com; original source Unnamed lenders and credit investors (reported) ; from gnews:4d8fb36192b66cfb50e49f46 2026-10-02] Lenders reportedly question Nvidia's plans to help finance up to $500 billion of infrastructure expansion, with banks and credit investors said to be assessing whether advanced chips will retain enough value over time to serve as reliable collateral and some lenders reportedly seeking broader guarantees and additional contractual protections. Unconfirmed, attributed to unnamed lenders; it does not establish that Nvidia has granted any guarantee or credit support.\n    Quote: The financing discussion comes as lenders reportedly question Nvidia’s plans to help finance up to $500 billion of infrastructure expansion. … Banks and credit investors are said to be assessing whether advanced chips will retain enough value over time to serve as reliable collateral. Some lenders are reportedly seeking broader guarantees and additional contractual protections.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e988, e1017, e1034; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e988 [rumour; Reuters; original source Unnamed banking sources and credit managers ; from gnews:d2db7f7e14e0a30d3e7a23de 2026-10-03] Unnamed banking sources and credit managers told Reuters that some lenders want stronger guarantees than Nvidia initially outlined for its chip-backed financing plan, even for Nvidia's AI chips, as they try to determine how long the chips can generate revenue. The sources also said bankers and asset managers doubt Nvidia's chips can serve as long-term collateral on their own and want greater protections around Nvidia's $500 billion financing plan.\n    Quote: Some lenders want stronger guarantees than the company initially outlined, even for Nvidia’s industry-leading AI processing power, as they try to determine how long the revenue-generating life of its chips will last, banking sources and credit managers told Reuters. Several requested anonymity to discuss the financing structures. … Bankers and asset managers said they have doubts about whether Nvi\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e961; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e989 [fact; Reuters; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:d2db7f7e14e0a30d3e7a23de 2026-10-03] Nvidia announced in August a $500 billion AI financing plan with financiers including Blackstone, Apollo and KKR that envisioned using Nvidia chips as collateral with limited guarantees to give AI developers access to Nvidia compute.\n    Quote: Nvidia’s plan, announced in August with financiers including Blackstone, Apollo and KKR, envisioned using its chips as collateral with limited guarantees, allowing AI developers to gain access to Nvidia’s compute. The strategy was seen as an expensive but potentially necessary way to create a financing market similar to the one used to finance aircraft.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1154; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e990 [party_statement; Reuters; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:d2db7f7e14e0a30d3e7a23de 2026-10-03] Nvidia has said some deals under its $500 billion financing initiative could carry a residual-value guarantee of no more than 25%, arguing the initiative is designed to address concerns over circular financing.\n    Quote: Nvidia has said some deals could carry a residual-value guarantee of no more than 25%, arguing that the initiative is designed to address concerns over circular financing.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1130; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of e961 (); both are in force: Lenders reportedly question Nvidia's plans to help finance up to $500 billion of infrastructure expansion, with banks and credit investors said to be assessing whether advanced chips will retain enough value over time to serve as reliable collateral and some lenders reportedly seeking broader guarantees and additional contractual protections. Unconfirmed, attributed to unnamed lenders; it does not establish that Nvidia has granted any guarantee or credit support.\n- e991 [rumour; Reuters; original source Three unnamed banking sources ; from gnews:d2db7f7e14e0a30d3e7a23de 2026-10-03] Three banking sources not part of Nvidia's original financing group said Nvidia may ultimately need to provide guarantees on all of its financing deals, or have them backed by revenue streams from investment-grade customers such as major technology companies to cover debt payments. The sources added that the market is not yet ready to treat AI compute as an investment-grade asset comparable to aircraft.\n    Quote: Three banking sources familiar with the matter but not part of the original financing group said Nvidia may ultimately need to provide guarantees on all of its deals, or have them backed by revenue streams from investment-grade customers such as major technology companies to cover debt payments. … For now, the sources said, the market is not yet ready to treat AI compute as an investment-grade ass\n- e992 [rumour; Reuters; original source Unnamed banking sources ; from gnews:d2db7f7e14e0a30d3e7a23de 2026-10-03] Banking sources said Nvidia is providing previously unreported details to address lender concerns, and that tens of billions of dollars in loan deals in the pipeline are likely to include stronger guarantees and contractual protections. Some structures under consideration could provide lenders with guarantees, and the initial deals will be secured by Nvidia chips and backed by customer contracts and Nvidia's underlying guarantee, according to the sources.\n    Quote: Nvidia is seeking to address some of these concerns, the sources said, providing details that had not previously been reported. … Tens of billions of dollars in loan deals currently in the pipeline are likely to include stronger guarantees and contractual protections, one source said. A second source said some structures under consideration could provide lenders with guarantees. … The initial deal\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e500; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e993 [party_statement; Reuters; original source NVIDIA (CEO Jensen Huang; Nvidia citing third-party studies) ; from gnews:d2db7f7e14e0a30d3e7a23de 2026-10-03] Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang has argued Nvidia GPUs have a useful life of up to a decade, and Nvidia has cited third-party studies showing major cloud companies extending server depreciation periods to five or six years from three or four, plus a Barkr finding that GB300 NVL72 systems could have a useful life of nine to 10 years.\n    Quote: Huang has addressed some of the skepticism by arguing that Nvidia’s graphics processing units, or GPUs, have a useful life of up to a decade. … Nvidia has pointed to several third-party studies showing that major cloud companies are extending the depreciation periods for servers to five or six years, from three or four. … The company has also cited a finding from Barkr, which provides valuations f\n- e994 [third_party_view; Reuters; original source Tony Trzcinka (Impax Asset Management); Andrew Chang (S&P Global Ratings) ; from gnews:d2db7f7e14e0a30d3e7a23de 2026-10-03] Impax Asset Management's Tony Trzcinka said banks typically underwrite GPUs over a 3-4 year depreciation schedule, versus Nvidia's decade claim, and that investors are likely to demand higher interest rates, larger financial cushions and stronger repayment protections before backing chip-secured loans. S&P Global Ratings' Andrew Chang said S&P takes a conservative view of the chips' value.\n    Quote: “Banks typically underwrite GPUs over a 3-4 year depreciation schedule,” Trzcinka of Impax said. … “That is different than Nvidia, which argues top-tier GPUs can earn revenue for a decade,” he said, adding that investors are likely to demand higher interest rates, larger financial cushions and stronger repayment protections before backing loans secured by AI chips. … “Nvidia would imply that the G\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e829; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e995 [fact; Reuters, citing S&P and Moody's; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:d2db7f7e14e0a30d3e7a23de 2026-10-03] Nvidia previously provided a residual-value guarantee to support financing for SB Energy's Ohio data center project, according to S&P and Moody's.\n    Quote: Nvidia itself previously provided a residual-value guarantee to support financing for SB Energy’s Ohio data center project, according to S&P and Moody’s.\n- e1048 [fact; Yahoo Finance; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:ed14a9d1371ea43f3c5dcd3d 2026-10-04] NVIDIA reported $279 billion in supply commitments and guarantee obligations limited at $108.5 billion for AI cloud and data center partners.\n    Quote: NVIDIA reported $279 billion in supply commitments and guarantee obligations limited at $108.5 billion for AI cloud and data center partners.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e113; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e1050 [party_statement; Yahoo Finance; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:ed14a9d1371ea43f3c5dcd3d 2026-10-04] NVIDIA expects the AI labs it supports to account for roughly a quarter of its business next year.\n    Quote: NVIDIA expects the AI labs it supports to account for roughly a quarter of its business next year.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e18; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e1063 [fact; finance.yahoo.com; original source NVIDIA Corporation ; from gnews:b0e3eb6b8404ada08d7535e1 2026-10-04] NVIDIA disclosed in August guarantees capped at $105 billion for an OpenAI-related SB Energy buildout. The guarantees generally begin as the relevant leases commence, with the first construction phase expected in fiscal 2029, and payments depend on specified tenant defaults; they cover defined lease and power payments and decline over the leases. The article describes this as contingent exposure rather than a current $105 billion cash bill.\n    Quote: NVIDIA also disclosed August guarantees capped at $105 billion for an OpenAI-related SB Energy buildout. They generally begin as the relevant leases commence, with the first construction phase expected in fiscal 2029, and payments depend on specified tenant defaults. The guarantees cover defined lease and power payments and decline over the leases; they are contingent exposure, not a current $105 \n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e42; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e1106 [fact; Benzinga; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:3785635fc4c5f95c340a6f52 2026-10-05] Nvidia is backing AI infrastructure by providing credit support for 4.25 GW at SB Energy's Ohio campus and investing $1.5 billion in the developer, according to the article.\n    Quote: Nvidia is already backing that infrastructure, providing credit support for 4.25 GW at SB Energy's Ohio campus and investing $1.5 billion in the developer.\n- e1130 [third_party_view; Morningstar; original source Morningstar 2026-10-05; from gnews:62b41f9768d1b65460f326ca 2026-10-05] Morningstar estimates that Nvidia may backstop up to 25% of qualifying financings under its partnership with Apollo, BlackRock, Blackstone, Brookfield, Goldman Sachs and KKR, equivalent to a maximum potential exposure of $125 billion if applied across the full target. It adds that Nvidia's actual balance-sheet exposure will depend on its collateral, the transaction volume and structure, and the final allocation of credit and residual-value risk.\n    Quote: In addition, Nvidia may backstop up to 25% of qualifying financings (equivalent to a maximum potential exposure of $125 billion), if applied across the full target. The company’s actual balance sheet exposure will depend on its collateral, the transaction volume and structure, and the final allocation of credit and residual-value risk.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e990; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e1136 [party_statement; Trefis; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-26; from gnews:02fb6007185a3496e48b4046 2026-10-05] On its fiscal Q2 2027 earnings call on 26 August 2026, NVIDIA management said the company has invested nearly $50 billion in frontier AI labs.\n    Quote: On its fiscal Q2 2027 call on August 26, 2026, management said NVIDIA has invested nearly $50 billion in frontier AI labs.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e20; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e1137 [party_statement; Trefis; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-26; from gnews:02fb6007185a3496e48b4046 2026-10-05] On the same fiscal Q2 2027 call (26 August 2026), NVIDIA announced partnerships with six infrastructure investors to set up financing platforms that are planned to raise over $500 billion of outside capital.\n    Quote: It also announced partnerships with six infrastructure investors to set up financing platforms that will raise over $500 billion of outside capital.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e796; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e1138 [fact; Trefis; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:02fb6007185a3496e48b4046 2026-10-05] NVIDIA structures its dealings with NeoClouds (newer AI-focused cloud providers) by guaranteeing a minimum level of revenue on part of a NeoCloud facility's capacity and taking a share of what the NeoCloud earns above that level, according to the article's description of the arrangement.\n    Quote: NVIDIA goes further for NeoClouds, the newer cloud providers built for AI. It guarantees a minimum level of revenue on part of a NeoCloud facility’s capacity, and it takes a share of what the NeoCloud earns above that level.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e23; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e1144 [party_statement; Trefis; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:02fb6007185a3496e48b4046 2026-10-05] NVIDIA management said in August 2026 that it expects the AI labs it supports with its balance sheet to contribute roughly a quarter of its business in the following year, and acknowledged that some will call this circular financing.\n    Quote: Management said in August that it expects the AI labs it supports with its balance sheet to contribute roughly a quarter of its business in the following year. It also acknowledged that some will call this circular financing.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e18; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e1146 [fact; Trefis; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:02fb6007185a3496e48b4046 2026-10-05] The wait for NVIDIA to be paid rose to 60 days in fiscal Q2 2027 as the company gave certain investment-grade customers longer payment terms on large purchases; the article flags the fiscal Q3 2027 call, scheduled for 17 November 2026, as the next checkpoint on that figure.\n    Quote: The fiscal Q3 2027 call is scheduled for November 17. One figure to follow then is how long NVIDIA waits to be paid. That wait rose to 60 days in Q2 as NVIDIA gave certain investment-grade customers longer payment terms on large purchases.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e24; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e1154 [fact; Darryn Pollock, beincrypto.com (republished by Yahoo Finance), citing CNBC; original source Nvidia (announcement), as reported by CNBC ; from gnews:76c7aa5a044eaaa4dbb5521c 2026-10-05] Nvidia announced in August [2026] preliminary agreements with six financial firms for about $500 billion in customer financing, according to CNBC, aimed at funding customers that buy its chips. The agreements were described as preliminary; the item gives no signing or closing date and does not state how much of the financing, if any, Nvidia itself guarantees or backstops.\n    Quote: Nvidia invests in customers that buy its chips. In August, it announced preliminary agreements with six financial firms for about $500 billion in customer financing, CNBC reported.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e989; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e1164 [fact; The Motley Fool (Daniel Foelber); original source NVIDIA (with a consortium of six financial institutions) ; from gnews:b0cf215ea487392e67e586be 2026-10-05] The Motley Fool reported that Nvidia is partnering with a consortium of six financial institutions to fund $500 billion in AI infrastructure, an arrangement that enables customers to finance their compute needs rather than buying chips directly. The item does not state how much of the $500 billion Nvidia itself bears or whether Nvidia guarantees any of the financing.\n    Quote: Demand is so massive that Nvidia is partnering with a consortium of six financial institutions to fund $500 billion in AI infrastructure, enabling customers to finance their compute needs rather than buying chips directly.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e796; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e1174 [fact; Trefis; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:6bfaa6b41767a6c301bdec6d 2026-10-05] NVIDIA is funding some of its own customers through investments, having put nearly $50 billion into AI labs, according to Trefis.\n    Quote: NVIDIA is also funding some of its own customers: it has invested nearly $50 billion in AI labs.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e20; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n\n[Themes in force, 6]\n- v10 (ai-capex, due 2027-03-31) How many gigawatts of new data-center capacity will be added in the United States during calendar 2026, as determined by full-year 2026 grid-operator and utility interconnection/capacity data and industry data-center capacity reports?: now rung 2 from 10 GW to below 15 GW; judged 2026-10-05; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The reference is Huang’s rough 10–20 GW annual estimate: its 10–<15 portion is rung 2, its 15–<20 portion is rung 3, and 20 GW reaches rung 4; the midpoint, 15 GW, is rung 3. I forecast below that midpoint because the quantified trades shortfall, the stated net power shortfall, and the two-to-three-\n- v11 (semiconductor-cycle, due 2027-03-31) What is the percentage change in average DRAM (including HBM) contract prices in calendar 2026 versus calendar 2025, according to TrendForce/DRAMeXchange full-year 2026 contract-price data?: now rung 5 30 percent or more; judged 2026-10-05; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The reference is the opening no-reference position, rung 3 (10% to below 20%); no consensus, company guidance, or TrendForce/DRAMeXchange full-year figure for this measure is supplied. The forecast is above that reference: Micron says the DRAM imbalance has no visible return-to-balance date and that\n- v3 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will U.S. authorities authorize exports of Nvidia’s current Blackwell-generation AI chips to China and Chinese authorities allow those shipments by January 31, 2027, as verified by official export-control or licensing decisions, Chinese regulatory or customs actions, or Nvidia disclosures?: now rung 3 Still open; judged 2026-10-05; reasons: The nearest observable market reference is NVIDIA’s Q3 FY2027 guidance, which assumes no China Data Center compute revenue; that sits at rung 3, Still open, rather than establishing that approval is formally ruled out by the due date. The evidence implies no verified U.S. authorization for Blackwell\n- v5 (ai-capex, due 2027-03-31) What will the five largest AI hyperscalers' combined data-center capital expenditure be in calendar 2026, according to their full-year earnings and capital-expenditure disclosures?: now rung 3 from 750,000,000,000 USD to below 850,000,000,000 USD; judged 2026-10-05; reasons: The reference is Nvidia's estimate of nearly $800 billion for the big five in calendar 2026, which sits in rung 3. Amazon's approximately $220 billion 2026 capex projection, versus its previous $200 billion level, supports about a $20 billion increase to that reference: roughly $820 billion, still w\n- v7 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will China's Ministry of Industry and Information Technology (MIIT) formally approve purchases of Nvidia's RTX Pro 5500 by Alibaba, ByteDance, and other Chinese firms by January 31, 2027?: now rung 1 Yes; judged 2026-09-30; reasons: The reference is rung 3: no market consensus or formal decision is supplied, and the reported status is still deliberation rather than approval or rejection. The forecast nevertheless favors rung 1: the specific report says officials indicated purchases were likely to be approved and MIIT intended t\n- v9 (ai-capex, due 2028-03-30) How much will the five largest AI hyperscalers spend in total on data centers in calendar 2027, as shown by their capex disclosures and reported 2027 results?: now rung 3 from 1,200,000,000,000 USD to below 1,400,000,000,000 USD; judged 2026-10-05; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The reference is Nvidia's projection of $1.3 trillion for calendar 2027, which sits in rung 3. Barclays' approximately $1.1 trillion figure is for cloud capital spending and is not a like-for-like estimate of the five hyperscalers' data-center capex; the demand evidence, including the new customer-g\n\n[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 5; reasons: The reference is the opening no-reference position, rung 3 ($15bn to below $30bn). The strongest quantified evidence is NVIDIA's reported $108.5bn cap on guarantee obligations, already above rung 5's $50bn threshold. The $105bn OpenAI-related guarantee is part of the existing exposure, not an amount to add to the $108.5bn cap; the $500bn financing plan is capital mobilization, not NVIDIA's guarantee amount, and its up-to-25% residual-value support applies only to selected opportunities. Reports of stronger protections in financing deals could add exposure, but do not size NVIDIA's share. The implied disclosed amount therefore remains at least $108.5bn, firmly in rung 5.\n\nAnswer per the JSON schema. cites may name only the evidence ids listed above; cites_views must be empty: this thesis depends on no other thesis; theme_pushes only the theme ids listed above; a theme id goes only in theme_pushes, never in cites or cites_views.",
docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/carried-run/packet-figures-2.json:1345:   "request": "Thesis v2 (NVDA, asset, focus \"The amount of purchase obligations/supply commitments NVIDIA reports in its contractual obligations and purchase commitment disclosures in the Form 10-K, versus the prior year, given the analyst report that NVIDIA is taking on larger supply commitments.\", FY2027, due 2027-04-01): How much will NVIDIA report in purchase obligations and supply commitments in its FY2027 Form 10-K disclosure?\nLadder: rung 1 Strongly bullish: below 50,000,000,000 USD / rung 2 Bullish: from 50,000,000,000 USD to below 75,000,000,000 USD / rung 3 Neutral: from 75,000,000,000 USD to below 100,000,000,000 USD (no reference figure when the question opened) / rung 4 Bearish: from 100,000,000,000 USD to below 125,000,000,000 USD / rung 5 Strongly bearish: 125,000,000,000 USD or more\n\n[Evidence in view under this thesis, 29 items]\n[Not shown: 8 more pieces in force, read in earlier judgements; the previous conclusion carries them]\n- e44 [third_party_view; Zacks Research Daily; original source Zacks analyst (Zacks Investment Research) 2026-09-22; from gnews:529d51ca192a19aae5f1c7eb 2026-09-22] A Zacks analyst research report on NVIDIA, published in the 22 September 2026 Zacks Research Daily, stated that NVIDIA is taking on larger supply commitments, customer credit support and long-dated guarantees, while noting that China remains largely closed to data center compute. No amounts, counterparties or dates were given for the increased commitments.\n    Quote: China remains largely closed to data center compute, and NVIDIA is taking on larger supply commitments, customer credit support and long-dated guarantees.\n- e106 [party_statement; fool.com (article author); original source NVIDIA (management; CFO Colette Kress) ; from gnews:46b3435210a9cc3557b44d78 2026-09-23] On NVIDIA's fiscal 2027 second-quarter earnings call, CFO Colette Kress said customer forecasts point to the company's growth doubling next year, but that NVIDIA expects to grow approximately 70% because it is supply-constrained; NVIDIA's fiscal 2027 second-quarter report forecast 70% sales growth for fiscal 2028, and leadership said sales could double in fiscal 2028 if constraints on the supply of commodities needed for its hardware were overcome.\n    Quote: In its fiscal 2027 second-quarter report, Management forecast that in fiscal 2028, its sales will rise by 70%, thanks to continued demand for AI chips. … But what's really interesting is that Nvidia leadership said it could double its sales in fiscal 2028 if it were able to overcome the constraints on the supply of commodities needed to produce its hardware. … On Nvidia's second-quarter fiscal 202\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e104; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e108 [third_party_view; fool.com (article author); original source Micron Technology (as reported) ; from gnews:46b3435210a9cc3557b44d78 2026-09-23] The article reports that Micron Technology's memory chips are in severe shortage, with demand far outpacing supply, which has driven skyrocketing memory prices, higher margins for Micron and global supply constraints.\n    Quote: For example, Micron Technology 's memory chips are having a significant problem, with demand far outpacing supply. That's led to skyrocketing memory prices, higher margins for Micron, and global supply constraints.\n- e113 [fact; 24/7 Wall St. (finance.yahoo.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:dac89e12f005c43e4ec94571 2026-09-23] The article lists supply commitments of $279 billion among NVIDIA's real risks.\n    Quote: Real risks include supply commitments of $279 billion, guarantee obligations capped at $108.5 billion, and DSO extending to 60 days.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e202, e526, e683, e766, e777, e1048, e1062, e1073; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e136 [fact; Yahoo Finance (finance.yahoo.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:3e5fcbff91ec0ab7c56e3c7e 2026-09-23] The article reports that NVIDIA's supply commitments have surged to $279 billion and its guarantee obligations are capped at $108.5 billion, describing both as real balance-sheet exposures if demand slips.\n    Quote: Bear: the sale is large, outside a prearranged plan, and arrives as the market questions whether the spending cycle is self-funded. Supply commitments surged to $279 billion, and guarantee obligations are capped at $108.5 billion. Those are real balance-sheet exposures if demand slips.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e114; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e202 [fact; Zacks (zacks.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:7fb0b2b7d22b8bb07625b212 2026-09-24] NVIDIA had committed $279 billion to secure supply and production capacity as of the end of the second quarter of fiscal 2027, up from $119 billion at the end of the first quarter, with most commitments tied to memory and manufacturing facilities needed for current and future data center products, Zacks reports.\n    Quote: At the end of the second quarter of fiscal 2027, the company had committed $279 billion to secure supply and production capacity, up from $119 billion in the first quarter. … Most commitments are tied to memory and manufacturing facilities needed for current and future data center products.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e113; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e236 [party_statement; Trefis; original source NVIDIA (CFO) ; from gnews:06218d9421731e5e59c1dbef 2026-09-24] NVIDIA's CFO expects supply to remain a bottleneck at least through the end of fiscal 2028 and calls memory pricing extreme; the article reports memory prices have risen more than management expected.\n    Quote: The CFO expects supply to stay a bottleneck at least through the end of fiscal 2028 and calls memory pricing extreme. … Memory prices have risen more than management expected, and the guide has that margin settling in the 72% to 73% range in fiscal 2028, helped by price increases NVIDIA has already executed.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e271, e413, e414, e556, e653, e774, e864, e865; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e271 [party_statement; Trefis (trefis.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:a6d9db55f2f71f072c5ba33c 2026-09-25] NVIDIA management expects supply to remain a bottleneck at least through the end of fiscal 2028, as summarised by Trefis from the latest earnings call.\n    Quote: Management expects supply to remain a bottleneck at least through the end of fiscal 2028.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e236; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e272 [party_statement; Trefis (trefis.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:a6d9db55f2f71f072c5ba33c 2026-09-25] NVIDIA reset its gross margin expectations after memory prices rose more than it expected, guiding gross margin to 74% for fiscal Q3 2027, down from 75% in fiscal Q2 2027, and expecting gross margin to bottom at 71% to 72% in fiscal Q4 2027.\n    Quote: Memory prices rose more than NVIDIA expected, and NVIDIA reset its margin expectations. Gross margin is the share of sales left after production costs. NVIDIA guided it to 74% for fiscal Q3, down from 75% in Q2. NVIDIA expects gross margin to bottom at 71% to 72% in fiscal Q4.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e557, e682, e866, e1123, e1173; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of e49 (); both are in force: Analyst estimates compiled by Bloomberg show NVIDIA's gross margin sliding to under 72% by the fourth quarter, with a recovery expected thereafter, as reported by qz.com; rising costs for key components such as memory chips are cited as a central factor.\n- e393 [fact; Geoffrey Seiler (Fool.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:3e0b54b2bb227716c646f29f 2026-09-27] According to Fool.com columnist Geoffrey Seiler, Nvidia has locked in $500 billion worth of supply-constrained high-bandwidth memory (HBM), the memory used to optimize its AI chips. The item gives no counterparty, contract term or date for the commitment.\n    Quote: It locked in $500 billion worth of supply constrained high-bandwidth memory (HBM), which is needed to optimize AI chips\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1040; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e523 [rumour; GuruFocus (gurufocus.com); original source Nvidia, Apple, Advanced Micro Devices, Qualcomm and MediaTek (as reported) ; from gnews:896d25da7b9b8340a736b2c0 2026-09-28] Nvidia, together with Apple, Advanced Micro Devices, Qualcomm and MediaTek, is reportedly raising its 2-nanometer orders at TSMC by 10% to 20%, according to the GuruFocus article of 28 September 2026, with the article tying the increase to customers preparing next-generation processors and AI accelerators.\n    Quote: Apple ( NASDAQ:AAPL ), Nvidia ( NASDAQ:NVDA ), Advanced Micro Devices, Qualcomm and MediaTek are reportedly lifting 2nm orders by 10% to 20%. That gives TSMC additional visibility as customers prepare next-generation processors and AI accelerators.\n- e619 [party_statement; taekim.substack.com; original source NVIDIA Corporation (CEO Jensen Huang) 2026-09-28; from gnews:0e3eeae7aedc281011283fa0 2026-09-29] NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang, speaking to reporters at The Korea Society's annual gala in New York, said NVIDIA sits at the center of the largest industrial infrastructure build-out in human history and will generate increasing cash, while continuing to make strategic and operational investments and having to secure supply-chain commitments.\n    Quote: “We’re really fortunate that NVIDIA is at the center of the largest industrial infrastructure build out in human history. We’re going to generate a lot of cash in the future. Now of course, we’ll continue to make strategic investments and operational investments and we have to secure supply chain commitments and so on and so forth. … But even then, with all of that, we’re going to generate more an\n- e672 [party_statement; Yahoo Finance; original source Jensen Huang, Nvidia CEO ; from gnews:4bf03094449259d91214e126 2026-09-30] On Nvidia's latest August 2026 earnings call, CEO Jensen Huang said he sees 70% revenue growth for fiscal year 2028, above analyst forecasts for 45% growth; he said the sales gain would be larger—in excess of 100%—if not for memory chip shortages.\n    Quote: On its latest August earnings call, Huang said he sees 70% revenue growth for fiscal year 2028. This was above analyst forecasts for 45% growth. The sales gain would be larger — in excess of 100% — if not for memory chip shortages , Huang said.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e104; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e730 [party_statement; Barchart.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:87a7d1b498492ae728c74167 2026-09-30] Nvidia's unconstrained demand growth—what customers would buy if supply were unlimited—is running above 100%, meaning Nvidia could sell even more if it could build more.\n    Quote: Unconstrained demand growth, meaning what customers would buy if supply were unlimited, is running above 100%. In other words, Nvidia could sell even more if it could build more.\n- e731 [rumour; Jensen Huang; original source Unnamed reports cited by Jensen Huang 2026-09-10; from gnews:87a7d1b498492ae728c74167 2026-09-30] Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang cited reports he had seen that morning showing Grace Blackwell NVLink 72 rack volumes were up 27% from one month to the next.\n    Quote: Huang pointed to reports he had seen that morning showing Grace Blackwell NVLink 72 rack volumes up 27% from one month to the next.\n- e732 [party_statement; Jensen Huang; original source Nvidia (Jensen Huang) 2026-09-10; from gnews:87a7d1b498492ae728c74167 2026-09-30] Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang said the supply chain is the bottleneck, listing packaging, memory, connectors, voltage regulators and wafers as tight spots, and said land, power and building space for data centers are also scarce.\n    Quote: The bottleneck is the supply chain. Huang listed packaging, memory, connectors, voltage regulators, and wafers as tight spots. Land, power, and building space for data centers are also scarce.\n- e766 [fact; Zacks; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:95eab8d6d71dd0f94a8f763e 2026-10-01] NVIDIA's supplier commitments reached $279 billion at the end of fiscal Q2 2027, largely tied to memory procurement.\n    Quote: At the same time, NVIDIA is committing significant resources to secure supply. Supplier commitments reached $279 billion at the end of the second quarter, largely tied to memory procurement.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e113; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e771 [party_statement; Zacks; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:95eab8d6d71dd0f94a8f763e 2026-10-01] NVIDIA management expects fiscal 2028 revenues to grow approximately 70% and described the expectation as supply-constrained, indicating the outlook is limited partly by available supply rather than customer demand.\n    Quote: Management expects fiscal 2028 revenues to grow approximately 70%. It also described this expectation as supply-constrained, suggesting that the company's current revenue outlook is based partly on available supply rather than a lack of customer demand.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e104; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e777 [fact; finance.yahoo.com article (contributor piece); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:890bfe7d206f0531a54beb16 2026-10-01] NVIDIA's supply obligations reached $279.0 billion, mostly memory for Vera Rubin, according to the article.\n    Quote: Supply obligations reached $279.0 billion, mostly memory for Vera Rubin, and guarantee obligations for partners are capped at $108.5 billion.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e113; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e807 [party_statement; finance.yahoo.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:e35845cd1fb0497077e2d464 2026-10-01] NVIDIA plans to produce up to $500 billion of AI infrastructure in the United States together with partners including TSMC, Foxconn, Wistron and Corning, according to the article; no timeline or contractual commitment amounts are given.\n    Quote: NVIDIA plans to produce up to $500 billion of AI infrastructure in the U.S. with partners including TSMC, Foxconn, Wistron, Corning, and others.\n- e873 [third_party_view; Dave Kovaleski, fool.com; original source fool.com (Dave Kovaleski) 2026-10-02; from gnews:edb22ee9082181453e2be651 2026-10-02] In this 2 October 2026 column, Fool.com's Dave Kovaleski forecasts that Taiwan Semiconductor Manufacturing (TSMC), Nvidia's main foundry, will generate some $33 billion of revenue from its largest customer, Nvidia, in calendar 2026. This is the author's forecast of Nvidia's annual payments to TSMC, not a reported actual or a disclosed contractual commitment.\n    Quote: TSMC makes millions of advanced chips every year and will generate some $33 billion from its largest customer, Nvidia, in 2026.\n- e933 [party_statement; Zacks Investment Ideas; original source Micron Technology (management) ; from gnews:23fdf5ee8cbf0b27ad04e66c 2026-10-02] Micron management said the company has 26 multi-year take-or-pay agreements covering more than 35% of its expected revenue through 2030, and that customer financial commitments rose to $32 billion from $22 billion in the prior quarter, with the vast majority in cash deposits.\n    Quote: According to management, Micron has 26 multi-year take-or-pay agreements, covering more than 35% of expected revenue through 2030. Customer financial commitments have soared to $32 billion from $22 billion last quarter, with the vast majority in cash deposits.\n- e934 [party_statement; Micron Technology, reported by Zacks Investment Ideas; original source Micron Technology ; from gnews:23fdf5ee8cbf0b27ad04e66c 2026-10-02] Micron confirmed that the DRAM supply/demand imbalance remains and said it does not have line-of-sight to when supply and demand will return to balance, in its earnings release the night before the Zacks article of 2 October 2026.\n    Quote: Last night, Micron confirmed that the DRAM supply/demand imbalance remains, saying: … \"We do not have line-of-sight to when supply and demand will return to balance.\"\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1027; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of e108 (); both are in force: The article reports that Micron Technology's memory chips are in severe shortage, with demand far outpacing supply, which has driven skyrocketing memory prices, higher margins for Micron and global supply constraints.\n- e953 [fact; finance.yahoo.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:d38374c4abf4bd13d2cd2ccd 2026-10-02] Nvidia disclosed $366 billion in multiyear AI infrastructure commitments, of which $279 billion is tied mostly to memory procurement.\n    Quote: Its own books carry weight too. Nvidia disclosed $366 billion in multiyear AI infrastructure commitments, with $279 billion of that tied mostly to memory procurement.\n- e1027 [party_statement; Sanjay Mehrotra, Micron CEO, on an earnings call (reported by finance.biggo.com); original source Micron Technology (CEO Sanjay Mehrotra) ; from gnews:d5aa9ac26fd22e8535ac3698 2026-10-03] Micron Technology and other memory suppliers have warned that, as production lines shift toward High Bandwidth Memory for AI data centers, supply of general-purpose high-performance memory such as LPDDR5X has become extremely constrained, and they project the supply-demand imbalance will persist through 2027 and 2028, dismissing early normalization; Micron CEO Sanjay Mehrotra said on an earnings call that \"demand will exceed supply in 2027 and 2028\" and that the company cannot predict when supply and demand will return to balance.\n    Quote: Major memory suppliers including Micron Technology have warned that as production lines shift toward High Bandwidth Memory (HBM) for AI data centers, supply of general-purpose high-performance memory such as LPDDR5X has become extremely constrained. These suppliers project the supply-demand imbalance will persist through 2027 and 2028, dismissing prospects for early normalization. … Micron CEO San\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e934; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e1040 [fact; Geoffrey Seiler, The Motley Fool; original source SK Hynix (with Nvidia) ; from gnews:8cba65ded3fbaa69dfc8a83c 2026-10-04] The Motley Fool's Geoffrey Seiler reports that SK Hynix, which he describes as Nvidia's main high-bandwidth memory (HBM) supplier, recently formed a supply and co-development partnership with Nvidia under which SK Hynix will supply Nvidia with an estimated $500 billion worth of memory over the next several years. The $500 billion figure is the article's estimate; no start date, volume or pricing terms are given.\n    Quote: It is also the main HBM supplier to Nvidia, and the two companies recently formed an important supply and co-development partnership. As part of the deal, SK Hynix will supply Nvidia with an estimated $500 billion worth of memory over the next several years.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e393; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e1048 [fact; Yahoo Finance; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:ed14a9d1371ea43f3c5dcd3d 2026-10-04] NVIDIA reported $279 billion in supply commitments and guarantee obligations limited at $108.5 billion for AI cloud and data center partners.\n    Quote: NVIDIA reported $279 billion in supply commitments and guarantee obligations limited at $108.5 billion for AI cloud and data center partners.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e113; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e1062 [fact; finance.yahoo.com; original source NVIDIA Corporation ; from gnews:b0e3eb6b8404ada08d7535e1 2026-10-04] NVIDIA's second-quarter filing disclosed $279 billion of supply and capacity commitments, up from $119 billion at the previous quarter-end. The filing scheduled $92 billion of these commitments to the remainder of fiscal 2027, $87 billion to fiscal 2028 and $88 billion to fiscal 2029, with smaller amounts afterward, and said NVIDIA may cancel, reschedule or adjust some agreements before firm orders, though changes can carry costs.\n    Quote: Its second-quarter filing disclosed $279 billion of supply and capacity commitments, up from $119 billion at the previous quarter-end. … The filing allocated $92 billion of these commitments to the remainder of fiscal 2027, $87 billion to fiscal 2028 and $88 billion to fiscal 2029, with smaller amounts afterward. NVIDIA also said it may cancel, reschedule or adjust some agreements before firm orde\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e113; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e1073 [fact; Yahoo Finance (finance.yahoo.com); original source Nvidia ; from gnews:4f6390171510e65b5e369925 2026-10-04] Nvidia disclosed $279 billion of supply and capacity commitments as of July 26, primarily related to memory procurement.\n    Quote: Nvidia also disclosed $279 billion of supply and capacity commitments as of July 26, primarily related to memory procurement, while its CFO flagged \"extreme pricing conditions in memory\" as a margin headwind.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e113; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n\n[Themes in force, 6]\n- v10 (ai-capex, due 2027-03-31) How many gigawatts of new data-center capacity will be added in the United States during calendar 2026, as determined by full-year 2026 grid-operator and utility interconnection/capacity data and industry data-center capacity reports?: now rung 2 from 10 GW to below 15 GW; judged 2026-10-05; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The reference is Huang’s rough 10–20 GW annual estimate: its 10–<15 portion is rung 2, its 15–<20 portion is rung 3, and 20 GW reaches rung 4; the midpoint, 15 GW, is rung 3. I forecast below that midpoint because the quantified trades shortfall, the stated net power shortfall, and the two-to-three-\n- v11 (semiconductor-cycle, due 2027-03-31) What is the percentage change in average DRAM (including HBM) contract prices in calendar 2026 versus calendar 2025, according to TrendForce/DRAMeXchange full-year 2026 contract-price data?: now rung 5 30 percent or more; judged 2026-10-05; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The reference is the opening no-reference position, rung 3 (10% to below 20%); no consensus, company guidance, or TrendForce/DRAMeXchange full-year figure for this measure is supplied. The forecast is above that reference: Micron says the DRAM imbalance has no visible return-to-balance date and that\n- v3 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will U.S. authorities authorize exports of Nvidia’s current Blackwell-generation AI chips to China and Chinese authorities allow those shipments by January 31, 2027, as verified by official export-control or licensing decisions, Chinese regulatory or customs actions, or Nvidia disclosures?: now rung 3 Still open; judged 2026-10-05; reasons: The nearest observable market reference is NVIDIA’s Q3 FY2027 guidance, which assumes no China Data Center compute revenue; that sits at rung 3, Still open, rather than establishing that approval is formally ruled out by the due date. The evidence implies no verified U.S. authorization for Blackwell\n- v5 (ai-capex, due 2027-03-31) What will the five largest AI hyperscalers' combined data-center capital expenditure be in calendar 2026, according to their full-year earnings and capital-expenditure disclosures?: now rung 3 from 750,000,000,000 USD to below 850,000,000,000 USD; judged 2026-10-05; reasons: The reference is Nvidia's estimate of nearly $800 billion for the big five in calendar 2026, which sits in rung 3. Amazon's approximately $220 billion 2026 capex projection, versus its previous $200 billion level, supports about a $20 billion increase to that reference: roughly $820 billion, still w\n- v7 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will China's Ministry of Industry and Information Technology (MIIT) formally approve purchases of Nvidia's RTX Pro 5500 by Alibaba, ByteDance, and other Chinese firms by January 31, 2027?: now rung 1 Yes; judged 2026-09-30; reasons: The reference is rung 3: no market consensus or formal decision is supplied, and the reported status is still deliberation rather than approval or rejection. The forecast nevertheless favors rung 1: the specific report says officials indicated purchases were likely to be approved and MIIT intended t\n- v9 (ai-capex, due 2028-03-30) How much will the five largest AI hyperscalers spend in total on data centers in calendar 2027, as shown by their capex disclosures and reported 2027 results?: now rung 3 from 1,200,000,000,000 USD to below 1,400,000,000,000 USD; judged 2026-10-05; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The reference is Nvidia's projection of $1.3 trillion for calendar 2027, which sits in rung 3. Barclays' approximately $1.1 trillion figure is for cloud capital spending and is not a like-for-like estimate of the five hyperscalers' data-center capex; the demand evidence, including the new customer-g\n\n[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 5; reasons: The implied amount is well above rung 5's $125bn floor, so it remains strongly bearish on this ladder. The reported $279bn is a current supply/capacity commitment balance, not the FY2027 year-end amount; the larger multi-year HBM estimate is not added in full because its timing, terms, and overlap with reported commitments are unspecified.\n\nAnswer per the JSON schema. cites may name only the evidence ids listed above; cites_views must be empty: this thesis depends on no other thesis; theme_pushes only the theme ids listed above; a theme id goes only in theme_pushes, never in cites or cites_views.",
docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/carried-run/codex-figures-controls.stream:6:{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'cat docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/carried-run/packet-figures-controls.json'","aggregated_output":"{\n \"statements\": [\n  {\n   \"day\": \"2026-10-04\",\n   \"statement\": \"NVIDIA generated $96.22 billion of second-quarter revenue and $63.73 billion of operating income, an operating margin of about 66%, according to the article.\",\n   \"figures\": [\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"revenue\",\n     \"period\": \"second quarter\",\n     \"value\": \"$96.22 billion\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": \"company-reported quarterly revenue\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"operating margin\",\n     \"period\": \"second quarter\",\n     \"value\": \"about 66%\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": \"derived from reported revenue and operating income\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"reports\": 1,\n   \"ref\": \"S1\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"day\": \"2026-09-22\",\n   \"statement\": \"NVIDIA's latest reported quarter generated $89 billion of data-center revenue out of $96.2 billion in total revenue, so roughly 92.5% of sales came from the infrastructure businesses powering large-scale AI, according to GuruFocus's recounting of the results in its 22 September 2026 article.\",\n   \"figures\": [\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"Data-center revenue\",\n     \"period\": \"latest reported quarter\",\n     \"value\": \"$89 billion\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": \"Data-center segment revenue, as reported in the company's quarterly results\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"Total revenue\",\n     \"period\": \"latest reported quarter\",\n     \"value\": \"$96.2 billion\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": \"Company-wide revenue\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"Data-center share of total sales\",\n     \"period\": \"latest reported quarter\",\n     \"value\": \"roughly 92.5%\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": \"Data-center revenue divided by total revenue, as calculated by GuruFocus\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"reports\": 16,\n   \"ref\": \"S2\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"day\": \"2026-09-24\",\n   \"statement\": \"NVIDIA's gross margin is guided to settle in the 72% to 73% range in fiscal 2028, lowered because memory prices have risen more than management expected, partly helped by price increases the company has already executed.\",\n   \"figures\": [\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"Gross margin\",\n     \"period\": \"Fiscal 2028\",\n     \"value\": \"72% to 73%\",\n     \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n     \"basis\": \"Company gross margin guidance, lowered on higher-than-expected memory costs and helped by price increases already executed\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"reports\": 2,\n   \"ref\": \"S3\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"day\": \"2026-09-28\",\n   \"statement\": \"NVIDIA said on Monday 28 September 2026 that it authorized an additional $150 billion for its share repurchase program, taking the total authorization to $235 billion, which it described as the largest increase in a share repurchase authorization in history; the company said it expects to complete the total remaining buyback program through fiscal 2028.\",\n   \"figures\": [\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"additional share repurchase authorization\",\n     \"period\": null,\n     \"value\": \"$150 billion\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": \"Additional authorization; takes total buyback authorization to $235 billion, expected to be completed through fiscal 2028.\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"total share repurchase authorization\",\n     \"period\": null,\n     \"value\": \"$235 billion\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": \"Total program after the additional $150 billion authorization.\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"reports\": 32,\n   \"ref\": \"S4\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"day\": \"2026-09-29\",\n   \"statement\": \"NVIDIA has a $150 billion share buyback plan, described by Yahoo Finance as the largest ever, which the article cites alongside its cash generation as justification for the repurchase programme.\",\n   \"figures\": [\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"share buyback plan\",\n     \"period\": null,\n     \"value\": \"$150 billion\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": \"announced repurchase authorisation; described as the largest ever\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"reports\": 2,\n   \"ref\": \"S5\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"day\": \"2026-09-24\",\n   \"statement\": \"NVIDIA reported fiscal 2027 first-quarter revenue growth of 85% year over year, with Data Center revenues up 92% year over year, as retold by Zacks Equity Research in its Analyst Blog highlights.\",\n   \"figures\": [\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"revenue growth\",\n     \"period\": \"fiscal 2027 first quarter\",\n     \"value\": \"85%\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": \"year over year, company total\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"Data Center revenues growth\",\n     \"period\": \"fiscal 2027 first quarter\",\n     \"value\": \"92%\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": \"year over year, Data Center segment\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"reports\": 3,\n   \"ref\": \"S6\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"day\": \"2026-09-25\",\n   \"statement\": \"NVIDIA reset its gross margin expectations after memory prices rose more than it expected, guiding gross margin to 74% for fiscal Q3 2027, down from 75% in fiscal Q2 2027, and expecting gross margin to bottom at 71% to 72% in fiscal Q4 2027.\",\n   \"figures\": [\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"gross margin\",\n     \"period\": \"fiscal Q3 2027\",\n     \"value\": \"74%\",\n     \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n     \"basis\": \"company guided, down from fiscal Q2 2027\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"gross margin\",\n     \"period\": \"fiscal Q2 2027\",\n     \"value\": \"75%\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": \"company\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"gross margin\",\n     \"period\": \"fiscal Q4 2027\",\n     \"value\": \"71% to 72%\",\n     \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n     \"basis\": \"expected bottom\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"reports\": 6,\n   \"ref\": \"S7\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"day\": \"2026-09-27\",\n   \"statement\": \"NVIDIA says its $108 billion revenue forecast for the current quarter assumes no China data-center chip revenue; any China data-center chip sales would come on top of that outlook.\",\n   \"figures\": [\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"Revenue forecast for the current quarter\",\n     \"period\": \"Current quarter\",\n     \"value\": \"$108 billion\",\n     \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n     \"basis\": \"Company forecast; assumes no China data-center chip revenue\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"reports\": 1,\n   \"ref\": \"S8\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"day\": \"2026-09-23\",\n   \"statement\": \"NVIDIA's second-quarter net income rose 126% year-on-year to $59.7bn, according to the Yahoo Finance UK article, which notes earnings grew roughly 4.5 times faster than the share price over the same comparison.\",\n   \"figures\": [\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"Net income\",\n     \"period\": \"Second quarter (year-on-year)\",\n     \"value\": \"$59.7bn\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": null\n    },\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"Net income growth, year-on-year\",\n     \"period\": \"Second quarter\",\n     \"value\": \"126%\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": null\n    }\n   ],\n   \"reports\": 3,\n   \"ref\": \"S9\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"day\": \"2026-10-01\",\n   \"statement\": \"NVIDIA reported Q2 FY2027 EPS of $2.22, topping the $2.09 consensus and marking its fifth consecutive beat, according to the article.\",\n   \"figures\": [\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"Earnings per share\",\n     \"period\": \"Q2 FY2027\",\n     \"value\": \"$2.22\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": \"as reported\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"Consensus earnings per share\",\n     \"period\": \"Q2 FY2027\",\n     \"value\": \"$2.09\",\n     \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n     \"basis\": \"consensus estimate\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"reports\": 1,\n   \"ref\": \"S10\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"day\": \"2026-10-04\",\n   \"statement\": \"Nvidia reported free cash flow of $27.02 billion in the fiscal year ended January 2024, $60.85 billion in fiscal 2025 and $96.68 billion in fiscal 2026.\",\n   \"figures\": [\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"free cash flow\",\n     \"period\": \"fiscal year ended January 2024\",\n     \"value\": \"$27.02 billion\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": null\n    },\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"free cash flow\",\n     \"period\": \"fiscal year ended January 2025\",\n     \"value\": \"$60.85 billion\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": null\n    },\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"free cash flow\",\n     \"period\": \"fiscal year ended January 2026\",\n     \"value\": \"$96.68 billion\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": null\n    }\n   ],\n   \"reports\": 1,\n   \"ref\": \"S11\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"day\": \"2026-09-29\",\n   \"statement\": \"NVIDIA's free cash flow fell by more than half in the quarter before the 29 September 2026 article, mostly because accounts receivable rose by more than $22 billion; the article states the sales were booked but the cash had not yet been collected.\",\n   \"figures\": [\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"free cash flow\",\n     \"period\": \"last quarter\",\n     \"value\": \"fell by more than half\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": \"company change in free cash flow versus prior year quarter\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"reports\": 1,\n   \"ref\": \"S12\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"day\": \"2026-09-25\",\n   \"statement\": \"Fool.com reports that Nvidia's sales to two direct customers represented 22% and 14% of total revenue, respectively, in fiscal 2026, and that two customers make up more than a third of sales — a customer concentration it flags as a risk.\",\n   \"figures\": [\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"Sales to a direct customer as % of total revenue\",\n     \"period\": \"fiscal 2026 (ended January 2026)\",\n     \"value\": \"22%\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": \"direct customer\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"Sales to a direct customer as % of total revenue\",\n     \"period\": \"fiscal 2026 (ended January 2026)\",\n     \"value\": \"14%\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": \"direct customer\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"reports\": 3,\n   \"ref\": \"S13\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"day\": \"2026-09-23\",\n   \"statement\": \"Nvidia, in its quarterly results reported in the month before this 23 September 2026 Barchart article, posted what the article calls a market-beating fiscal Q2 and issued guidance for about 70% revenue growth in fiscal 2028.\",\n   \"figures\": [\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"revenue growth\",\n     \"period\": \"fiscal 2028\",\n     \"value\": \"about 70%\",\n     \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n     \"basis\": \"Nvidia's own guidance as reported by Barchart; growth measure not further specified in the item\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"reports\": 17,\n   \"ref\": \"S14\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"day\": \"2026-10-02\",\n   \"statement\": \"NVIDIA management's gross margin outlook remains at 74%, plus or minus 50 basis points, according to Insider Monkey; the item gives no period or statement date for the outlook, and the author adds that holding that margin requires absorbing rising high-bandwidth memory costs and higher R&D spending.\",\n   \"figures\": [\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"gross margin outlook\",\n     \"period\": null,\n     \"value\": \"74%, plus or minus 50 basis points\",\n     \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n     \"basis\": \"management outlook as reported by the article; item does not state the period covered\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"reports\": 1,\n   \"ref\": \"S15\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"day\": \"2026-10-01\",\n   \"statement\": \"NVIDIA generated $24.08 billion in operating cash flow and $21.34 billion in free cash flow during fiscal Q2 2027.\",\n   \"figures\": [\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"Operating cash flow\",\n     \"period\": \"Q2 fiscal 2027\",\n     \"value\": \"$24.08 billion\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": null\n    },\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"Free cash flow\",\n     \"period\": \"Q2 fiscal 2027\",\n     \"value\": \"$21.34 billion\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": null\n    }\n   ],\n   \"reports\": 1,\n   \"ref\": \"S16\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"day\": \"2026-09-22\",\n   \"statement\": \"NVIDIA management guided fiscal third-quarter revenue to approximately $108 billion, which the article says implies 89% growth.\",\n   \"figures\": [\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"Revenue guidance\",\n     \"period\": \"Fiscal third quarter\",\n     \"value\": \"approximately $108 billion\",\n     \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n     \"basis\": \"Company guidance; implied 89% growth is the article's calculation\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"reports\": 30,\n   \"ref\": \"S17\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"day\": \"2026-09-23\",\n   \"statement\": \"Nvidia told shareholders in May 2026 that it would raise its quarterly dividend to $0.25 per share from $0.01 per share, an increase the Morningstar author calculates at 2,400%. The author estimates roughly $25 billion per year would be returned to shareholders through the dividend, which would make Nvidia one of the largest dividend payers in the US market; that annual figure is the author's approximation, not a company-disclosed total.\",\n   \"figures\": [\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"quarterly dividend per share\",\n     \"period\": \"from the May 2026 declaration\",\n     \"value\": \"$0.25 per share\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": \"quarterly payout, raised from $0.01 per share\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"prior quarterly dividend per share\",\n     \"period\": \"prior to the May 2026 declaration\",\n     \"value\": \"$0.01 per share\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": null\n    },\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"increase in quarterly dividend\",\n     \"period\": \"May 2026 declaration\",\n     \"value\": \"2,400%\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": \"author's calculation\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"annual dividend paid to shareholders\",\n     \"period\": \"annual, based on the May 2026 rate\",\n     \"value\": \"$25 billion per year\",\n     \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n     \"basis\": \"author's approximation (\\\"Something like\\\"); total annual dividend return to shareholders\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"reports\": 2,\n   \"ref\": \"S18\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"day\": \"2026-07-07\",\n   \"statement\": \"Broadcom AI semiconductor revenue was $10.8 billion in Q2, up 143% year over year.\",\n   \"figures\": [],\n   \"reports\": 1,\n   \"ref\": \"S19\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"day\": \"2026-09-29\",\n   \"statement\": \"Nvidia reported free cash flow of $69.9 billion for the first half of fiscal 2027, 77% higher than a year before, or around $35 billion a quarter; CEO Jensen Huang linked the buyback increase to the company's \\\"cash generation\\\" in the announcement.\",\n   \"figures\": [\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"free cash flow\",\n     \"period\": \"fiscal 2027 first half\",\n     \"value\": \"$69.9 billion\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": \"company reported; around $35 billion a quarter\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"free cash flow growth\",\n     \"period\": \"fiscal 2027 first half vs a year before\",\n     \"value\": \"77%\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": \"higher than a year before\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"reports\": 2,\n   \"ref\": \"S20\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"day\": \"2026-09-28\",\n   \"statement\": \"Former Wall Street analyst David Bennett, writing on X.com and quoted by the article, stated that Nvidia generated $74.4 billion of operating cash flow in the first half of fiscal year 2027 alone while still returning $46.1 billion to shareholders, which he said lets the company fund AI expansion and buy back stock at the same time.\",\n   \"figures\": [\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"operating cash flow\",\n     \"period\": \"first half of fiscal year 2027 (through July 2026)\",\n     \"value\": \"$74.4 billion\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": \"operating cash flow, company level\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"reports\": 2,\n   \"ref\": \"S21\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"day\": \"2026-09-22\",\n   \"statement\": \"NVIDIA's revenue more than doubled year over year in fiscal Q2 2027.\",\n   \"figures\": [\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"Revenue, year-over-year change\",\n     \"period\": \"Fiscal Q2 2027\",\n     \"value\": \"more than doubled\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": \"Company-level, year over year\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"reports\": 38,\n   \"ref\": \"S22\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"day\": \"2026-09-25\",\n   \"statement\": \"NVIDIA's board approved raising the quarterly dividend from $0.01 to $0.25 per share in May 2026, a 2,400% increase that lifted the quarterly payout from about $243 million (fiscal 2027 first quarter, when the penny dividend was still in force) to approximately $6.0 billion. The first $0.25 payment went out on 26 June 2026 to holders of record as of 4 June, and a second $0.25 distribution went ex-dividend on 10 September 2026 with payment scheduled for 1 October 2026; the new annual rate is $1 per share.\",\n   \"figures\": [\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"quarterly dividend per share\",\n     \"period\": \"quarterly, from May 2026\",\n     \"value\": \"$0.25 per share\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": \"raised from $0.01 per share per quarter; board-approved in May\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"quarterly dividend payout, total\",\n     \"period\": \"per quarter after the increase\",\n     \"value\": \"approximately $6.0 billion\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": \"approximate, based on company disclosures and share counts at the time of payment\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"quarterly dividend cash outlay\",\n     \"period\": \"fiscal 2027 first quarter\",\n     \"value\": \"about $243 million\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": \"at the prior $0.01 per share quarterly rate\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"reports\": 1,\n   \"ref\": \"S23\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"day\": \"2026-10-05\",\n   \"statement\": \"NVIDIA spent $20 billion on share repurchases in its most recent quarter and $40 billion in the first half of fiscal 2027, split evenly at $20 billion in each of the first and second quarters, according to the article.\",\n   \"figures\": [\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"share repurchases (buybacks)\",\n     \"period\": \"most recent quarter (Q2 fiscal 2027)\",\n     \"value\": \"$20 billion\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": \"company-wide buyback spend\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"share repurchases (buybacks)\",\n     \"period\": \"first half of fiscal 2027\",\n     \"value\": \"$40 billion\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": \"$20 billion in each of the first and second quarters\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"reports\": 1,\n   \"ref\": \"S24\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"day\": \"2026-09-29\",\n   \"statement\": \"Nvidia's diluted share count for its fiscal 2027 second quarter, which ended July 26, 2026, was only about 1% smaller than a year before despite almost $40 billion of buybacks in the fiscal year's first half; earnings per share more than doubled.\",\n   \"figures\": [\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"earnings per share growth\",\n     \"period\": \"fiscal 2027 second quarter\",\n     \"value\": \"more than doubled\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": null\n    }\n   ],\n   \"reports\": 1,\n   \"ref\": \"S25\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"day\": \"2026-09-28\",\n   \"statement\": \"NVIDIA said its revenue opportunity per gigawatt of AI infrastructure, based on its reference designs, has risen from roughly $18 billion during the Hopper generation to $25 billion with Blackwell and $40 billion with Rubin.\",\n   \"figures\": [\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"Revenue opportunity per gigawatt of AI infrastructure (Hopper generation)\",\n     \"period\": null,\n     \"value\": \"roughly $18 billion\",\n     \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n     \"basis\": \"NVIDIA's estimate based on its reference designs; per gigawatt of AI infrastructure\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"Revenue opportunity per gigawatt of AI infrastructure (Blackwell generation)\",\n     \"period\": null,\n     \"value\": \"$25 billion\",\n     \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n     \"basis\": \"NVIDIA's estimate based on its reference designs; per gigawatt of AI infrastructure\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"Revenue opportunity per gigawatt of AI infrastructure (Rubin generation)\",\n     \"period\": null,\n     \"value\": \"$40 billion\",\n     \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n     \"basis\": \"NVIDIA's estimate based on its reference designs; per gigawatt of AI infrastructure\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"reports\": 5,\n   \"ref\": \"S26\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"day\": \"2026-10-04\",\n   \"statement\": \"NVIDIA says Grace Blackwell is already one generation behind Vera Rubin and that Vera Rubin delivers 35 times lower token costs than Grace Blackwell Ultra; NVIDIA estimates about $25 billion of revenue per gigawatt for Grace Blackwell and roughly $40 billion per gigawatt for Vera Rubin.\",\n   \"figures\": [],\n   \"reports\": 1,\n   \"ref\": \"S27\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"day\": \"2026-09-29\",\n   \"statement\": \"In NVIDIA's fiscal Q2 2027, sales to the largest cloud companies were $49 billion, up 13% from the prior quarter, while sales to all other data center customers were $40 billion, up 25% from the prior quarter.\",\n   \"figures\": [\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"sales to the largest cloud companies\",\n     \"period\": \"fiscal Q2 2027\",\n     \"value\": \"$49 billion\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": \"largest cloud company customers only\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"sales to the largest cloud companies, change from prior quarter\",\n     \"period\": \"fiscal Q2 2027\",\n     \"value\": \"13%\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": \"quarter over quarter\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"sales to all other data center customers\",\n     \"period\": \"fiscal Q2 2027\",\n     \"value\": \"$40 billion\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": \"data center customers other than the largest cloud companies\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"sales to all other data center customers, change from prior quarter\",\n     \"period\": \"fiscal Q2 2027\",\n     \"value\": \"25%\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": \"quarter over quarter\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"reports\": 3,\n   \"ref\": \"S28\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"day\": \"2026-09-28\",\n   \"statement\": \"Trefis reports that NVIDIA's operating margin — the share of revenue left after running costs — is 65%, the highest in ten years, versus a three-year average of 59% (the article does not state the exact measurement period).\",\n   \"figures\": [\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"operating margin\",\n     \"period\": null,\n     \"value\": \"65%\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": \"share of revenue left after running costs; highest in ten years\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"operating margin (three-year average)\",\n     \"period\": \"three-year average\",\n     \"value\": \"59%\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": \"three-year average\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"reports\": 1,\n   \"ref\": \"S29\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"day\": \"2026-09-27\",\n   \"statement\": \"NVIDIA has already set price increases that take effect in fiscal Q1 2028, and management expects the higher prices to lift gross margin to 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028.\",\n   \"figures\": [\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"Gross margin\",\n     \"period\": \"fiscal 2028\",\n     \"value\": \"72% to 73%\",\n     \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n     \"basis\": \"management expectation, helped by price increases taking effect in fiscal Q1 2028\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"reports\": 1,\n   \"ref\": \"S30\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"day\": \"2026-09-27\",\n   \"statement\": \"The low end of NVIDIA management's gross margin guidance for fiscal Q3 2027 is 73.5%, per the article.\",\n   \"figures\": [\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"Gross margin guidance, low end\",\n     \"period\": \"fiscal Q3 2027\",\n     \"value\": \"73.5%\",\n     \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n     \"basis\": \"low end of management's gross margin guide for the quarter\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"reports\": 1,\n   \"ref\": \"S31\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"day\": \"2026-09-22\",\n   \"statement\": \"fool.com reports that NVIDIA generated $192.9 billion in net income over the trailing 12 months, compared with $6.4 billion for AMD, citing this in a valuation comparison of the two AI chip makers.\",\n   \"figures\": [\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"net income\",\n     \"period\": \"trailing 12 months\",\n     \"value\": \"$192.9 billion\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": null\n    }\n   ],\n   \"reports\": 2,\n   \"ref\": \"S32\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"day\": \"2026-09-25\",\n   \"statement\": \"Fool.com reports that Nvidia's fiscal 2026, which ended in January 2026, produced revenue of nearly $215.9 billion, up roughly 65.5% year over year, net income of approximately $120.1 billion, and a net margin of approximately 55.6%, consistent with the 55.8% net margin of the previous fiscal year.\",\n   \"figures\": [\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"Revenue\",\n     \"period\": \"fiscal 2026 (ended January 2026)\",\n     \"value\": \"$215.9 billion\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": \"company total; up roughly 65.5% year over year\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"Net income\",\n     \"period\": \"fiscal 2026 (ended January 2026)\",\n     \"value\": \"$120.1 billion\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": \"company total\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"Net margin\",\n     \"period\": \"fiscal 2026 (ended January 2026)\",\n     \"value\": \"55.6%\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": \"company net margin; prior fiscal year 55.8%\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"reports\": 6,\n   \"ref\": \"S33\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"day\": \"2026-10-02\",\n   \"statement\": \"In its latest reported quarter, Nvidia spent $19.7 billion on share buybacks and $6 billion on dividends, versus Apple's $25.9 billion of buybacks and $4 billion of dividends in Apple's fiscal 2026 third quarter; a year earlier, in the second quarter of Nvidia's fiscal 2026, Nvidia's buybacks and dividends combined totaled $10 billion.\",\n   \"figures\": [\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"share buybacks\",\n     \"period\": \"latest reported quarter\",\n     \"value\": \"$19.7 billion\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": \"Nvidia share repurchases\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"dividends paid\",\n     \"period\": \"latest reported quarter\",\n     \"value\": \"$6 billion\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": \"Nvidia dividends\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"buybacks and dividends combined\",\n     \"period\": \"second quarter of fiscal 2026 (year-ago quarter)\",\n     \"value\": \"$10 billion\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": \"Nvidia share repurchases plus dividends, combined\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"reports\": 1,\n   \"ref\": \"S34\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"day\": \"2026-09-27\",\n   \"statement\": \"NVIDIA management expects Vera Rubin to account for about 20% of data center revenue in fiscal Q3 2027.\",\n   \"figures\": [\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"Vera Rubin share of data center revenue\",\n     \"period\": \"fiscal Q3 2027\",\n     \"value\": \"about 20%\",\n     \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n     \"basis\": \"management expectation; share of data center revenue\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"reports\": 5,\n   \"ref\": \"S35\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"day\": \"2026-07-12\",\n   \"statement\": \"Broadcom plans to ramp production of custom AI chips for Meta Platforms, Anthropic, and OpenAI from late 2026 into 2027.\",\n   \"figures\": [],\n   \"reports\": 1,\n   \"ref\": \"S36\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"day\": \"2026-10-01\",\n   \"statement\": \"NVIDIA returned approximately $25.78 billion through share repurchases and dividends in fiscal Q2 2027, and in late September 2026 increased its share repurchase authorization by $150 billion, leaving a total of $235 billion available for future buybacks.\",\n   \"figures\": [\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"Share repurchases and dividends\",\n     \"period\": \"Q2 fiscal 2027\",\n     \"value\": \"$25.78 billion\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": \"Approximately\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"Increase in share repurchase authorization\",\n     \"period\": \"Late September 2026\",\n     \"value\": \"$150 billion\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": null\n    },\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"Total share repurchase authorization available\",\n     \"period\": \"After late September 2026 increase\",\n     \"value\": \"$235 billion\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": \"Available for future buybacks\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"reports\": 4,\n   \"ref\": \"S37\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"day\": \"2026-09-26\",\n   \"statement\": \"Nvidia estimates that its revenue growth for fiscal 2027 will be around 70%, according to Fool.com contributor Keithen Drury.\",\n   \"figures\": [\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"revenue growth\",\n     \"period\": \"FY 2027\",\n     \"value\": \"around 70%\",\n     \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n     \"basis\": \"Nvidia estimate as cited for the full fiscal year\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"reports\": 9,\n   \"ref\": \"S38\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"day\": \"2026-09-29\",\n   \"statement\": \"Nvidia management said demand for Vera Rubin currently implies its CPU revenue will more than double in fiscal 2028, which would position the company as one of the world's leading server CPU suppliers, according to the article.\",\n   \"figures\": [\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"Vera Rubin CPU revenue growth\",\n     \"period\": \"fiscal 2028\",\n     \"value\": \"more than double\",\n     \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n     \"basis\": \"management statement; demand-implied\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"reports\": 1,\n   \"ref\": \"S39\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"day\": \"2026-09-23\",\n   \"statement\": \"Nvidia announced an $80 billion share buyback program at the time of its May 2026 dividend declaration, per the Morningstar article.\",\n   \"figures\": [\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"share buyback program\",\n     \"period\": \"announced at the May 2026 dividend declaration\",\n     \"value\": \"$80 billion\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": \"buyback authorization announced alongside the dividend increase\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"reports\": 4,\n   \"ref\": \"S40\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"day\": \"2026-09-24\",\n   \"statement\": \"NVIDIA management said Vera Rubin, its newest platform, is expected to be the fastest product ramp in the company's history, and put NVIDIA's revenue opportunity at about $40 billion per gigawatt of capacity with Vera Rubin versus about $25 billion per gigawatt with Blackwell.\",\n   \"figures\": [\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"Revenue opportunity per gigawatt of capacity with Vera Rubin\",\n     \"period\": null,\n     \"value\": \"$40 billion per gigawatt\",\n     \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n     \"basis\": \"Management's estimate of NVIDIA revenue opportunity per gigawatt of capacity, for Vera Rubin\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"Revenue opportunity per gigawatt of capacity with Blackwell\",\n     \"period\": null,\n     \"value\": \"$25 billion\",\n     \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n     \"basis\": \"Management's estimate of NVIDIA revenue opportunity per gigawatt of capacity, for Blackwell (comparison figure)\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"reports\": 2,\n   \"ref\": \"S41\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"day\": \"2026-10-05\",\n   \"statement\": \"NVIDIA's data center revenue was $89 billion in fiscal Q2 2027, up from $39 billion in the quarter of the lost H20 sales.\",\n   \"figures\": [\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"data center revenue\",\n     \"period\": \"fiscal Q2 2027\",\n     \"value\": \"$89 billion\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": \"data center segment\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"data center revenue\",\n     \"period\": \"quarter of the lost H20 sales (fiscal Q1 2026)\",\n     \"value\": \"$39 billion\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": \"data center segment\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"reports\": 1,\n   \"ref\": \"S42\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"day\": \"2026-09-28\",\n   \"statement\": \"Nvidia repurchased approximately $34 billion of its shares in fiscal year 2025, over $40.4 billion in fiscal year 2026, and $39 billion in the first half of fiscal year 2027 (the six months through July 2026), per the article.\",\n   \"figures\": [\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"shares repurchased\",\n     \"period\": \"fiscal year 2025\",\n     \"value\": \"approximately $34 billion\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": \"share repurchases\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"shares repurchased\",\n     \"period\": \"fiscal year 2026\",\n     \"value\": \"over $40.4 billion\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": \"share repurchases\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"shares repurchased\",\n     \"period\": \"first half of fiscal year 2027 (through July 2026)\",\n     \"value\": \"$39 billion\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": \"share repurchases\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"reports\": 4,\n   \"ref\": \"S43\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"day\": \"2026-07-06\",\n   \"statement\": \"Bloomberg Intelligence forecast custom ASICs to grow at a 27% CAGR through 2033, compared with 16% for AI accelerators.\",\n   \"figures\": [],\n   \"reports\": 1,\n   \"ref\": \"S44\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"day\": \"2026-10-02\",\n   \"statement\": \"On its August 2026 earnings call, Nvidia said it returned 60% of first-half fiscal 2027 free cash flow to shareholders, ahead of its 50% target, and that going forward it intends to increase the return of free cash flow net of strategic uses.\",\n   \"figures\": [\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"free cash flow returned to shareholders\",\n     \"period\": \"first half fiscal 2027\",\n     \"value\": \"60%\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": \"share of free cash flow returned via buybacks and dividends, company statement\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"free cash flow return target\",\n     \"period\": \"first half fiscal 2027\",\n     \"value\": \"50%\",\n     \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n     \"basis\": \"company target for share of free cash flow returned to shareholders\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"reports\": 1,\n   \"ref\": \"S45\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"day\": \"2026-09-29\",\n   \"statement\": \"NVIDIA's sales grew 83% in the twelve months to fiscal Q2 2027, per Trefis.\",\n   \"figures\": [\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"sales growth\",\n     \"period\": \"twelve months to fiscal Q2 2027\",\n     \"value\": \"83%\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": \"trailing twelve months\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"reports\": 2,\n   \"ref\": \"S46\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"day\": \"2026-10-01\",\n   \"statement\": \"Nvidia's revenue and profit climbed in the triple digits in its latest three-month period, to $96 billion in revenue and $59 billion in profit, according to the article, continuing the double- and triple-digit quarterly growth it says Nvidia delivered in recent years.\",\n   \"figures\": [\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"revenue\",\n     \"period\": \"latest three-month period\",\n     \"value\": \"$96 billion\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": null\n    },\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"profit\",\n     \"period\": \"latest three-month period\",\n     \"value\": \"$59 billion\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": null\n    }\n   ],\n   \"reports\": 2,\n   \"ref\": \"S47\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"day\": \"2026-07-06\",\n   \"statement\": \"Amazon disclosed that its Trainium business has reached multi-billion-dollar scale.\",\n   \"figures\": [],\n   \"reports\": 1,\n   \"ref\": \"S48\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"day\": \"2026-10-01\",\n   \"statement\": \"In fiscal Q2 2027, NVIDIA's hyperscale revenues increased 117% year over year to $48.71 billion, while ACIE revenues, which include AI clouds, industrial and enterprise customers, jumped 138% to $40.31 billion.\",\n   \"figures\": [\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"Hyperscale revenues\",\n     \"period\": \"Q2 fiscal 2027\",\n     \"value\": \"$48.71 billion\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": \"Up 117% year over year\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"ACIE revenues\",\n     \"period\": \"Q2 fiscal 2027\",\n     \"value\": \"$40.31 billion\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": \"Includes AI clouds, industrial and enterprise customers; up 138% year over year\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"reports\": 1,\n   \"ref\": \"S49\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"day\": \"2026-09-25\",\n   \"statement\": \"Nvidia's revenue rose 96% year over year to $178 billion and net income surged 161% to $118 billion in the first half of its fiscal 2027, the period ended July 26, 2026, according to the article.\",\n   \"figures\": [\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"Revenue\",\n     \"period\": \"First half of fiscal 2027 (ended July 26, 2026)\",\n     \"value\": \"$178 billion\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": \"Company-wide revenue, up 96% year over year\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"Net income\",\n     \"period\": \"First half of fiscal 2027 (ended July 26, 2026)\",\n     \"value\": \"$118 billion\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": \"Company-wide net income, up 161% year over year\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"reports\": 1,\n   \"ref\": \"S50\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"day\": \"2026-09-26\",\n   \"statement\": \"The article states that Nvidia's data center business is still growing 117% year over year, without specifying which quarter the growth rate refers to.\",\n   \"figures\": [\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"data center revenue growth, year over year\",\n     \"period\": null,\n     \"value\": \"117%\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": \"company's data center business; quarter not specified\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"reports\": 3,\n   \"ref\": \"S51\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"day\": \"2026-09-27\",\n   \"statement\": \"Nvidia's China H200 sales remain below 1% of data-center revenue, and CEO Jensen Huang has said the company's share of China's advanced AI chip market effectively fell from around 95% to zero as restrictions tightened.\",\n   \"figures\": [\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"China H200 sales as share of data-center revenue\",\n     \"period\": null,\n     \"value\": \"below 1%\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": \"Nvidia China H200 sales, share of data-center revenue\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"reports\": 1,\n   \"ref\": \"S52\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"day\": \"2026-09-30\",\n   \"statement\": \"Nvidia told investors in its prior-quarter report that it expects 70% revenue growth in its next fiscal year, citing massive AI demand, according to The Motley Fool.\",\n   \"figures\": [\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"revenue growth\",\n     \"period\": \"next fiscal year (FY2028, ending late January 2028)\",\n     \"value\": \"70%\",\n     \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n     \"basis\": \"Company revenue growth year over year, attributed by Nvidia to massive AI demand\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"reports\": 1,\n   \"ref\": \"S53\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"day\": \"2026-09-29\",\n   \"statement\": \"Nvidia's Grace CPUs, launched in 2021, generated over $5 billion in revenue over the last year, the article reports.\",\n   \"figures\": [\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"Grace CPU revenue\",\n     \"period\": \"last year\",\n     \"value\": \"over $5 billion\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": \"Grace CPUs; trailing 12 months\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"reports\": 2,\n   \"ref\": \"S54\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"day\": \"2026-09-29\",\n   \"statement\": \"Nvidia expects $20 billion in CPU sales this year, according to the article.\",\n   \"figures\": [\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"CPU sales\",\n     \"period\": \"this year (calendar 2026 as described in the article)\",\n     \"value\": \"$20 billion\",\n     \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n     \"basis\": \"company expectation for stand-alone CPU sales\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"reports\": 2,\n   \"ref\": \"S55\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"day\": \"2026-09-22\",\n   \"statement\": \"NVIDIA's gross margin came in at 75% in the last quarter, as reported by qz.com.\",\n   \"figures\": [\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"gross margin\",\n     \"period\": \"last quarter\",\n     \"value\": \"75%\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": \"company gross margin, as reported\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"reports\": 11,\n   \"ref\": \"S56\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"day\": \"2026-09-30\",\n   \"statement\": \"NVIDIA's gross margin was 75% in fiscal second-quarter 2027 and is expected to be in the 72% to 73% range for fiscal 2028, with memory costs having risen more than management expected, as reported by Fool.com.\",\n   \"figures\": [\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"gross margin\",\n     \"period\": \"fiscal Q2 2027\",\n     \"value\": \"75%\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": \"company gross margin\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"gross margin\",\n     \"period\": \"fiscal 2028\",\n     \"value\": \"72% to 73%\",\n     \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n     \"basis\": \"expected range; memory costs have risen more than management expected\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"reports\": 1,\n   \"ref\": \"S57\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"day\": \"2026-07-06\",\n   \"statement\": \"Broadcom guided Q3 AI semiconductor revenue to $16.0 billion, up more than 200% year over year.\",\n   \"figures\": [],\n   \"reports\": 1,\n   \"ref\": \"S58\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"day\": \"2026-09-24\",\n   \"statement\": \"NVIDIA raised its dividend 25-fold in June and set a policy to return at least 50% of free cash flow to shareholders, according to its public June disclosures.\",\n   \"figures\": [\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"dividend increase\",\n     \"period\": \"June 2026\",\n     \"value\": \"25-fold\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": null\n    },\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"free cash flow returned to shareholders\",\n     \"period\": \"Policy set in June 2026; forward-looking\",\n     \"value\": \"at least 50% of free cash flow\",\n     \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n     \"basis\": \"Company capital-return policy per its public June disclosures\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"reports\": 2,\n   \"ref\": \"S59\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"day\": \"2026-09-22\",\n   \"statement\": \"NVIDIA's days of sales outstanding, the average time to collect cash after a sale, increased to 60 days; management said this reflects extended payment terms for large purchases by certain investment-grade customers, shipped over multiple quarters.\",\n   \"figures\": [\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"Days of sales outstanding\",\n     \"period\": null,\n     \"value\": \"60 days\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": \"Average time to collect cash after a sale; management attributes the increase to extended payment terms for large purchases by certain investment-grade customers shipped over multiple quarters\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"reports\": 4,\n   \"ref\": \"S60\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"day\": \"2026-09-30\",\n   \"statement\": \"NVIDIA's operating margin stands at 65%, a rate the article says the company has roughly maintained for the last two years.\",\n   \"figures\": [\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"Operating margin\",\n     \"period\": \"Last two years (roughly maintained)\",\n     \"value\": \"65%\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": \"Company operating margin; the article says the rate has been roughly maintained for the last two years\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"reports\": 1,\n   \"ref\": \"S61\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"day\": \"2026-09-25\",\n   \"statement\": \"Zacks states that NVIDIA's net profit margin is 63.7%, compared with Micron's 55.9%, and that NVIDIA's return on equity of 96% exceeds Micron's 72.5%.\",\n   \"figures\": [\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"Net profit margin\",\n     \"period\": null,\n     \"value\": \"63.7%\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": \"NVIDIA, compared with Micron's 55.9%\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"reports\": 1,\n   \"ref\": \"S62\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"day\": \"2026-09-30\",\n   \"statement\": \"On Nvidia's Q2 earnings call, executives said the company had begun shipping a small volume of H200 accelerators to China but that these shipments amounted to less than 1 percent of its data-center revenues.\",\n   \"figures\": [\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"H200 accelerator shipments to China as a share of datacenter revenues\",\n     \"period\": \"Q2\",\n     \"value\": \"less than 1 percent\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": \"shipments of a small volume of H200 accelerators to the region\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"reports\": 2,\n   \"ref\": \"S63\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"day\": \"2026-10-02\",\n   \"statement\": \"Nvidia's non-GAAP EPS was $2.22 in fiscal Q2 2027, up from $1.59 in fiscal Q4 2026, a gain of about 40% in two quarters; its adjusted EPS for the latest four reported quarters was around $6.95 versus $4.64 for fiscal 2026, up about 50%. Nvidia now treats stock-based compensation as a cost in adjusted earnings and restated earlier quarters to match, according to the article.\",\n   \"figures\": [\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"Non-GAAP EPS\",\n     \"period\": \"Fiscal Q2 2027\",\n     \"value\": \"$2.22\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": \"Adjusted; stock-based compensation treated as a cost\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"Non-GAAP EPS\",\n     \"period\": \"Fiscal Q4 2026\",\n     \"value\": \"$1.59\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": \"Adjusted; restated\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"Non-GAAP EPS\",\n     \"period\": \"Latest four reported quarters\",\n     \"value\": \"around $6.95\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": \"Adjusted\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"Non-GAAP EPS\",\n     \"period\": \"Fiscal 2026\",\n     \"value\": \"$4.64\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": \"Adjusted\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"Non-GAAP EPS growth\",\n     \"period\": \"Fiscal Q4 2026 to fiscal Q2 2027\",\n     \"value\": \"around 40%\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": \"Two quarters\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"Non-GAAP EPS growth\",\n     \"period\": \"Latest four reported quarters vs fiscal 2026\",\n     \"value\": \"about 50%\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": null\n    }\n   ],\n   \"reports\": 1,\n   \"ref\": \"S64\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"day\": \"2026-09-22\",\n   \"statement\": \"NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress guided for 70% revenue growth in fiscal 2028, a figure the article says far exceeded the 45% Wall Street had previously modeled.\",\n   \"figures\": [\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"Revenue growth guidance\",\n     \"period\": \"Fiscal 2028\",\n     \"value\": \"70%\",\n     \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n     \"basis\": \"Company guidance\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"Consensus revenue growth estimate\",\n     \"period\": \"Fiscal 2028\",\n     \"value\": \"45%\",\n     \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n     \"basis\": \"Wall Street model previously expected, per the article\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"reports\": 11,\n   \"ref\": \"S65\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"day\": \"2026-10-05\",\n   \"statement\": \"NVIDIA itself expects its volume sales to double, an expectation the MarketBeat article ties to the Vera Rubin release and to the next wave of data center construction.\",\n   \"figures\": [\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"Volume sales\",\n     \"period\": \"Next year\",\n     \"value\": \"double\",\n     \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n     \"basis\": \"NVIDIA's own expectation, stated as underpinned by the Vera Rubin release\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"reports\": 1,\n   \"ref\": \"S66\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"day\": \"2026-10-05\",\n   \"statement\": \"NVIDIA returned $26.0 billion to shareholders in the quarter and had $99.0 billion remaining under its buyback authorization.\",\n   \"figures\": [\n    {\n     \"measure\": \"Buyback authorization remaining\",\n     \"period\": \"As of the report\",\n     \"value\": \"$99.0 billion\",\n     \"kind\": \"actual\",\n     \"basis\": \"remaining under buyback authorization\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"reports\": 1,\n   \"ref\": \"S67\"\n  }\n ],\n \"questions\": [\n  {\n   \"request\": \"Thesis v16 (custom-silicon, theme, focus \\\"Spending on custom AI silicon (ASICs) at cloud providers in calendar 2026\\\", 2026, due 2027-03-31): How much will cloud providers spend on custom AI silicon (ASICs) in calendar 2026?\\nLadder: rung 1 Below $40 billion: Custom-silicon spending is less than $40 billion. / rung 2 $40 billion to below $60 billion: Custom-silicon spending is at least $40 billion and less than $60 billion. / rung 3 $60 billion to below $80 billion: Custom-silicon spending is at least $60 billion and less than $80 billion. / rung 4 $80 billion to below $100 billion: Custom-silicon spending is at least $80 billion and less than $100 billion. / rung 5 $100 billion or more: Custom-silicon spending is at least $100 billion.\\n\\n[Evidence in view under this thesis, 2 items]\\n- e327 [rumour; moomoo.com; original source Meta ; from gnews:7b39859113d4e2b8ebd8aa86 2026-07-09] Meta reportedly plans to start manufacturing its AI chip in September 2026.\\n    Quote: Meta Platforms (META.US)$ is reportedly planning to start manufacturing its AI chip in September, Reuters reported Thursday, citing the company’s internal memo.\\n- e502 [party_statement; Fool.com analyst, reporting Marvell’s statement; original source Marvell Technology ; from gnews:37d2648cf990f4943cad6208 2026-07-14] Marvell says it has more than 50 potential custom AI chip opportunities across over 10 customers, representing potential lifetime revenue of $75 billion; no timeframe beyond “in the long run” is specified.\\n    Quote: In all, Marvell notes that it can tap into more than 50 custom AI chip opportunities across over 10 customers in the long run, leading to potential lifetime revenue of $75 billion.\\n\\n[Current conclusions of the theses it depends on]\\n- v6 (open; last_end) What share of calendar-2026 data-center AI accelerator spending will NVIDIA hold, by the first published full-year estimate?: now rung 3 Neutral; reasons: The closest market reference is the estimate that NVIDIA’s AI-chip share will be 75% in 2026, which is rung 3; longer-term estimates of 70%–75% and near 75% point to the same rung, while undated 80% estimates are less specific. The implied outcome is about 75%: those estimates concern the broader AI-chip market rather than precisely calendar-2026 data-center accelerator spending, but they are the best quantified proxies supplied. NVIDIA’s strong training position and Huawei’s low performance-adjusted share support a higher outcome; the lower inference share, growing custom-silicon alternatives\\n\\n[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 1; reasons: No supplied consensus, company guidance, or stated spending plan gives a market reference or a quantified 2026 spending estimate. The reported September manufacturing start is late in the year and gives no production volume or spending figure; it therefore does not imply that custom-ASIC spending reaches $40 billion. v6's neutral NVIDIA-share conclusion is consistent with custom ASICs taking some share, but does not quantify custom-ASIC spending. The most likely outcome remains below $40 billion.\\n\\nAnswer per the JSON schema. cites may name only the evidence ids listed above; cites_views only the dependency thesis ids listed above.\",\n   \"answer\": {\n    \"rung\": 1,\n    \"implied_outcome\": \"The implied calendar-2026 spending remains below $40 billion. Marvell’s $75 billion is potential lifetime revenue across more than 50 opportunities with no specified timeframe, not 2026 spending; Meta’s reported September manufacturing start gives no production volume or spend figure, implying at most an unquantified late-year contribution. v6’s neutral NVIDIA-share conclusion is consistent with custom silicon taking some share, but does not quantify its spending.\",\n    \"reasons\": \"No quantified market consensus, company guidance, or stated 2026 spending plan is supplied, so there is no reference rung to place on the ladder. The new evidence points to some 2026 custom-silicon activity, but neither item quantifies calendar-2026 provider spending; the implied outcome remains below $40 billion rather than crossing the $40 billion boundary.\"\n   },\n   \"ref\": \"Q13\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"ref\": \"Q4\",\n   \"request\": \"Thesis v4 (NVDA, asset, focus \\\"Whether Vera Rubin moves from full production to volume customer shipments and first revenue recognition within NVIDIA's fiscal 2027, as shown in the company's quarterly results and Form 10-K.\\\", 2027, due 2027-01-31): Will NVIDIA recognize revenue from Vera Rubin volume customer shipments by the end of FY2027, as disclosed in its quarterly results or FY2027 Form 10-K?\\nLadder: rung 1 Yes: NVIDIA's quarterly results or FY2027 Form 10-K disclose that it has begun recognizing revenue from Vera Rubin volume customer shipments by 2027-01-31. / rung 2 Ruled out: After 2026-09-23 and before 2027-01-31, NVIDIA formally abandons or cancels the Vera Rubin platform. This rung holds when that, or any other decision against the event of rung 1 as a whole, or a formal abandonment or withdrawal of it as a whole, was made after 2026-09-23 and stands on 2027-01-31, and only when rung 1 does not. / rung 3 Still open: Neither rung 1 nor rung 2 holds on 2027-01-31.\\n\\n[Evidence in view under this thesis, 53 items]\\n[Not shown: 37 more pieces in force, read in earlier judgements; the previous conclusion carries them]\\n- e85 [party_statement; Zacks.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:44b5d56e3c6718335d33ab2b 2026-09-23] NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin platform has entered the full production stage, positioning the company to benefit from the next phase of AI infrastructure investment, as reported by Zacks.\\n    Quote: Additionally, the next-generation Vera Rubin platform has entered the full production stage, positioning NVIDIA to benefit from the next phase of AI infrastructure investment.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e90, e185, e251, e283, e291, e320, e343; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e89 [party_statement; barchart.com; original source Nebius Group; CoreWeave ; from gnews:a5837f6a8cb2c7893ec87974 2026-09-23] Nebius Group announced another increase in prices for NVIDIA GPU rentals, and CoreWeave said it was continuing to contract new compute capacity at higher prices; the article cites both as fresh evidence that demand for NVIDIA-powered computing remains strong. No price levels, capacity amounts or dates are given.\\n    Quote: Meanwhile, demand from AI cloud providers remains robust as Nebius Group (NBIS) announced another increase in prices for Nvidia GPU rentals, while CoreWeave (CRWV) said it was continuing to contract new compute capacity at higher prices, providing fresh evidence that demand for Nvidia-powered computing remains strong.\\n- e90 [party_statement; barchart.com; original source NVIDIA management 2026-08-26; from gnews:a5837f6a8cb2c7893ec87974 2026-09-23] NVIDIA management pointed to full production of its next-generation Vera Rubin AI platform, and the article says continued AI infrastructure strength is accelerating NVIDIA's AI growth trajectory. The statement does not say Vera Rubin has begun volume customer shipments or revenue recognition.\\n    Quote: It's worth noting that Nvidia is not merely maintaining its AI growth trajectory; it is accelerating it, with management pointing to full production of its next-generation Vera Rubin platform and continued strength across AI infrastructure.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e85; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e181 [fact; GuruFocus (gurufocus.com); original source Super Micro Computer 2026-09-24; from gnews:cd0056006ad115260ae2b646 2026-09-24] Super Micro Computer moved into commercial shipments of servers built around NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin platform, shipping Vera Rubin NVL72 racks through its Data Center Building Block Solutions that combine computing hardware with liquid cooling, networking, storage and power components; each rack design uses 72 Rubin GPUs and 36 Vera CPUs, according to a GuruFocus report published 24 September 2026.\\n    Quote: the server maker moved into commercial shipments of systems built around NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin platform, adding another data-center product to its AI infrastructure lineup. … The company is shipping Vera Rubin NVL72 racks through its Data Center Building Block Solutions, combining computing hardware with liquid cooling, networking, storage and power components. The rack design uses 7\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e859, e951; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e188 [party_statement; fool.com (article via Yahoo Finance); original source SpaceX (Elon Musk, CEO) ; from gnews:943a57daed49d866fe468605 2026-09-24] On SpaceX's second-quarter earnings call, SpaceX CEO Elon Musk said SpaceX has decided to build exclusively on NVIDIA hardware because he considers the Vera Rubin architecture the best architecture and the best AI computer, citing close cooperation and partnership with NVIDIA; the article characterises this as making SpaceX (which owns xAI and its Grok model) exclusive to NVIDIA.\\n    Quote: During SpaceX's second-quarter earnings call, Musk had this to say about SpaceX's relationship with Nvidia: … We've decided to build exclusively on NVIDIA because we think the Vera Rubin architecture is the best architecture. We think it's the best AI computer, and we greatly value our close cooperation and partnership on many levels with NVIDIA. So, we're exclusive to NVIDIA.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e387, e396; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e208 [fact; Zacks (zacks.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:7fb0b2b7d22b8bb07625b212 2026-09-24] NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin platform began production shipments in the third quarter, according to Zacks; the item gives no volume, customer or revenue-recognition figure for the shipments.\\n    Quote: Vera Rubin began production shipments in the third quarter.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e234, e409, e598, e650, e721, e857; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e234 [fact; Trefis; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:06218d9421731e5e59c1dbef 2026-09-24] NVIDIA began production shipments of its Vera Rubin platform in August, with purchase orders already received from every major hyperscaler, AI cloud and system OEM, according to a 24 September 2026 article.\\n    Quote: Production shipments began in August, with purchase orders already in from every major hyperscaler, AI cloud, and system OEM.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e208; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e236 [party_statement; Trefis; original source NVIDIA (CFO) ; from gnews:06218d9421731e5e59c1dbef 2026-09-24] NVIDIA's CFO expects supply to remain a bottleneck at least through the end of fiscal 2028 and calls memory pricing extreme; the article reports memory prices have risen more than management expected.\\n    Quote: The CFO expects supply to stay a bottleneck at least through the end of fiscal 2028 and calls memory pricing extreme. … Memory prices have risen more than management expected, and the guide has that margin settling in the 72% to 73% range in fiscal 2028, helped by price increases NVIDIA has already executed.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e271, e413, e414, e556, e653, e774, e864, e865; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e243 [fact; Kieron Allen, Cloud Wars (cloudwars.com); original source Amazon Web Services and NVIDIA (joint announcement) ; from gnews:7c4283c55cf02ba9cfd9a835 2026-09-24] Amazon Web Services and NVIDIA announced an expansion of their long-standing partnership under which AWS will deploy 2 million additional NVIDIA GPUs across its infrastructure between 2027 and 2028, spanning NVIDIA's Blackwell Ultra, Rubin and Rubin Ultra architectures and deployed alongside NVIDIA Vera CPUs; the addition builds on the more than 1 million NVIDIA GPUs AWS had previously committed to deploying beginning in 2026, and Amazon said the aim is to meet surging demand for AI infrastructure from frontier labs, global enterprises, start-ups and governments. The number of GPUs is a planned deployment, not reported deliveries or revenue.\\n    Quote: Now, the two powerhouses have announced a staggering expansion of this existing partnership which will, among other things — but this is certainly the standout headline — include the deployment of 2 million additional NVIDIA GPUs across AWS infrastructure between 2027 and 2028. … The aim, according to Amazon, is to meet surging demand for AI infrastructure “from frontier labs, global enterprises, \\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e105; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e279 [third_party_view; qz.com, citing MarketWatch; original source Mehdi Hosseini, Susquehanna analyst (remarks to MarketWatch) ; from gnews:460160402f7a10882d1e925f 2026-09-25] Susquehanna analyst Mehdi Hosseini, in remarks reported by MarketWatch and cited by qz.com, said Micron's November-quarter (fiscal Q1 2026) sales forecast is strong enough to satisfy investors but will not fully reflect the benefits of the Nvidia product ramp; he said some estimated revenue is expected to shift toward next year instead of this year, implying the Nvidia-chip-related revenue payoff may be delayed.\\n    Quote: Micron $MU Technology posted record fiscal fourth-quarter revenue and issued strong guidance for the current quarter, but one analyst says investors may need to wait longer to see the full payoff from an Nvidia $NVDA chip launch. … Susquehanna analyst Mehdi Hosseini told MarketWatch that Micron 's November-quarter sales forecast looks strong enough to satisfy investors, though it won't fully refle\\n- e283 [party_statement; MarketBeat; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:91b917994e36036e304616e8 2026-09-25] NVIDIA states that its Vera Rubin AI platform is already in full production, with production shipments beginning in August 2026 and purchase orders received from every major hyperscaler, AI cloud and system original equipment manufacturer (OEM), as reported by MarketBeat.\\n    Quote: The trigger for this move could come from subsequent earnings reports and continued progress with Vera Rubin. The platform is already in full production, with NVIDIA reporting production shipments beginning in August and purchase orders from every major hyperscaler, AI cloud and system original equipment manufacturer (OEM).\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e85; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e370 [fact; cryptorank.io; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:844106b7a25ecc1175290290 2026-09-26] Nvidia reported that in the latest MLPerf Inference v6.1 tests its next-generation Vera Rubin NVL72 delivered up to 3.7x the throughput of its GB300 NVL72 on the Qwen3-VL benchmark and up to 2.5x on DeepSeek-R1, and described the Vera Rubin entry in its official MLPerf announcement as a preview submission ahead of broader deployment.\\n    Quote: In the latest MLPerf Inference v6.1 tests, Vera Rubin NVL72 delivered up to 3.7x the throughput of Nvidia’s GB300 NVL72 on the Qwen3-VL benchmark. On DeepSeek-R1, the improvement reached as much as 2.5x. … Nvidia detailed the results in its official MLPerf announcement , describing Vera Rubin’s entry as a preview submission ahead of broader deployment.\\n- e409 [fact; Trefis; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:d4f3bddf04a4d029a097541c 2026-09-27] NVIDIA began production shipments of its newest data center system, Vera Rubin, in August 2026, according to the article.\\n    Quote: The company began production shipments of Vera Rubin, its newest data center system, in August 2026.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e208; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e410 [party_statement; NVIDIA management; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:d4f3bddf04a4d029a097541c 2026-09-27] NVIDIA management expects Vera Rubin to account for about 20% of data center revenue in fiscal Q3 2027.\\n    Quote: Management expects Vera Rubin to bring in about 20% of data center revenue in fiscal Q3 2027.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e555, e764, e782, e813, e858; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e416 [party_statement; NVIDIA management; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:d4f3bddf04a4d029a097541c 2026-09-27] NVIDIA management expects sales to its largest cloud companies to speed up again in fiscal Q4 2027 as Vera Rubin supply grows.\\n    Quote: Management expects sales to the largest cloud companies to speed up again in fiscal Q4 2027, as Vera Rubin supply grows.\\n- e551 [fact; Trefis; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:4334522792e44c6fd9d18ccd 2026-09-28] NVIDIA management said on its fiscal Q2 2027 earnings call that inventory rose to $32 billion as the company prepared for the Vera Rubin launch.\\n    Quote: On the fiscal Q2 2027 call, management said inventory rose to $32 billion as NVIDIA prepared for the Vera Rubin launch.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e588; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e555 [party_statement; Trefis; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:4334522792e44c6fd9d18ccd 2026-09-28] NVIDIA management expects the newly launched Vera Rubin platform to make up about 20% of data center revenue in fiscal Q3 2027, according to Trefis.\\n    Quote: The newly launched Vera Rubin platform is one source of that revenue. Management expects it to make up about 20% of data center revenue in fiscal Q3 2027.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e410; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e633 [third_party_view; Adam Spatacco, fool.com; original source  ; from gnews:0e4e3f9b17e0cda6b72d47fc 2026-09-29] The article reports a forecast that Nvidia's next-generation Vera Rubin CPU architecture will reach $20 billion in sales next quarter.\\n    Quote: Meanwhile, the next-generation Vera Rubin CPU architecture is forecast to reach $20 billion in sales next quarter.\\n- e659 [fact; NVIDIA Corporation (NVIDIA Blog); original source CoreWeave ; from nvblog:coreweave-agentic-ai-vera-rubin 2026-09-30] CoreWeave announced at its CoreWeave Fully Connected event (held the week of the 30 September 2026 NVIDIA blog post) that NVIDIA Vera Rubin NVL72 systems with Spectrum-X 102.4T Ethernet networking are available on CoreWeave Cloud, making CoreWeave one of the first cloud providers to deliver the platform in customers' hands; per the announcement, Cognition, the applied AI lab behind the Devin AI software engineer, is the first customer running production workloads on Vera Rubin.\\n    Quote: At CoreWeave Fully Connected, running this week in San Francisco, CoreWeave announced availability of NVIDIA Vera Rubin NVL72 systems with Spectrum-X 102.4T Ethernet networking. Cognition, the applied AI lab behind the Devin AI software engineer, is the first customer running production workloads on Vera Rubin. … CoreWeave announced availability of NVIDIA Vera Rubin NVL72 on CoreWeave Cloud, makin\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e751, e860, e894, e895; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e660 [fact; NVIDIA Corporation (NVIDIA Blog); original source CoreWeave ; from nvblog:coreweave-agentic-ai-vera-rubin 2026-09-30] CoreWeave received its first NVIDIA Vera Rubin NVL72 production racks earlier in September 2026, before the 30 September 2026 article.\\n    Quote: Earlier this month, CoreWeave received its first Vera Rubin NVL72 production racks.\\n- e720 [fact; CRN; original source TD Synnex (CEO Patrick Zammit), with customer identity reported by CRN ; from gnews:84569ea837e5d2d93ff16093 2026-09-30] TD Synnex CEO Patrick Zammit said on the distributor's third-quarter earnings call (the Thursday before the 30 September 2026 article) that TD Synnex and Houston-based systems integrator Mark III Systems recently signed an agreement to support an NVIDIA AI factory powered by Vera Rubin NVL72 systems, which he described as 'one of the largest' enterprise AI factory infrastructure deployments expected to be delivered through the channel, bundling design, integration, deployment, Day 2 co-admin operations, financing and supply chain. CRN reported, citing what it learned, that the customer is a US-based Fortune 100 company. No contract value, system count or delivery date was disclosed.\\n    Quote: After TD Synnex revealed that Mark III Systems is set to deliver ‘one of the largest’ enterprise AI factory deployments in the channel, an executive at the systems integrator explained to CRN how its AI factory expertise and enterprise trends helped it seal the deal. … As hyperscalers and neoclouds begin to launch offerings based on Nvidia’s new flagship Vera Rubin rack-scale AI platform, one syst\\n- e751 [fact; GuruFocus (gurufocus.com); original source CoreWeave ; from gnews:2b6641fb175eb6c57e2bdff2 2026-09-30] CoreWeave (CRWV) announced at its Fully Connected AI conference in San Francisco that it is bringing NVIDIA (NVDA) Vera CPUs into its cloud portfolio for AI-agent workloads and will offer NVIDIA Vera Rubin NVL72 systems for production use, with Cognition named as the first customer running workloads on the platform. CoreWeave said the Rubin systems are already operating on its cloud service, which it described as a move from infrastructure deployment toward commercial use, and that customers can access the hardware under its existing operating model for NVIDIA systems.\\n    Quote: The company is bringing NVIDIA ( NVDA ) Vera CPUs to its cloud portfolio for workloads tied to AI agents. It will also offer NVIDIA Vera Rubin NVL72 systems for production use, with Cognition serving as the first customer running workloads on the platform. … CoreWeave said the Rubin systems are already operating on its cloud service, marking a move from infrastructure deployment toward commercial \\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e659; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e753 [third_party_view; NVIDIA Corporation (NVIDIA Blog); original source SemiAnalysis (AgentX data) ; from nvblog:productive-durable-fungible-ai-factories 2026-10-01] SemiAnalysis AgentX data cited in NVIDIA's blog states that NVIDIA Vera Rubin NVL72 systems deliver over 30x higher throughput per megawatt than NVIDIA GB300 NVL72 systems, and up to 45x lower cost per million tokens on the DeepSeek V4 Pro model.\\n    Quote: SemiAnalysis AgentX data shows NVIDIA Vera Rubin NVL72 systems deliver over 30x higher throughput per megawatt than NVIDIA GB300 NVL72, and up to 45x lower cost per million tokens on the DeepSeek V4 Pro model.\\n- e764 [party_statement; Zacks; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:95eab8d6d71dd0f94a8f763e 2026-10-01] NVIDIA said it started shipping its Vera Rubin platform in August 2026 and expects Vera Rubin to account for about 20% of Data Center revenues in fiscal Q3 2027.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA started shipping Vera Rubin in August and expects the platform to account for about 20% of Data Center revenues in the third quarter.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e410; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e765 [party_statement; Zacks; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:95eab8d6d71dd0f94a8f763e 2026-10-01] NVIDIA estimates revenue opportunity per gigawatt of AI infrastructure at approximately $18 billion for Hopper, $25 billion for Blackwell and $40 billion for Vera Rubin.\\n    Quote: The company estimates that revenue opportunity per gigawatt of AI infrastructure has increased from approximately $18 billion with Hopper to $25 billion with Blackwell and $40 billion with Vera Rubin.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e488; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e775 [party_statement; finance.yahoo.com article (contributor piece); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:890bfe7d206f0531a54beb16 2026-10-01] NVIDIA's Vera Rubin platform is now in full production, and the article states revenue per gigawatt of deployed capacity has risen from $18 billion for Hopper to $25 billion for Blackwell and $40 billion for Vera Rubin.\\n    Quote: Revenue per gigawatt rose from $18 billion for Hopper to $25 billion for Blackwell and $40 billion for Vera Rubin, now in full production.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e488; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e782 [third_party_view; finance.yahoo.com article (contributor piece); original source  ; from gnews:890bfe7d206f0531a54beb16 2026-10-01] The article estimates Vera Rubin will make up about 20% of NVIDIA's data center revenue in Q3 FY2027, with NVIDIA's next earnings report due November 17.\\n    Quote: Vera Rubin should make up about 20% of data center revenue in Q3, and the next earnings report lands November 17.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e410; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e811 [fact; fool.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:efb160acb7c94e14a52df9fa 2026-10-01] Nvidia launched its Vera CPU, a central processing unit positioned for agentic-AI workloads; the article reports the launch while noting that the agentic AI boom will likely raise CPU demand. The item gives no launch date, volume, customer or shipment timing.\\n    Quote: There is a significant opportunity in some of the niches it targets. For instance, the agentic AI boom will likely lead to increased demand for CPUs (central processing units), and to that end, Nvidia launched its Vera CPU.\\n- e813 [party_statement; Daniel Foelber, fool.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:e84d920e300dc3c29e501037 2026-10-01] Nvidia began shipping its Rubin platform in August and expects Rubin to account for 20% of data center revenue in the third quarter of fiscal 2027, which the company will report in November, according to the article.\\n    Quote: Nvidia began shipping Rubin in August and already expects it to account for 20% of data center revenue in the third quarter of fiscal 2027, which it will report in November.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e410; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e814 [party_statement; Daniel Foelber, fool.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:e84d920e300dc3c29e501037 2026-10-01] Nvidia is guiding for 70% year-over-year revenue growth in fiscal 2028, which the article attributes to massive demand for its Rubin platform.\\n    Quote: Demand for Rubin is so massive that Nvidia is guiding for 70% year-over-year revenue growth in fiscal 2028.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e104; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e832 [third_party_view; Keithen Drury, fool.com; original source Keithen Drury (fool.com) 2026-10-01; from gnews:6793603da478ba6858e0827d 2026-10-01] In his 1 October 2026 column, Keithen Drury (fool.com) wrote that Nvidia's new Rubin-generation chips are starting to become available, will provide more advanced capabilities than prior generations, and that Nvidia will charge a higher price for them, which he called a built-in revenue escalator 'launching shortly'. No shipment volumes, dates or prices are given, and the claim is the author's characterisation rather than a company announcement.\\n    Quote: Even though competition is intensifying for which company has the best available option, Nvidia's new Rubin generation of chips will make an even greater impact than prior iterations. These computing units are starting to become available and will provide more advanced capabilities than prior generations. Nvidia will also charge a higher price tag for them, so it has a built-in revenue escalator t\\n- e857 [fact; Yahoo Finance (finance.yahoo.com); original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:8867ec84bb48b27c3ae694f5 2026-10-02] NVIDIA management said on the fiscal Q2 2027 earnings call that the company began production shipments of its Vera Rubin AI data-center platform in August 2026.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA began production shipments in August 2026, management said on the fiscal Q2 2027 call.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e208; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e858 [party_statement; Yahoo Finance (finance.yahoo.com); original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:8867ec84bb48b27c3ae694f5 2026-10-02] NVIDIA management forecast on the fiscal Q2 2027 call that Vera Rubin would make up about 20% of data center revenue in fiscal Q3 2027; the article notes this is a forecast, not a result.\\n    Quote: Management expects Vera Rubin to make up about 20% of data center revenue in fiscal Q3 2027. That is a forecast, not a result.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e410; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e859 [fact; Yahoo Finance (finance.yahoo.com); original source Supermicro 2026-09-23; from gnews:8867ec84bb48b27c3ae694f5 2026-10-02] Supermicro said on 23 September 2026 that it is shipping racks built on NVIDIA's Vera Rubin platform.\\n    Quote: Supermicro said on September 23, 2026 that it is shipping racks built on Vera Rubin.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e181; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e860 [fact; Yahoo Finance (finance.yahoo.com); original source CoreWeave 2026-09-30; from gnews:8867ec84bb48b27c3ae694f5 2026-10-02] CoreWeave, a cloud company, said on 30 September 2026 that one of its customers runs production workloads on NVIDIA's Vera Rubin system.\\n    Quote: CoreWeave, a cloud company, said on September 30 that one of its customers runs production workloads on the system.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e659; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e864 [party_statement; Yahoo Finance (finance.yahoo.com); original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:8867ec84bb48b27c3ae694f5 2026-10-02] NVIDIA management says the company cannot build enough to meet demand, called its fiscal 2028 forecast supply-constrained, expects supply to stay a bottleneck through at least the end of that year, and said on the fiscal Q2 2027 call that its entire supply chain is running flat out.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA cannot build enough to meet demand, management says. Management called its fiscal 2028 forecast supply-constrained, and expects supply to stay a bottleneck through at least the end of that year. Asked on the fiscal Q2 2027 call what holds supply back, management said its entire supply chain is running flat out.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e236; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e894 [fact; wccftech.com, quoting NVIDIA VP Ian Buck; original source NVIDIA and CoreWeave ; from gnews:4875d3af5072a2d9c8532a7f 2026-10-02] NVIDIA and CoreWeave announced at CoreWeave Fully Connected 2026 that the NVIDIA Vera Rubin NVL72 platform is available on CoreWeave Cloud, with NVIDIA VP Ian Buck stating that CoreWeave is bringing Vera Rubin NVL72 into production even as its older V100 GPUs still run customer workloads nearly a decade after Volta launched.\\n    Quote: At CoreWeave Fully Connected 2026, NVIDIA and CoreWeave announce the availability of NVIDIA Vera Rubin NVL72 on CoreWeave Cloud. … “NVIDIA accelerated computing delivers value across generations,” said Ian Buck, vice president of hyperscale and high-performance computing at NVIDIA. “CoreWeave’s NVIDIA V100 GPUs are still running customer workloads nearly a decade after Volta launched, even as Core\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e659; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e895 [fact; wccftech.com; original source CoreWeave / Cognition ; from gnews:4875d3af5072a2d9c8532a7f 2026-10-02] Cognition is the first customer to secure installations of NVIDIA Vera Rubin NVL72 servers at CoreWeave, and CoreWeave stood up a production Vera Rubin cluster for it in days, with Cognition scaling to thousands of GPUs within nine months; Cognition had earlier benchmarked Vera Rubin against Grace Blackwell in September, recording a 4.8x gain in total token throughput for SWE-2 inference workloads.\\n    Quote: Cognition is the first to secure a hold on these latest server installations at CoreWeave, and has been able to scale to thousands of GPUs within nine months. Earlier in September, Cognition was the first to benchmark Vera Rubin against Grace Blackwell in inference, and was able to record a 4.8x gain in total token throughput for SWE-2 inference workloads. … In days, CoreWeave stood up a productio\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e659; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e907 [party_statement; Daniel Foelber (The Motley Fool); original source Nvidia (guidance); margin/demand attribution by the article's author ; from gnews:d9f1147865cff881040d3dd0 2026-10-02] The article states that Nvidia is already guiding for a 70% increase in revenue for fiscal 2028, and the author attributes continued high margins to what he calls insatiable demand for the Vera Rubin compute platform.\\n    Quote: For fiscal 2028, Nvidia is already guiding for a 70% increase in revenue. … And its margins should remain sky high thanks to insatiable demand for the Vera Rubin compute platform .\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e104; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e951 [fact; finance.yahoo.com; original source Supermicro 2026-09-23; from gnews:d38374c4abf4bd13d2cd2ccd 2026-10-02] On Sept. 23, Supermicro said it had started shipping racks built on Nvidia's newest platform, Vera Rubin; each rack carries 72 Rubin GPUs.\\n    Quote: On Sept. 23, Supermicro said it had started shipping racks, the standard cabinets that hold servers inside a data center, built on Nvidia's newest platform, Vera Rubin. Each rack carries 72 Rubin GPUs.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e181; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e978 [fact; barchart.com; original source Akamai Technologies 2026-09-24; from gnews:6121a00939cb0069709b5c54 2026-10-03] Akamai Technologies said on 24 September 2026 that it had significantly expanded its partnership with Anthropic; Anthropic committed to spend $11.6 billion over seven years on dedicated cloud capacity and related services for its growing CPU workloads, with an option to commit an additional $9 billion if conditions are met, bringing the total value to about $20 billion. The article says the deal does not directly involve Nvidia based on public disclosure.\\n    Quote: A major new agreement between cloud infrastructure provider Akamai Technologies (AKAM) and frontier AI lab Anthropic could add another piece to that story. The nearly $12 billion deal does not directly involve Nvidia, at least based on what has been publicly disclosed so far. … On Sept. 24, cloud infrastructure company Akamai Technologies significantly expanded its partnership with frontier AI lab\\n- e979 [third_party_view; barchart.com; original source GF Securities (Henry Huang) ; from gnews:6121a00939cb0069709b5c54 2026-10-03] GF Securities analyst Henry Huang said the Akamai-Anthropic CPU cloud deal is expected to be on Nvidia's Vera CPUs, which he said Akamai had previously disclosed as its planned platform for agentic workloads; the article presents this as an expectation, not confirmation.\\n    Quote: \\\"The deal is expected to be on Nvidia's Vera CPUs, which Akamai previously disclosed as its planned platform for agentic workloads,\\\" according to GF Securities analyst Henry Huang.\\n- e980 [fact; barchart.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:6121a00939cb0069709b5c54 2026-10-03] Nvidia said in an August 2026 blog post that it had delivered Vera CPU systems to Anthropic; the article also notes Nvidia had named Anthropic among AI labs planning to adopt Vera at GTC Taipei in May. The delivery is reported as completed, but no volume or revenue is disclosed.\\n    Quote: Nvidia said at GTC Taipei that Anthropic was among the AI labs planning to adopt Vera, and the chipmaker added in an August blog post that it had delivered Vera CPU systems to Anthropic.\\n- e981 [party_statement; barchart.com; original source Akamai Technologies ; from gnews:6121a00939cb0069709b5c54 2026-10-03] Akamai expects roughly $5.5 billion of capital expenditures tied to Anthropic's commitment, including an additional $1.7 billion in 2026 to secure and pre-purchase critical supply-chain components; the article says not all of that spending will go toward Nvidia's CPUs because Akamai also needs memory, ABF substrates and other components.\\n    Quote: Akamai expects roughly $5.5 billion in capital expenditures tied to Anthropic's commitment, including an additional $1.7 billion in 2026 to secure and pre-purchase critical supply-chain components. It is worth noting that not all of that spending will go toward Nvidia's CPUs, as Akamai will also need to purchase memory, ABF substrates, and other components.\\n- e982 [party_statement; barchart.com; original source NVIDIA (CFO Colette Kress) ; from gnews:6121a00939cb0069709b5c54 2026-10-03] On Nvidia's Q2 earnings call, CFO Colette Kress said trailing-12-month revenue from Grace CPUs had already exceeded $5 billion and that next-generation Vera was in full production; she said Nvidia expects Vera to be deployed by every major hyperscaler, neocloud, AI lab and system OEM, continues to see demand for roughly $20 billion of server CPUs, and preliminarily expects CPU revenue to more than double year-over-year in fiscal 2028.\\n    Quote: Nvidia CFO Colette Kress said on the Q2 earnings call last month that trailing-12-month revenue from Grace CPUs had already exceeded $5 billion, while next-generation Vera was in full production. Kress said Nvidia expects Vera to be deployed by every major hyperscaler, neocloud, AI lab, and system OEM, adding that the company continues to see demand for roughly $20 billion of server CPUs. She also\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e632; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1046 [party_statement; Yahoo Finance; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:ed14a9d1371ea43f3c5dcd3d 2026-10-04] NVIDIA says Grace Blackwell is already one generation behind Vera Rubin and that Vera Rubin delivers 35 times lower token costs than Grace Blackwell Ultra; NVIDIA estimates about $25 billion of revenue per gigawatt for Grace Blackwell and roughly $40 billion per gigawatt for Vera Rubin.\\n    Quote: Grace Blackwell is already one generation behind Vera Rubin. NVIDIA says Vera Rubin delivers 35X lower token costs than Grace Blackwell Ultra. NVIDIA estimates about $25 billion per gigawatt of revenue for Grace Blackwell and roughly $40 billion for Vera Rubin.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e117; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1060 [fact; Brett Schafer, Fool.com; original source Taiwan Semiconductor Manufacturing (TSMC) ; from gnews:654a1658fd6ce4b1694951fc 2026-10-04] Taiwan Semiconductor Manufacturing (TSMC), the sole at-scale manufacturer of Nvidia's chips, reported revenue of $143 billion over the past 12 months and revenue up 34% year over year in the most recent quarter, with its high-performance-compute (HPC) segment rising 20% quarter over quarter and now at 66% of the overall business, per Fool.com's account.\\n    Quote: Revenue was $143 billion during the past 12 months and rose 34% year-over-year last quarter. The company is seeing booming demand from its high performance compute (HPC) segment, which increased 20% quarter-over-quarter and now makes up 66% of the overall business.\\n- e1098 [fact; finance.yahoo.com; original source Foxconn (Hon Hai Precision Industry) 2026-10-05; from gnews:3b878790fc298c0b29ba8205 2026-10-05] Foxconn, described as Nvidia's biggest server maker, reported third-quarter 2026 revenue up 47% to T$3.03 trillion ($95.4 billion), above the LSEG SmartEstimate of T$2.83 trillion — a supplier volume signal for Nvidia's AI server business, though the item does not specify which Nvidia platform drove it.\\n    Quote: This morning, Foxconn, Nvidia's biggest server maker, reported third-quarter revenue up 47% to T$3.03 trillion ($95.4 billion), above the LSEG SmartEstimate of T$2.83 trillion.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1180; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1101 [fact; fool.com; original source NVIDIA (product status reported by fool.com) ; from gnews:a6cfdb974af32e5f975e0d4f 2026-10-05] NVIDIA's GPU release cadence continues: after Blackwell and Blackwell Ultra launched in recent years, the release of the current Vera Rubin system is now unfolding, according to fool.com's 5 October 2026 article.\\n    Quote: Nvidia has pledged to update its GPUs on an annual basis, and it's following through on this, with Blackwell and Blackwell Ultra launched in recent years and the current release of the Vera Rubin system now unfolding.\\n- e1125 [party_statement; NVIDIA management (via finance.yahoo.com); original source NVIDIA 2026-08-26; from gnews:17d71c8bf611131c4216e2eb 2026-10-05] NVIDIA management said each new product generation brings in more money for the same data-center capacity, putting revenue per gigawatt at roughly $18 billion for Hopper, $25 billion for Blackwell and $40 billion for Vera Rubin.\\n    Quote: Each new product generation also brings in more money for the same amount of data center capacity. Management put revenue per gigawatt at roughly $18 billion for Hopper, $25 billion for Blackwell and $40 billion for Vera Rubin.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e488; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1148 [party_statement; MarketBeat; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:db898bbdf8476a62d9167361 2026-10-05] NVIDIA itself expects its volume sales to double, an expectation the MarketBeat article ties to the Vera Rubin release and to the next wave of data center construction.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA itself expects volume sales to double, underpinned by the Vera Rubin release.\\n- e1177 [fact; Business Wire (AM Intelligence press release, via Yahoo Finance UK); original source AM Intelligence (AMI) 2026-10-05; from gnews:8ec8d22f933c8556d24c6c9d 2026-10-05] AM Intelligence (AMI), the AI infrastructure platform set up by promoters of Greenko, announced on 5 October 2026 two further firm and binding orders for 20,000 NVIDIA Rubin GPUs to be deployed as NVIDIA Vera Rubin NVL72 rack-scale systems, on top of its inaugural commitment announced 25 August 2026 for 9,000 NVIDIA Rubin GPUs at its first AI Factory in Hyderabad. The follow-on capacity totals about 70 MW at Malaysia and India with delivery scheduled for Q2 2027, and the three orders together bring AMI's committed frontier compute to approximately 29,000 Rubin GPUs and close to 100 MW, which AMI describes as making it among the earliest and largest committed buyers of Rubin GPUs outside North America.\\n    Quote: Two further binding orders of ~20,000 NVIDIA GPUs take AMI's committed NVIDIA Vera Rubin capacity to approximately ~29,000 GPUs and close to 100 MW, part of its global development pipeline of ~400 MW … today announced two further firm and binding orders for 20,000 NVIDIA Rubin GPUs , to be deployed as NVIDIA Vera Rubin NVL72 rack-scale systems. … These orders are in addition to the AMI's inaugural\\n- e1178 [party_statement; Business Wire (AM Intelligence press release, via Yahoo Finance UK); original source AM Intelligence (AMI) 2026-10-05; from gnews:8ec8d22f933c8556d24c6c9d 2026-10-05] AM Intelligence said it plans to bring an additional 300 MW of compute capacity to market in the next 15 months, requiring capital expenditure of more than $20 billion on top of the $6 billion already committed for its initial 100 MW. It said it has developed a pipeline of about 400 MW of Compute-as-a-Service capacity across India, the United States, Europe and Malaysia, that the three Rubin GPU orders are the first committed tranche of that pipeline, and that the balance is under development for delivery in subsequent phases in 2027 and early 2028.\\n    Quote: AMI plans to bring additional 300 MWs of capacity to market in the next 15 months, involving capital expenditure of more than $20+ billion in addition to the $6 billion already committed for the initial $100 MW. … AMI has developed a pipeline of ~400 MW of Compute-as-a-Service capacity across global locations, including India, the United States, Europe and Malaysia. The three orders placed to date\\n\\n[Themes in force, 6]\\n- v10 (ai-capex, due 2027-03-31) How many gigawatts of new data-center capacity will be added in the United States during calendar 2026, as determined by full-year 2026 grid-operator and utility interconnection/capacity data and industry data-center capacity reports?: now rung 2 from 10 GW to below 15 GW; judged 2026-10-05; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The reference is Huang’s rough 10–20 GW annual estimate: its 10–<15 portion is rung 2, its 15–<20 portion is rung 3, and 20 GW reaches rung 4; the midpoint, 15 GW, is rung 3. I forecast below that midpoint because the quantified trades shortfall, the stated net power shortfall, and the two-to-three-\\n- v11 (semiconductor-cycle, due 2027-03-31) What is the percentage change in average DRAM (including HBM) contract prices in calendar 2026 versus calendar 2025, according to TrendForce/DRAMeXchange full-year 2026 contract-price data?: now rung 5 30 percent or more; judged 2026-10-05; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The reference is the opening no-reference position, rung 3 (10% to below 20%); no consensus, company guidance, or TrendForce/DRAMeXchange full-year figure for this measure is supplied. The forecast is above that reference: Micron says the DRAM imbalance has no visible return-to-balance date and that\\n- v3 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will U.S. authorities authorize exports of Nvidia’s current Blackwell-generation AI chips to China and Chinese authorities allow those shipments by January 31, 2027, as verified by official export-control or licensing decisions, Chinese regulatory or customs actions, or Nvidia disclosures?: now rung 3 Still open; judged 2026-10-05; reasons: The nearest observable market reference is NVIDIA’s Q3 FY2027 guidance, which assumes no China Data Center compute revenue; that sits at rung 3, Still open, rather than establishing that approval is formally ruled out by the due date. The evidence implies no verified U.S. authorization for Blackwell\\n- v5 (ai-capex, due 2027-03-31) What will the five largest AI hyperscalers' combined data-center capital expenditure be in calendar 2026, according to their full-year earnings and capital-expenditure disclosures?: now rung 3 from 750,000,000,000 USD to below 850,000,000,000 USD; judged 2026-10-05; reasons: The reference is Nvidia's estimate of nearly $800 billion for the big five in calendar 2026, which sits in rung 3. Amazon's approximately $220 billion 2026 capex projection, versus its previous $200 billion level, supports about a $20 billion increase to that reference: roughly $820 billion, still w\\n- v7 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will China's Ministry of Industry and Information Technology (MIIT) formally approve purchases of Nvidia's RTX Pro 5500 by Alibaba, ByteDance, and other Chinese firms by January 31, 2027?: now rung 1 Yes; judged 2026-09-30; reasons: The reference is rung 3: no market consensus or formal decision is supplied, and the reported status is still deliberation rather than approval or rejection. The forecast nevertheless favors rung 1: the specific report says officials indicated purchases were likely to be approved and MIIT intended t\\n- v9 (ai-capex, due 2028-03-30) How much will the five largest AI hyperscalers spend in total on data centers in calendar 2027, as shown by their capex disclosures and reported 2027 results?: now rung 3 from 1,200,000,000,000 USD to below 1,400,000,000,000 USD; judged 2026-10-05; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The reference is Nvidia's projection of $1.3 trillion for calendar 2027, which sits in rung 3. Barclays' approximately $1.1 trillion figure is for cloud capital spending and is not a like-for-like estimate of the five hyperscalers' data-center capex; the demand evidence, including the new customer-g\\n\\n[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 1; reasons: The reference is NVIDIA management's expectation that Vera Rubin will make up about 20% of Data Center revenue in fiscal Q3 2027, which is rung 1 because Q3 falls within FY2027. That guidance alone is a forecast, not proof of recognized revenue. But the evidence also says production shipments began in August, Supermicro is shipping Vera Rubin racks, and CoreWeave has the platform running production workloads for Cognition; these make the guided Q3 revenue share more likely to be realized and disclosed by the due date. The supply-bottleneck and possible-delay evidence is a counterweight, but does not outweigh the near-term guidance and customer deployment evidence. The updated capacity and DRAM themes add further caution; they do not move the forecast below rung 1.\\n\\nAnswer per the JSON schema. cites may name only the evidence ids listed above; cites_views must be empty: this thesis depends on no other thesis; theme_pushes only the theme ids listed above; a theme id goes only in theme_pushes, never in cites or cites_views.\",\n   \"answer\": {\n    \"rung\": 1,\n    \"implied_outcome\": \"The most likely outcome is that NVIDIA recognizes Vera Rubin revenue in Q3 FY2027 and discloses it in its quarterly results, with the guided contribution of about 20% of Data Center revenue. This is inferred from the guide together with production shipments beginning in August and customer production deployments; it is not yet established by a reported result in the evidence shown.\",\n    \"reasons\": \"The reference is NVIDIA management's expectation that Vera Rubin will account for about 20% of Data Center revenue in fiscal Q3 2027; because Q3 results are due before 2027-01-31, that reference sits at rung 1. The forecast remains rung 1: the company says production shipments began in August, its guide gives a substantial Q3 revenue contribution, and customers and suppliers report Vera Rubin systems in production or shipping. These facts make recognition in Q3 and disclosure by the due date more likely than not, although the evidence shown does not itself establish that revenue has already been recognized. Supply constraints and the AMI orders' later delivery schedule temper the forecast but do not overturn it; there is no evidence of abandonment to support rung 2.\"\n   }\n  }\n ]\n}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/carried-run/codex-figures-controls.stream:10:{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; p=json.load(open(\\\"docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/carried-run/packet-figures-controls.json\\\")); [print(\\\"QUESTION\\\",q[\\\"ref\\\"],\\\"\\\\n\\\",q[\\\"request\\\"]) for q in p[\\\"questions\\\"]]'\"","aggregated_output":"QUESTION Q13 \n Thesis v16 (custom-silicon, theme, focus \"Spending on custom AI silicon (ASICs) at cloud providers in calendar 2026\", 2026, due 2027-03-31): How much will cloud providers spend on custom AI silicon (ASICs) in calendar 2026?\nLadder: rung 1 Below $40 billion: Custom-silicon spending is less than $40 billion. / rung 2 $40 billion to below $60 billion: Custom-silicon spending is at least $40 billion and less than $60 billion. / rung 3 $60 billion to below $80 billion: Custom-silicon spending is at least $60 billion and less than $80 billion. / rung 4 $80 billion to below $100 billion: Custom-silicon spending is at least $80 billion and less than $100 billion. / rung 5 $100 billion or more: Custom-silicon spending is at least $100 billion.\n\n[Evidence in view under this thesis, 2 items]\n- e327 [rumour; moomoo.com; original source Meta ; from gnews:7b39859113d4e2b8ebd8aa86 2026-07-09] Meta reportedly plans to start manufacturing its AI chip in September 2026.\n    Quote: Meta Platforms (META.US)$ is reportedly planning to start manufacturing its AI chip in September, Reuters reported Thursday, citing the company’s internal memo.\n- e502 [party_statement; Fool.com analyst, reporting Marvell’s statement; original source Marvell Technology ; from gnews:37d2648cf990f4943cad6208 2026-07-14] Marvell says it has more than 50 potential custom AI chip opportunities across over 10 customers, representing potential lifetime revenue of $75 billion; no timeframe beyond “in the long run” is specified.\n    Quote: In all, Marvell notes that it can tap into more than 50 custom AI chip opportunities across over 10 customers in the long run, leading to potential lifetime revenue of $75 billion.\n\n[Current conclusions of the theses it depends on]\n- v6 (open; last_end) What share of calendar-2026 data-center AI accelerator spending will NVIDIA hold, by the first published full-year estimate?: now rung 3 Neutral; reasons: The closest market reference is the estimate that NVIDIA’s AI-chip share will be 75% in 2026, which is rung 3; longer-term estimates of 70%–75% and near 75% point to the same rung, while undated 80% estimates are less specific. The implied outcome is about 75%: those estimates concern the broader AI-chip market rather than precisely calendar-2026 data-center accelerator spending, but they are the best quantified proxies supplied. NVIDIA’s strong training position and Huawei’s low performance-adjusted share support a higher outcome; the lower inference share, growing custom-silicon alternatives\n\n[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 1; reasons: No supplied consensus, company guidance, or stated spending plan gives a market reference or a quantified 2026 spending estimate. The reported September manufacturing start is late in the year and gives no production volume or spending figure; it therefore does not imply that custom-ASIC spending reaches $40 billion. v6's neutral NVIDIA-share conclusion is consistent with custom ASICs taking some share, but does not quantify custom-ASIC spending. The most likely outcome remains below $40 billion.\n\nAnswer per the JSON schema. cites may name only the evidence ids listed above; cites_views only the dependency thesis ids listed above.\nQUESTION Q4 \n Thesis v4 (NVDA, asset, focus \"Whether Vera Rubin moves from full production to volume customer shipments and first revenue recognition within NVIDIA's fiscal 2027, as shown in the company's quarterly results and Form 10-K.\", 2027, due 2027-01-31): Will NVIDIA recognize revenue from Vera Rubin volume customer shipments by the end of FY2027, as disclosed in its quarterly results or FY2027 Form 10-K?\nLadder: rung 1 Yes: NVIDIA's quarterly results or FY2027 Form 10-K disclose that it has begun recognizing revenue from Vera Rubin volume customer shipments by 2027-01-31. / rung 2 Ruled out: After 2026-09-23 and before 2027-01-31, NVIDIA formally abandons or cancels the Vera Rubin platform. This rung holds when that, or any other decision against the event of rung 1 as a whole, or a formal abandonment or withdrawal of it as a whole, was made after 2026-09-23 and stands on 2027-01-31, and only when rung 1 does not. / rung 3 Still open: Neither rung 1 nor rung 2 holds on 2027-01-31.\n\n[Evidence in view under this thesis, 53 items]\n[Not shown: 37 more pieces in force, read in earlier judgements; the previous conclusion carries them]\n- e85 [party_statement; Zacks.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:44b5d56e3c6718335d33ab2b 2026-09-23] NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin platform has entered the full production stage, positioning the company to benefit from the next phase of AI infrastructure investment, as reported by Zacks.\n    Quote: Additionally, the next-generation Vera Rubin platform has entered the full production stage, positioning NVIDIA to benefit from the next phase of AI infrastructure investment.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e90, e185, e251, e283, e291, e320, e343; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e89 [party_statement; barchart.com; original source Nebius Group; CoreWeave ; from gnews:a5837f6a8cb2c7893ec87974 2026-09-23] Nebius Group announced another increase in prices for NVIDIA GPU rentals, and CoreWeave said it was continuing to contract new compute capacity at higher prices; the article cites both as fresh evidence that demand for NVIDIA-powered computing remains strong. No price levels, capacity amounts or dates are given.\n    Quote: Meanwhile, demand from AI cloud providers remains robust as Nebius Group (NBIS) announced another increase in prices for Nvidia GPU rentals, while CoreWeave (CRWV) said it was continuing to contract new compute capacity at higher prices, providing fresh evidence that demand for Nvidia-powered computing remains strong.\n- e90 [party_statement; barchart.com; original source NVIDIA management 2026-08-26; from gnews:a5837f6a8cb2c7893ec87974 2026-09-23] NVIDIA management pointed to full production of its next-generation Vera Rubin AI platform, and the article says continued AI infrastructure strength is accelerating NVIDIA's AI growth trajectory. The statement does not say Vera Rubin has begun volume customer shipments or revenue recognition.\n    Quote: It's worth noting that Nvidia is not merely maintaining its AI growth trajectory; it is accelerating it, with management pointing to full production of its next-generation Vera Rubin platform and continued strength across AI infrastructure.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e85; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e181 [fact; GuruFocus (gurufocus.com); original source Super Micro Computer 2026-09-24; from gnews:cd0056006ad115260ae2b646 2026-09-24] Super Micro Computer moved into commercial shipments of servers built around NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin platform, shipping Vera Rubin NVL72 racks through its Data Center Building Block Solutions that combine computing hardware with liquid cooling, networking, storage and power components; each rack design uses 72 Rubin GPUs and 36 Vera CPUs, according to a GuruFocus report published 24 September 2026.\n    Quote: the server maker moved into commercial shipments of systems built around NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin platform, adding another data-center product to its AI infrastructure lineup. … The company is shipping Vera Rubin NVL72 racks through its Data Center Building Block Solutions, combining computing hardware with liquid cooling, networking, storage and power components. The rack design uses 7\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e859, e951; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e188 [party_statement; fool.com (article via Yahoo Finance); original source SpaceX (Elon Musk, CEO) ; from gnews:943a57daed49d866fe468605 2026-09-24] On SpaceX's second-quarter earnings call, SpaceX CEO Elon Musk said SpaceX has decided to build exclusively on NVIDIA hardware because he considers the Vera Rubin architecture the best architecture and the best AI computer, citing close cooperation and partnership with NVIDIA; the article characterises this as making SpaceX (which owns xAI and its Grok model) exclusive to NVIDIA.\n    Quote: During SpaceX's second-quarter earnings call, Musk had this to say about SpaceX's relationship with Nvidia: … We've decided to build exclusively on NVIDIA because we think the Vera Rubin architecture is the best architecture. We think it's the best AI computer, and we greatly value our close cooperation and partnership on many levels with NVIDIA. So, we're exclusive to NVIDIA.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e387, e396; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e208 [fact; Zacks (zacks.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:7fb0b2b7d22b8bb07625b212 2026-09-24] NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin platform began production shipments in the third quarter, according to Zacks; the item gives no volume, customer or revenue-recognition figure for the shipments.\n    Quote: Vera Rubin began production shipments in the third quarter.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e234, e409, e598, e650, e721, e857; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e234 [fact; Trefis; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:06218d9421731e5e59c1dbef 2026-09-24] NVIDIA began production shipments of its Vera Rubin platform in August, with purchase orders already received from every major hyperscaler, AI cloud and system OEM, according to a 24 September 2026 article.\n    Quote: Production shipments began in August, with purchase orders already in from every major hyperscaler, AI cloud, and system OEM.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e208; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e236 [party_statement; Trefis; original source NVIDIA (CFO) ; from gnews:06218d9421731e5e59c1dbef 2026-09-24] NVIDIA's CFO expects supply to remain a bottleneck at least through the end of fiscal 2028 and calls memory pricing extreme; the article reports memory prices have risen more than management expected.\n    Quote: The CFO expects supply to stay a bottleneck at least through the end of fiscal 2028 and calls memory pricing extreme. … Memory prices have risen more than management expected, and the guide has that margin settling in the 72% to 73% range in fiscal 2028, helped by price increases NVIDIA has already executed.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e271, e413, e414, e556, e653, e774, e864, e865; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e243 [fact; Kieron Allen, Cloud Wars (cloudwars.com); original source Amazon Web Services and NVIDIA (joint announcement) ; from gnews:7c4283c55cf02ba9cfd9a835 2026-09-24] Amazon Web Services and NVIDIA announced an expansion of their long-standing partnership under which AWS will deploy 2 million additional NVIDIA GPUs across its infrastructure between 2027 and 2028, spanning NVIDIA's Blackwell Ultra, Rubin and Rubin Ultra architectures and deployed alongside NVIDIA Vera CPUs; the addition builds on the more than 1 million NVIDIA GPUs AWS had previously committed to deploying beginning in 2026, and Amazon said the aim is to meet surging demand for AI infrastructure from frontier labs, global enterprises, start-ups and governments. The number of GPUs is a planned deployment, not reported deliveries or revenue.\n    Quote: Now, the two powerhouses have announced a staggering expansion of this existing partnership which will, among other things — but this is certainly the standout headline — include the deployment of 2 million additional NVIDIA GPUs across AWS infrastructure between 2027 and 2028. … The aim, according to Amazon, is to meet surging demand for AI infrastructure “from frontier labs, global enterprises, \n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e105; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e279 [third_party_view; qz.com, citing MarketWatch; original source Mehdi Hosseini, Susquehanna analyst (remarks to MarketWatch) ; from gnews:460160402f7a10882d1e925f 2026-09-25] Susquehanna analyst Mehdi Hosseini, in remarks reported by MarketWatch and cited by qz.com, said Micron's November-quarter (fiscal Q1 2026) sales forecast is strong enough to satisfy investors but will not fully reflect the benefits of the Nvidia product ramp; he said some estimated revenue is expected to shift toward next year instead of this year, implying the Nvidia-chip-related revenue payoff may be delayed.\n    Quote: Micron $MU Technology posted record fiscal fourth-quarter revenue and issued strong guidance for the current quarter, but one analyst says investors may need to wait longer to see the full payoff from an Nvidia $NVDA chip launch. … Susquehanna analyst Mehdi Hosseini told MarketWatch that Micron 's November-quarter sales forecast looks strong enough to satisfy investors, though it won't fully refle\n- e283 [party_statement; MarketBeat; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:91b917994e36036e304616e8 2026-09-25] NVIDIA states that its Vera Rubin AI platform is already in full production, with production shipments beginning in August 2026 and purchase orders received from every major hyperscaler, AI cloud and system original equipment manufacturer (OEM), as reported by MarketBeat.\n    Quote: The trigger for this move could come from subsequent earnings reports and continued progress with Vera Rubin. The platform is already in full production, with NVIDIA reporting production shipments beginning in August and purchase orders from every major hyperscaler, AI cloud and system original equipment manufacturer (OEM).\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e85; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e370 [fact; cryptorank.io; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:844106b7a25ecc1175290290 2026-09-26] Nvidia reported that in the latest MLPerf Inference v6.1 tests its next-generation Vera Rubin NVL72 delivered up to 3.7x the throughput of its GB300 NVL72 on the Qwen3-VL benchmark and up to 2.5x on DeepSeek-R1, and described the Vera Rubin entry in its official MLPerf announcement as a preview submission ahead of broader deployment.\n    Quote: In the latest MLPerf Inference v6.1 tests, Vera Rubin NVL72 delivered up to 3.7x the throughput of Nvidia’s GB300 NVL72 on the Qwen3-VL benchmark. On DeepSeek-R1, the improvement reached as much as 2.5x. … Nvidia detailed the results in its official MLPerf announcement , describing Vera Rubin’s entry as a preview submission ahead of broader deployment.\n- e409 [fact; Trefis; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:d4f3bddf04a4d029a097541c 2026-09-27] NVIDIA began production shipments of its newest data center system, Vera Rubin, in August 2026, according to the article.\n    Quote: The company began production shipments of Vera Rubin, its newest data center system, in August 2026.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e208; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e410 [party_statement; NVIDIA management; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:d4f3bddf04a4d029a097541c 2026-09-27] NVIDIA management expects Vera Rubin to account for about 20% of data center revenue in fiscal Q3 2027.\n    Quote: Management expects Vera Rubin to bring in about 20% of data center revenue in fiscal Q3 2027.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e555, e764, e782, e813, e858; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e416 [party_statement; NVIDIA management; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:d4f3bddf04a4d029a097541c 2026-09-27] NVIDIA management expects sales to its largest cloud companies to speed up again in fiscal Q4 2027 as Vera Rubin supply grows.\n    Quote: Management expects sales to the largest cloud companies to speed up again in fiscal Q4 2027, as Vera Rubin supply grows.\n- e551 [fact; Trefis; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:4334522792e44c6fd9d18ccd 2026-09-28] NVIDIA management said on its fiscal Q2 2027 earnings call that inventory rose to $32 billion as the company prepared for the Vera Rubin launch.\n    Quote: On the fiscal Q2 2027 call, management said inventory rose to $32 billion as NVIDIA prepared for the Vera Rubin launch.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e588; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e555 [party_statement; Trefis; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:4334522792e44c6fd9d18ccd 2026-09-28] NVIDIA management expects the newly launched Vera Rubin platform to make up about 20% of data center revenue in fiscal Q3 2027, according to Trefis.\n    Quote: The newly launched Vera Rubin platform is one source of that revenue. Management expects it to make up about 20% of data center revenue in fiscal Q3 2027.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e410; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e633 [third_party_view; Adam Spatacco, fool.com; original source  ; from gnews:0e4e3f9b17e0cda6b72d47fc 2026-09-29] The article reports a forecast that Nvidia's next-generation Vera Rubin CPU architecture will reach $20 billion in sales next quarter.\n    Quote: Meanwhile, the next-generation Vera Rubin CPU architecture is forecast to reach $20 billion in sales next quarter.\n- e659 [fact; NVIDIA Corporation (NVIDIA Blog); original source CoreWeave ; from nvblog:coreweave-agentic-ai-vera-rubin 2026-09-30] CoreWeave announced at its CoreWeave Fully Connected event (held the week of the 30 September 2026 NVIDIA blog post) that NVIDIA Vera Rubin NVL72 systems with Spectrum-X 102.4T Ethernet networking are available on CoreWeave Cloud, making CoreWeave one of the first cloud providers to deliver the platform in customers' hands; per the announcement, Cognition, the applied AI lab behind the Devin AI software engineer, is the first customer running production workloads on Vera Rubin.\n    Quote: At CoreWeave Fully Connected, running this week in San Francisco, CoreWeave announced availability of NVIDIA Vera Rubin NVL72 systems with Spectrum-X 102.4T Ethernet networking. Cognition, the applied AI lab behind the Devin AI software engineer, is the first customer running production workloads on Vera Rubin. … CoreWeave announced availability of NVIDIA Vera Rubin NVL72 on CoreWeave Cloud, makin\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e751, e860, e894, e895; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e660 [fact; NVIDIA Corporation (NVIDIA Blog); original source CoreWeave ; from nvblog:coreweave-agentic-ai-vera-rubin 2026-09-30] CoreWeave received its first NVIDIA Vera Rubin NVL72 production racks earlier in September 2026, before the 30 September 2026 article.\n    Quote: Earlier this month, CoreWeave received its first Vera Rubin NVL72 production racks.\n- e720 [fact; CRN; original source TD Synnex (CEO Patrick Zammit), with customer identity reported by CRN ; from gnews:84569ea837e5d2d93ff16093 2026-09-30] TD Synnex CEO Patrick Zammit said on the distributor's third-quarter earnings call (the Thursday before the 30 September 2026 article) that TD Synnex and Houston-based systems integrator Mark III Systems recently signed an agreement to support an NVIDIA AI factory powered by Vera Rubin NVL72 systems, which he described as 'one of the largest' enterprise AI factory infrastructure deployments expected to be delivered through the channel, bundling design, integration, deployment, Day 2 co-admin operations, financing and supply chain. CRN reported, citing what it learned, that the customer is a US-based Fortune 100 company. No contract value, system count or delivery date was disclosed.\n    Quote: After TD Synnex revealed that Mark III Systems is set to deliver ‘one of the largest’ enterprise AI factory deployments in the channel, an executive at the systems integrator explained to CRN how its AI factory expertise and enterprise trends helped it seal the deal. … As hyperscalers and neoclouds begin to launch offerings based on Nvidia’s new flagship Vera Rubin rack-scale AI platform, one syst\n- e751 [fact; GuruFocus (gurufocus.com); original source CoreWeave ; from gnews:2b6641fb175eb6c57e2bdff2 2026-09-30] CoreWeave (CRWV) announced at its Fully Connected AI conference in San Francisco that it is bringing NVIDIA (NVDA) Vera CPUs into its cloud portfolio for AI-agent workloads and will offer NVIDIA Vera Rubin NVL72 systems for production use, with Cognition named as the first customer running workloads on the platform. CoreWeave said the Rubin systems are already operating on its cloud service, which it described as a move from infrastructure deployment toward commercial use, and that customers can access the hardware under its existing operating model for NVIDIA systems.\n    Quote: The company is bringing NVIDIA ( NVDA ) Vera CPUs to its cloud portfolio for workloads tied to AI agents. It will also offer NVIDIA Vera Rubin NVL72 systems for production use, with Cognition serving as the first customer running workloads on the platform. … CoreWeave said the Rubin systems are already operating on its cloud service, marking a move from infrastructure deployment toward commercial \n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e659; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e753 [third_party_view; NVIDIA Corporation (NVIDIA Blog); original source SemiAnalysis (AgentX data) ; from nvblog:productive-durable-fungible-ai-factories 2026-10-01] SemiAnalysis AgentX data cited in NVIDIA's blog states that NVIDIA Vera Rubin NVL72 systems deliver over 30x higher throughput per megawatt than NVIDIA GB300 NVL72 systems, and up to 45x lower cost per million tokens on the DeepSeek V4 Pro model.\n    Quote: SemiAnalysis AgentX data shows NVIDIA Vera Rubin NVL72 systems deliver over 30x higher throughput per megawatt than NVIDIA GB300 NVL72, and up to 45x lower cost per million tokens on the DeepSeek V4 Pro model.\n- e764 [party_statement; Zacks; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:95eab8d6d71dd0f94a8f763e 2026-10-01] NVIDIA said it started shipping its Vera Rubin platform in August 2026 and expects Vera Rubin to account for about 20% of Data Center revenues in fiscal Q3 2027.\n    Quote: NVIDIA started shipping Vera Rubin in August and expects the platform to account for about 20% of Data Center revenues in the third quarter.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e410; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e765 [party_statement; Zacks; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:95eab8d6d71dd0f94a8f763e 2026-10-01] NVIDIA estimates revenue opportunity per gigawatt of AI infrastructure at approximately $18 billion for Hopper, $25 billion for Blackwell and $40 billion for Vera Rubin.\n    Quote: The company estimates that revenue opportunity per gigawatt of AI infrastructure has increased from approximately $18 billion with Hopper to $25 billion with Blackwell and $40 billion with Vera Rubin.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e488; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e775 [party_statement; finance.yahoo.com article (contributor piece); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:890bfe7d206f0531a54beb16 2026-10-01] NVIDIA's Vera Rubin platform is now in full production, and the article states revenue per gigawatt of deployed capacity has risen from $18 billion for Hopper to $25 billion for Blackwell and $40 billion for Vera Rubin.\n    Quote: Revenue per gigawatt rose from $18 billion for Hopper to $25 billion for Blackwell and $40 billion for Vera Rubin, now in full production.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e488; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e782 [third_party_view; finance.yahoo.com article (contributor piece); original source  ; from gnews:890bfe7d206f0531a54beb16 2026-10-01] The article estimates Vera Rubin will make up about 20% of NVIDIA's data center revenue in Q3 FY2027, with NVIDIA's next earnings report due November 17.\n    Quote: Vera Rubin should make up about 20% of data center revenue in Q3, and the next earnings report lands November 17.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e410; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e811 [fact; fool.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:efb160acb7c94e14a52df9fa 2026-10-01] Nvidia launched its Vera CPU, a central processing unit positioned for agentic-AI workloads; the article reports the launch while noting that the agentic AI boom will likely raise CPU demand. The item gives no launch date, volume, customer or shipment timing.\n    Quote: There is a significant opportunity in some of the niches it targets. For instance, the agentic AI boom will likely lead to increased demand for CPUs (central processing units), and to that end, Nvidia launched its Vera CPU.\n- e813 [party_statement; Daniel Foelber, fool.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:e84d920e300dc3c29e501037 2026-10-01] Nvidia began shipping its Rubin platform in August and expects Rubin to account for 20% of data center revenue in the third quarter of fiscal 2027, which the company will report in November, according to the article.\n    Quote: Nvidia began shipping Rubin in August and already expects it to account for 20% of data center revenue in the third quarter of fiscal 2027, which it will report in November.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e410; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e814 [party_statement; Daniel Foelber, fool.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:e84d920e300dc3c29e501037 2026-10-01] Nvidia is guiding for 70% year-over-year revenue growth in fiscal 2028, which the article attributes to massive demand for its Rubin platform.\n    Quote: Demand for Rubin is so massive that Nvidia is guiding for 70% year-over-year revenue growth in fiscal 2028.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e104; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e832 [third_party_view; Keithen Drury, fool.com; original source Keithen Drury (fool.com) 2026-10-01; from gnews:6793603da478ba6858e0827d 2026-10-01] In his 1 October 2026 column, Keithen Drury (fool.com) wrote that Nvidia's new Rubin-generation chips are starting to become available, will provide more advanced capabilities than prior generations, and that Nvidia will charge a higher price for them, which he called a built-in revenue escalator 'launching shortly'. No shipment volumes, dates or prices are given, and the claim is the author's characterisation rather than a company announcement.\n    Quote: Even though competition is intensifying for which company has the best available option, Nvidia's new Rubin generation of chips will make an even greater impact than prior iterations. These computing units are starting to become available and will provide more advanced capabilities than prior generations. Nvidia will also charge a higher price tag for them, so it has a built-in revenue escalator t\n- e857 [fact; Yahoo Finance (finance.yahoo.com); original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:8867ec84bb48b27c3ae694f5 2026-10-02] NVIDIA management said on the fiscal Q2 2027 earnings call that the company began production shipments of its Vera Rubin AI data-center platform in August 2026.\n    Quote: NVIDIA began production shipments in August 2026, management said on the fiscal Q2 2027 call.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e208; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e858 [party_statement; Yahoo Finance (finance.yahoo.com); original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:8867ec84bb48b27c3ae694f5 2026-10-02] NVIDIA management forecast on the fiscal Q2 2027 call that Vera Rubin would make up about 20% of data center revenue in fiscal Q3 2027; the article notes this is a forecast, not a result.\n    Quote: Management expects Vera Rubin to make up about 20% of data center revenue in fiscal Q3 2027. That is a forecast, not a result.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e410; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e859 [fact; Yahoo Finance (finance.yahoo.com); original source Supermicro 2026-09-23; from gnews:8867ec84bb48b27c3ae694f5 2026-10-02] Supermicro said on 23 September 2026 that it is shipping racks built on NVIDIA's Vera Rubin platform.\n    Quote: Supermicro said on September 23, 2026 that it is shipping racks built on Vera Rubin.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e181; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e860 [fact; Yahoo Finance (finance.yahoo.com); original source CoreWeave 2026-09-30; from gnews:8867ec84bb48b27c3ae694f5 2026-10-02] CoreWeave, a cloud company, said on 30 September 2026 that one of its customers runs production workloads on NVIDIA's Vera Rubin system.\n    Quote: CoreWeave, a cloud company, said on September 30 that one of its customers runs production workloads on the system.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e659; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e864 [party_statement; Yahoo Finance (finance.yahoo.com); original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:8867ec84bb48b27c3ae694f5 2026-10-02] NVIDIA management